hadiths[681]{hadithnumber,text}:
"5248","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood among the people announcing that Allah had prescribed Hajj, whereupon a Bedouin inquired about its annual frequency. The Prophet ﷺ fell silent in anger for a long while, then demanded the questioner identify himself, and the Bedouin declared himself. > **""Indeed Allah has prescribed Hajj upon you. Who is this questioner? Woe to you, would it please you were I to say yes? By Allah, had I said yes it would have become obligatory, and had it become obligatory you would have abandoned it, and had you abandoned it you would have disbelieved. Verily those before you were destroyed by the imams of hardship. By Allah, had I permitted for you all that is upon the earth and forbidden you even as little as a camel's hoof, you would have fallen into it.""** > **""O you who believe, do not ask about things which, if made clear to you, would distress you.""**"
"5249","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that a man of Banu Sa'd ibn Bakr, among whom the Messenger ﷺ had been suckled, came forward addressing him as son of Abd al-Muttalib. The Prophet ﷺ answered his call, and the envoy stipulated that his questioning would be forceful and adjured the Prophet ﷺ not to resent it, to which the Prophet ﷺ agreed. He then questioned the creation of heavens, earth, Paradise and Hell, and adjured by their Creator whether the Prophet ﷺ had been sent with the testimony of tawhid and abandonment of al-Lat and al-Uzza, the five daily prayers, and Hajj to the House in Dhu al-Hijjah, receiving affirmation each time. He concluded he would neither add nor exceed those five, and after he turned away the Prophet ﷺ judged his end. > **""I have answered you. Yes. Allah. Yes. Yes. Yes. Yes. Yes. Indeed, if he does what he has said, he shall enter Paradise.""**"
"5250","Samurah رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanding steadfastness in worship and pilgrimage, with rectitude as its consequence. > **""Establish the prayer, give the zakah, perform Hajj and Umrah, and remain upright and you shall be dealt with uprightly.""**"
"5251","Ya'la ibn Umayyah رضي الله عنه reports that a man came in ihram for Umrah wearing sewn garments and saturated with khaluq perfume, asking direction concerning his Umrah. Thereupon Allah revealed the verse commanding completion of Hajj and Umrah, and the Prophet ﷺ summoned the questioner and instructed him in purification and analogy with Hajj. > **""And complete the Hajj and Umrah for Allah. Who is the questioner about Umrah? Cast off your garments, bathe, cleanse yourself as thoroughly as you can, and whatever you would do in your Hajj, do it in your Umrah.""**"
"5252","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports from the Prophet ﷺ a concise ruling on the status of the two pilgrimages. > **""Hajj is jihad, and Umrah is voluntary.""**"
"5253","Ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه states that four establishments were commanded: prayer, zakah, Hajj and Umrah toward the House, with Hajj being the Greater Hajj and Umrah the Lesser Hajj."
"5254","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ruling that Hajj performed in childhood, Bedouin life, or slavery does not suffice for the obligation once the impediment is removed. > **""Whichever boy performs Hajj then reaches puberty, upon him is another Hajj; whichever Bedouin performs Hajj then migrates, upon him is another Hajj; and whichever slave performs Hajj then is freed, upon him is another Hajj.""**"
"5255","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ urging enjoyment of the House, informing that it has twice been demolished and shall be raised away upon the third. > **""Take enjoyment from this House, for it has been demolished twice, and it shall be raised away at the third.""**"
"5256","Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir reports meeting Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما mounted on a defective she-camel worth less than ten dirhams. When asked whether he would perform Hajj upon it, Ibn Umar affirmed, citing the Prophet's ﷺ injunction and declaring he possessed no other mount and would not abandon Hajj. > **""Do not abandon Hajj even upon a defective she-camel worth ten dirhams.""**"
"5257","Al-Husayn ibn Ali رضي الله عنهما reports that a man confessed his cowardice and weakness to the Prophet ﷺ, who directed him to a struggle devoid of arms. > **""Come to a jihad in which there is no warfare: the Hajj.""**"
"5258","Uthman ibn Sulayman reports from his grandmother that a man desiring jihad in Allah's path was offered guidance to a non-combatant jihad, which upon acceptance was identified as pilgrimage to the House. > **""Shall I not direct you to a jihad in which there is no warfare? The Hajj to the House.""**"
"5259","Abu Sa'id al-Khudri رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conveying Allah's عزوجل declaration concerning the servant granted bodily health and ample provision who yet fails to visit Him within four years. > **""Verily Allah says: A servant whose body I have kept sound and whose provision I have expanded, if he does not come to Me within every four years, is indeed deprived.""**"
"5260","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ identifying the struggle of those excused from battle. > **""The jihad of the elderly, the weak, and the woman is Hajj and Umrah.""**"
"5261","Abu Juhayfah رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ warning that neglect of a Muslim brother's need, withholding spending in Allah's path, and abandoning Hajj for a worldly motive rebound in divine displeasure and missed opportunity before the worldly need is even fulfilled. > **""There is no servant nor maidservant who refuses to walk in fulfilling the need of his Muslim brother except that he walks therefrom in the wrath of Allah Almighty, nor refuses to spend an expenditure in the path of Allah except that he spends manifold multiples thereof in Allah's wrath, nor abandons Hajj for a worldly purpose except that he sees those with shaven heads before he fulfills that need.""**"
"5262","Amr ibn Abasah رضي الله عنه reports a man's successive questioning about Islam, best Islam, faith, best faith, migration, best migration, jihad, and best jihad. The Prophet ﷺ defined submission of the heart with safety of Muslims from tongue and hand, then faith in Allah, His angels, His Books, His Messengers and resurrection after death, then abandonment of evil, then fighting disbelievers upon encounter, with the most virtuous being he whose horse is hamstrung and whose blood is shed. He then named two deeds surpassing all except their like. > **""Submit your heart, and let the Muslims be safe from your tongue and your hand. Faith. That you believe in Allah, His angels, His Books, His Messengers, and resurrection after death. Migration. That you abandon evil. Jihad. That you fight the disbelievers when you meet them. He whose horse is hamstrung and whose blood is shed. Then two deeds are the best of deeds except for one who does their like: an accepted Hajj or an Umrah.""**"
"5263","Ma'iz رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ was asked which deed is most virtuous, and answered with belief in Allah alone, then jihad, then a righteous Hajj distinguished above all deeds as the distance between sunrise and sunset. > **""Faith in Allah alone, then jihad, then a righteous Hajj surpassing all other deeds as between the rising of the sun and its setting.""**"
"5264","Al-Shifa رضي الله عنها reports hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ answer a man's inquiry about the most virtuous deed with three acts. > **""Faith in Allah, jihad in His path, and an accepted Hajj.""**"
"5265","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ confining the recompense of the accepted Hajj to Paradise. > **""The accepted Hajj has no reward except Paradise.""**"
"5266","Jabir ibn Abdillah رضي الله عنهما reports the Prophet ﷺ declaring the reward of accepted Hajj, and when asked its righteousness explained it as charity of food and purity of speech. > **""The accepted Hajj has no reward except Paradise. Its righteousness is feeding food and kind speech.""**"
"5267","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ confining the recompense of the accepted Hajj to Paradise. > **""The accepted Hajj has no reward except Paradise.""**"
"5268","Buraydah رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ likening expenditure in Hajj to expenditure in Allah's path in manifold reward. > **""Expenditure in Hajj is like expenditure in the path of Allah, seven hundredfold.""**"
"5269","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ describing Hajj in Allah's path and the multiplication of its coin. > **""Hajj in the path of Allah: the dirham spent therein is seven hundred.""**"
"5270","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ describing the Kaaba with tongue and lips complaining to Allah of few visitors, and Allah's promise to create humble prostrating people yearning for it as the dove yearns for its eggs. > **""Verily the Kaaba has a tongue and two lips, and it complained to Allah saying: My Lord, my frequenters have lessened and my visitors have lessened. So Allah Almighty revealed: I shall create humble prostrating mortals who yearn for you as the dove yearns for its eggs.""**"
"5271","Abu Dharr رضي الله عنه reports the Prophet ﷺ narrating that Dawud the Prophet عليه السلام asked his Lord what is due to His servants when they visit Him in His House, and Allah affirmed the right of the visitor upon the visited with well-being in this world and forgiveness at meeting. > **""My God, what is due to Your servants upon You when they visit You in Your House? Verily every visitor has a right upon the visited. O Dawud, they have upon Me that I shall grant them well-being in this world and forgive them when I meet them.""**"
"5272","Jabir ibn Abdillah رضي الله عنهما conveys in marfu' form that a pilgrim never suffers impoverishment, explaining impoverishment as destitution when questioned. > **""No pilgrim ever suffers impoverishment.""**"
"5273","Aishah رضي الله عنها reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promising one who sets out for Hajj or Umrah and dies en route exemption from presentation and reckoning with entry into Paradise. She further reports his declaration of divine boasting over those performing circumambulation. > **""Whoever sets out upon this path for Hajj or Umrah then dies therein shall neither be presented nor reckoned, and it shall be said to him: Enter Paradise. Verily Allah boasts over those performing tawaf.""**"
"5274","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promising continuing reward until the Day of Resurrection for whoever dies after setting out as pilgrim, performer of Umrah, or warrior in Allah's path. > **""Whoever sets out as a pilgrim then dies, Allah writes for him the reward of the pilgrim until the Day of Resurrection. Whoever sets out as a performer of Umrah then dies, Allah writes for him the reward of the performer of Umrah until the Day of Resurrection. And whoever sets out as a warrior in the path of Allah then dies, Allah writes for him the reward of the warrior until the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"5275","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports the Prophet ﷺ describing this House as a support among Islam's supports, with whoever performs its Hajj or Umrah under Allah's guarantee of Paradise if he dies or return with reward and spoils if restored to his family. > **""Verily this House is a pillar among the pillars of Islam, so whoever performs Hajj to the House or Umrah is guaranteed by Allah: if he dies He admits him to Paradise, and if He returns him to his family He returns him with reward and spoils.""**"
"5276","Sahl ibn Sa'd رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promising that no Muslim setting out as a warrior in Allah's path or as a pilgrim raising his voice in ihram and talbiyah but the sun sets with his sins and he emerges from them. > **""No Muslim sets out in the path of Allah as a warrior, or as a pilgrim raising his voice in ihram or talbiyah, except that the sun sets with his sins and he emerges from them.""**"
"5277","Abdullah ibn Jarad رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanding Hajj for its purification of sins as water washes filth. > **""Perform Hajj, for Hajj washes sins as water washes dirt.""**"
"5278","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما addressed his sons urging departure from Makkah on foot for Hajj and return on foot, citing the Prophet's ﷺ distinction between rider and walker and explaining the good deeds of the Sanctuary as one deed worth a hundred thousand. > **""O my sons, go forth from Makkah as pilgrims on foot until you return to Makkah on foot, for I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: The mounted pilgrim has for every step his mount takes seventy good deeds, and the walking pilgrim has for every step he takes seven hundred good deeds among the good deeds of the Sanctuary. And the good deed of the Sanctuary is a good deed worth a hundred thousand good deeds.""**"
"5279","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that groups from Muzaynah, Hudhayl and Juhaynah came complaining that they had set out for Makkah on foot while others rode, and the Prophet ﷺ distinguished their rewards. > **""For the walker is the reward of seventy Hajjs, and for the rider is the reward of thirty Hajjs.""**"
"5280","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ contrasting one who intends the House from unlawful earning in disobedience with one who departs with lawful wealth. Each places his foot in the stirrup, his mount rises, and he proclaims talbiyah, whereupon a caller from heaven answers with rejection or acceptance, addressing his earning, mount, clothing and provision, and bidding him return burdened or rewarded with tidings of sorrow or joy. > **""Whoever intends this House from unlawful earning, proceeding in other than Allah's obedience, then raises his voice in ihram, places his foot in the stirrup, his mount rises with him and he says: Labbayk Allahumma Labbayk, a caller from heaven calls: No labbayk and no sa'dayk, your earning is unlawful, your provision unlawful, your mount unlawful, so return burdened without reward and receive tidings of what displeases you. And when a man goes forth as a pilgrim with lawful wealth, places his foot in the stirrup, his mount rises with him and he says: Labbayk Allahumma Labbayk, a caller from heaven calls: Labbayk and sa'dayk, I have answered you; your mount is lawful, your garments lawful, your provision lawful, so return rewarded without burden and receive tidings of what delights you.""**"
"5281","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ enjoining travel for soundness and security. > **""Travel, you shall be healthy and safe.""**"
"5282","Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id al-Maqburi reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ describing travel as a portion of torment preoccupying a man from fasting, prayer and worship, and urging hastened return to family once its purpose is fulfilled. > **""Travel is a portion of torment, for a man is preoccupied therein from his fasting, his prayer and his worship, so when one of you fulfills his purpose from his travel, let him hasten return to his family.""**"
"5283","Aishah رضي الله عنها and Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه report the Messenger of Allah ﷺ describing travel as a portion of torment depriving one of sleep, food, drink and pleasure, and urging hastened return to family once need is fulfilled. > **""Travel is a portion of torment, preventing one of you from his sleep, his food, his drink and his pleasure, so when one of you finishes his need, let him hasten to his family.""**"
"5284","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ instructing one intending travel to greet his brothers, whose supplications add good to his own. > **""When one of you intends travel, let him bid farewell to his brothers, for they add to his supplication with their supplications goodness.""**"
"5285","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that a boy intending Hajj came to the Prophet ﷺ, who walked with him, raised his head toward him and entrusted him to piety, goodness and sufficiency from anxiety. Upon return the boy greeted the Prophet ﷺ, who again raised his head toward him and invoked acceptance, expiation and replacement of expenditure. > **""O boy, may Allah provide you with piety, direct you toward good, and suffice you against anxiety. O boy, may Allah accept your Hajj, expiate your sin, and replace your expenditure.""**"
"5286","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that Umar رضي الله عنه sought the Prophet's ﷺ permission for Umrah, received it, and was asked to share in righteous supplication without forgetfulness. > **""O my brother, include us in your righteous supplication and do not forget us.""**"
"5287","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ extending divine forgiveness to the pilgrim and to those for whom he seeks forgiveness. > **""Allah forgives the pilgrim and him for whom the pilgrim seeks forgiveness.""**"
"5288","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ describing pilgrims and performers of Umrah as Allah's delegation, answered when called and granted when they ask. > **""The pilgrims and performers of Umrah are the delegation of Allah: He called them and they answered Him, and they ask Him and He grants them.""**"
"5289","Abu Musa رضي الله عنه conveys in marfu' form that the pilgrim intercedes for four hundred households of his house and emerges from sins as the day his mother bore him. > **""The pilgrim intercedes for four hundred households — or he said: of his household — and emerges from his sins as the day his mother bore him.""**"
"5290","Buraydah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would set out on Thursday when intending travel."
"5291","Ka'b ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would only set out on a journey or dispatch an expedition on Thursday."
"5292","Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ preferred to travel on Thursday."
"5293","Abu Ra'itah ibn Karamah al-Madhhiji رضي الله عنه reports being with the Prophet ﷺ when he addressed traveling folk, forbidding weak stray mounts, returning beggars if seeking profit and safety, and company of sorcerers, soothsayers, astrologers and poets among men and women for believers in Allah and the Last Day. He warned that Allah's intended torment descends to the lowest heaven, and prohibited disobedience to Allah in the evening. > **""Let no weak beast among these cattle accompany you — meaning strays — and let none of you accompany a stray, nor turn away a beggar if you desire profit and safety. And let there not accompany you, if you believe in Allah and the Last Day, any sorcerer or sorceress, nor soothsayer or soothsayeress, nor astrologer or astrologeress, nor poet or poetess. Verily every torment by which Allah intends to punish any of His servants is but sent down to the lowest heaven, so I forbid you from disobedience to Allah in the evening.""**"
"5294","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports the Prophet ﷺ sitting after Maghrib with his Companions when a caravan passed with its driver reciting and its guide chanting. The Prophet ﷺ hastened bare-shouldered to reach them despite offers of sufficiency, declaring his duty to convey their revelation. He questioned their destination and hour, learning they sought Yemen, then warned of Allah's mighty authority in heaven directed toward earth's folk, forbidding travel at that hour except for unavoidable relief. He instructed the driver to measured Arab rajaz, the rider to recitation, and all to night journeying for Allah folds the earth for the traveler as scrolls are folded and dawn praises night travel. He forbade company of poet, soothsayer or stray, and returning beggars for profit, safety and good companionship, expressing wonder at sleep while all eyes sleep when Allah forbids travel at that hour. > **""Leave me to convey to them what has been revealed to me concerning them. Where do you intend at this hour? Why do you journey at this hour? Verily Allah has in heaven a mighty authority which He directs toward the people of the earth, so do not travel, not a step except what a man finds in his belly and bladder of urine for which there is no escape. As for you, O driver of the folk, upon you is some speech of the Arabs from its rajaz, and when you are mounted then recite, and upon you is night journeying, for Allah Almighty has angels entrusted who fold the earth for the traveler as you fold scrolls, and after dawn the folk praise the night journey. Let no poet nor soothsayer accompany you, let no stray accompany you, and do not turn away a beggar if you desire profit, safety and good companionship. Wondrous to me how I sleep when all eyes sleep? Verily Allah Almighty forbids you from traveling at this hour.""**"
"5295","Anas رضي الله عنه reports the Prophet ﷺ commanding dismounting to grant mounts their share of herbage in fertility, swift passage through drought, and night journeying for the earth is folded at night. > **""When the land is fertile, dismount from your backs and give them their due of herbage, and when the land is barren, hasten through upon their backs, and upon you is night journeying, for the earth is folded at night.""**"
"5296","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanding pasturing and halting at stations in fertility, hastening and night journeying in drought, hastening to adhan when ghouls appear, and forbidding prayer, halting, or relieving oneself upon highways as abodes of serpents, beasts and curses. > **""When you are in fertility, enable the mounts their muzzles and do not pass beyond stations, and when you are in drought then hasten through, and upon you is night journeying for the earth is folded at night. And when the ghouls confront you, hasten to the adhan. Do not pray upon the main roads nor halt upon them, for they are the abode of serpents and beasts, nor relieve yourselves upon them, for they are places of curse.""**"
"5297","Abdur Rahman ibn A'idh reports the Prophet ﷺ naming three disliked halts and supplications: the ruined dwelling, the thoroughfare, and one who releases his beast then prays Allah to withhold it. > **""Three whom Allah does not love: a man who halts in a ruined house, a man who halts upon the traveled road, and a man who releases his beast then beseeches Allah to withhold it.""**"
"5298","Abdullah ibn Mughaffal رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanding swiftness in drought and night journeying when mounted upon dumb beasts, for Allah folds the distance. > **""When you mount these dumb beasts, if it be a year of drought then hasten through, and upon you is night journeying, for Allah folds it.""**"
"5299","Khalid ibn Ma'dan reports from his father from the Prophet ﷺ describing Allah as Gentle, loving and pleased with gentleness and aiding thereby beyond harshness. He commanded lodging dumb mounts at their stations, hastening through drought for the earth is folded by night beyond the day, and avoiding night halts upon roads as paths of beasts and abodes of serpents. > **""Verily Allah is Gentle, loves gentleness, is pleased with it, and aids thereby what He does not aid with harshness. So when you mount these dumb beasts, lodge them at their stations. If the land is barren, hasten through upon it, for the earth is folded by night what is not folded by day. And beware of halting at night upon the road, for it is the path of beasts and the abode of serpents.""**"
"5300","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ leaning against the House, exhorting the people with prohibition of prayer after Asr until night and after Fajr until sunrise, restriction of a woman's three-day journey to company of a mahram, and prohibition of marrying over paternal or maternal aunts. > **""Let none pray after Asr until night, nor after Fajr until the sun rises, let no woman travel a distance of three days except with a mahram, and let no woman be married over her paternal aunt nor over her maternal aunt.""**"
"5301","Adi ibn Hatim رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ limiting a woman's travel beyond three days to accompaniment of a mahram. > **""Let no woman travel beyond three days except with a mahram.""**"
"5302","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ judging a woman's journey with her own slave as wasteful ruin. > **""A woman's travel with her slave is ruin.""**"
"5303","Umm Sulaym رضي الله عنها reports being with the Prophet's ﷺ wives while a driver drove them, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ urged restraint likening women to glass vessels. > **""O Anjashah, gently, your driving with the glass vessels.""**"
"5304","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports the Prophet ﷺ addressing his wives in the year of the Farewell Hajj that this pilgrimage sufficed before remaining upon mats at home. All continued to perform Hajj except Zaynab bint Jahsh رضي الله عنها and Sawdah bint Zam'ah رضي الله عنها, who swore no beast would move them after hearing the Prophet ﷺ. > **""This, then the backs of mats. It is but this Hajj, then the backs of mats.""**"
"5305","Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressing them during the Farewell Hajj that only this Hajj remained before sitting upon mats within houses. > **""It is but this Hajj, then sitting upon the backs of mats within houses.""**"
"5306","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that when the Prophet ﷺ performed Hajj with his wives he declared this sufficed before remaining upon mats. > **""It is but this, then upon you is remaining upon the backs of mats.""**"
"5307","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ruling concerning a propertied married woman whose husband refuses permission that she may not depart without his leave. > **""It is not for her to set out except with her husband's permission.""**"
"5308","Aslam reports returning from travel and being asked by Umar رضي الله عنه whom he accompanied. When he named a man of Bakr ibn Wa'il, Umar reminded him of the Prophet's ﷺ warning against trusting even a Bakri brother. > **""Your Bakri brother, yet do not trust him.""**"
"5309","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ describing Satan's designs upon one and two travelers but not three. > **""Satan intends the one and the two, but when they are three he does not intend them.""**"
"5310","Husayl ibn Kharijah al-Ashja'i رضي الله عنه reports arriving at Madinah with trade goods, being brought to the Prophet ﷺ, and being offered twenty sa' of dates to guide his Companions to Khaybar. He complied, received his wage after its conquest, then embraced Islam. > **""I shall grant you twenty sa' of dates if you guide my Companions to the road of Khaybar.""**"
"5311","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports the Prophet ﷺ describing Iblis dispatching rebellious devils with orders to devote themselves to pilgrims and warriors to mislead them from the path. > **""Verily Iblis has rebels among the devils to whom he says: Upon you is the pilgrim and the warrior, so lead them astray from the path.""**"
"5312","Abu Imran reports asking Jundub ibn Abdillah رضي الله عنه whether they would press non-Arabs into service, and he affirmed they would employ them from village to village for road guidance then release them."
"5313","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ would walk on foot after performing Fajr prayer while traveling."
"5314","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports the Prophet ﷺ commanding loads be placed behind, for the leg is bound and the hand suspended. > **""When you load, delay the load, for the leg is bound and the hand is suspended.""**"
"5315","Jabir رضي الله عنه and Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما report the Messenger of Allah ﷺ fixing Dhu al-Hulayfah for Madinah, al-Juhfah for Sham, Yalamlam for Yemen and Tihamah, Qarn for Ta'if which is Najd, and Dhat Irq for Iraq."
"5316","Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما reports the Prophet ﷺ fixing Qarn as miqat for Najd."
"5317","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ fixing al-Aqiq for Mada'in, Dhat Irq for Basrah, Dhu al-Hulayfah for Madinah, and al-Juhfah for Sham."
"5318","Al-Harith ibn Amr رضي الله عنه reports coming to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at Mina or Arafat, where he fixed Yalamlam for Yemen as the place from which they raise ihram."
"5319","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports the Prophet ﷺ prohibiting crossing the miqat without ihram. > **""Do not pass beyond the miqat except in ihram.""**"
"5320","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promising one who assumes ihram from Bayt al-Maqdis entry in a forgiven state. > **""Whoever assumes ihram from Bayt al-Maqdis enters forgiven.""**"
"5321","Al-Hasan reports that Imran ibn Husayn رضي الله عنه assumed ihram from Basrah, and when he came to Umar رضي الله عنه the news having reached him, Umar spoke harshly warning that people would say a Companion of the Prophet ﷺ assumed ihram from an outlying garrison town."
"5322","Al-Hasan ibn al-Hadiyah reports meeting Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما, who asked his origin. Upon learning he was of Oman, Ibn Umar offered a Prophetic narration he had heard describing Oman as a land flanked by the sea, whose single Hajj surpasses two Hajjs from elsewhere. > **""Verily I know a land called Oman, flanked on its sides by the sea; a Hajj therefrom is superior to two Hajjs from other than it.""**"
"5323","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما states that it is from the Sunnah for a man to perform ghusl when he intends to enter ihram."
"5324","Aishah رضي الله عنها reports that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ intended to enter ihram he would wash his head with khitmi and ushnan and oil it with a small amount of oil, not much."
"5325","Abu Barzah رضي الله عنه reports that people asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about an uncircumcised man performing Hajj to the House of Allah. > **""No; Allah has forbidden me from that until he is circumcised.""**"
"5326","Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها reports that the Prophet ﷺ came to Dubaah bint al-Zubayr ibn Abd al-Muttalib رضي الله عنها while she was ill, intending the Farewell Hajj, and invited her to join the journey. She expressed fear her illness would detain her, so he instructed her to enter ihram with a condition. > **""Will you not go out with us in this journey of ours?""** > **""Enter ihram for Hajj and say: O Allah, my release is where You detain me.""**"
"5327","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ said to Dubaah رضي الله عنها. > **""Perform Hajj and stipulate that my release is where You detain me.""**"
"5328","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that Dubaah bint al-Zubayr رضي الله عنها intended Hajj, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to her. > **""Perform Hajj and say: My release is where You detain me.""**"
"5329","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ explained His saying {Al-Hajju ashhurun malumat}. > **""Shawwal, Dhul-Qadah and Dhul-Hijjah.""**"
"5330","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما explains the saying of Allah Most High {Al-Hajju ashhurun malumat} as Shawwal, Dhul-Qadah and Dhul-Hijjah, and that Hajj is not obligated except in them."
"5331","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما states that it is from the Sunnah not to raise the voice in ihram for Hajj except in the months of Hajj."
"5332","Umar ibn al-Khattab رضي الله عنه smelled perfume at Dhul-Hulayfah and traced it to Muawiyah رضي الله عنه, who said Umm Habibah رضي الله عنها had perfumed him claiming she had perfumed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at his ihram. Umar sent him to adjure her to wash it off, and she did so. In addition the Prophet ﷺ described the pilgrim as disheveled and dusty without perfume. > **""Verily the pilgrim is disheveled and dusty without perfume.""**"
"5333","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما instructs that one should apply perfume before entering ihram."
"5334","Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said. > **""Do not apply perfume while you are muhrimah, and do not touch henna, for it is perfume.""**"
"5335","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports from the Prophet ﷺ. > **""There is no harm for a man to enter ihram in a garment dyed with saffron that has been washed, having no shedding of color nor trace of scent.""**"
"5336","Jabir ibn Abdillah رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said. > **""Whoever does not find an izar while muhrim but finds trousers, let him wear them, and whoever does not find sandals, let him wear khuffs and cut them below the ankles.""**"
"5337","Abdullah ibn Amir ibn Rabiah reports that Umar ibn al-Khattab رضي الله عنه heard the camel-driver chanting at night while heading to Makkah, joined the people with Abdur Rahman ibn Awf رضي الله عنه, and at dawn ordered silence for remembrance of Allah. He then saw khuffs on Abdur Rahman, who said he had worn them with one better than Umar, meaning with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, so Umar adjured him to remove them fearing people would imitate him."
"5338","In another narration Abdur Rahman ibn Awf رضي الله عنه affirmed without doubt that he had worn the two khuffs with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."
"5339","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said. > **""There is no ihram restriction upon the woman except in her face.""**"
"5340","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that a muhrimah woman shall not wear niqab nor gloves nor burqa, and if she wishes to enter ihram while menstruating she may do so and stand at all the stations except tawaf of the House and between al-Safa and al-Marwah. > **""The muhrimah woman shall not wear niqab nor wear gloves nor burqa, and if she wishes to enter ihram while menstruating let her enter ihram and stand at the stations except tawaf of the House and between al-Safa and al-Marwah.""**"
"5341","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the wives of the Prophet ﷺ would dye with henna while muhrimat and wear muasfar garments while muhrimat."
"5342","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the wives of the Prophet ﷺ would circumambulate the House wearing red cloaks that were not let down trailing."
"5343","Asma bint Abi Bakr رضي الله عنهما reports that the women of the Prophet ﷺ would wear muasfar chemises while muhrimat."
"5344","Umaymah bint Ruqayqah رضي الله عنها reports that the wives of the Prophet ﷺ would make headbands containing wars and saffron, bind therewith the lower part of their hair away from their foreheads before entering ihram, then enter ihram in that state."
"5345","Habbah bint Amr رضي الله عنها, who had prayed toward both qiblas with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, would when intending ihram lower her gaze into her lap and wear whatever clothes she wished including muasfar, then raise the talbiyah."
"5346","Umm Salamah, wife of the Prophet ﷺ, رضي الله عنها reports that they would be with the Prophet ﷺ while muhrimat, and when a rider passed them one of them would let down her garment over her face from above her head, sometimes described as from above the khimar."
"5347","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed Wadi Usfan during Hajj he asked Abu Bakr رضي الله عنه which valley it was, and upon being told it was Usfan he described Hud and Salih عليهما السلام passing through it on red young she-camels with palm-fibre halters, wearing aba cloaks as izars and striped woolen shawls as ridas, reciting talbiyah while performing Hajj to the Ancient House. > **""O Abu Bakr, which valley is this? Hud and Salih passed through it on red young she-camels whose halters were of fibre, their izars were aba cloaks and their ridas were striped wool, reciting talbiyah performing Hajj to the Ancient House.""**"
"5348","Abu Musa رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that seventy prophets passed by the rock of Rawha, among them Musa the Prophet of Allah عليه السلام, barefoot wearing aba cloaks heading for the Ancient House of Allah. > **""There passed by the rock of Rawha seventy prophets, among them Musa the Prophet of Allah, barefoot wearing aba cloaks heading for the Ancient House of Allah.""**"
"5349","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that seventy prophets passed by the rock of Rawha barefoot wearing aba cloaks heading for the Ancient House, among them Musa the Prophet of Allah عليه السلام. > **""There passed by the rock of Rawha seventy prophets barefoot wearing aba cloaks heading for the Ancient House of Allah, among them Musa the Prophet of Allah.""**"
"5350","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports from the Prophet ﷺ that Musa عليه السلام performed Hajj upon a red ox wearing a Qatawani aba. > **""Musa performed Hajj upon a red ox wearing a Qatawani aba.""**"
"5351","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said. > **""As if I look at Musa ibn Imran in this valley muhrim between two Qatawani garments.""**"
"5352","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that in Masjid al-Khayf were seventy prophets including Musa عليه السلام, as if he looks at him wearing two Qatawani abas, muhrim upon a Shanui camel with a fibre halter and having two braids. > **""In Masjid al-Khayf were seventy prophets including Musa, as if I look at him wearing two Qatawani abas, muhrim upon a camel of Shanuah bridled with a halter of fibre having two braids.""**"
"5353","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ set out from Mina on the Day of Arafah, and when his mount rose with a blanket bought for four dirhams he supplicated. > **""O Allah, make it a Hajj in which there is no riya nor fame.""**"
"5354","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ entered ihram after prayer."
"5355","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ raised the talbiyah when his mount rose with him."
"5356","Al-Hasan ibn Ali رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ did each practice, raising the talbiyah when his mount steadied with him and raising it while at al-Bayda on the ground before his mount steadied."
"5357","Abu Dawud al-Mazini رضي الله عنه, a veteran of Badr, reports that they went out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ who entered the masjid of Dhul-Hulayfah, prayed four rakahs therein, then raised ihram in the masjid heard by those inside, then raised it when he mounted heard by those near the masjid as upon his mount steadying, then raised it when he stood upon al-Bayda heard by those there, and all reporters spoke truthfully."
"5358","Abdullah ibn Urwah reports hearing Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما while going for Umrah; when descending from the hill into the valley Ibn al-Zubayr performed ghusl, prayed two rakahs, and they did likewise, then raised the talbiyah Labbayk Allahumma Labbayk Labbayk la sharika laka Labbayk inna al-hamd wal-nimata laka wal-mulk la sharika lak. Ibn al-Zubayr swore this was the talbiyah of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ who did thus and entered ihram after prayer."
"5359","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the talbiyah of Musa عليه السلام was Labbayk Your servant and son of Your two servants, the talbiyah of Isa عليه السلام was Labbayk Your servant and son of Your bondwoman, and the talbiyah of the Prophet ﷺ was Labbayk there is no partner for You."
"5360","Al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim reports that Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما when reciting talbiyah would say Labbayk Labbayk Allahumma Labbayk la sharika laka Labbayk inna al-hamd wal-nimata laka wal-mulk la sharika lak, and would instruct to stop at these words for they are the talbiyah of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."
"5361","Amr ibn Madi رضي الله عنه recalls that in Jahiliyyah when performing Hajj of the House they would chant verses about Zubayd coming under compulsion across deserts and mountains having left idols desolate, but today they say as taught by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ Labbayk Allahumma Labbayk Labbayk la sharika laka Labbayk inna al-hamd wal-nimata laka wal-mulk la sharika lak. In another wording from Qarn they would say Labbayk in glorification with excuse, and recalls halting at Batn Muhassir fearing seizure by jinn, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ taught the talbiyah and ordered rising above Batn Uranah for the jinn become brothers when Muslim, while in Jahiliyyah they barred people from standing except at Batn Muhassir on the eve of Arafah for fear of jinn. > **""Rise above Batn Uranah, for they are your brothers when they embrace Islam.""** > **""Labbayk Allahumma Labbayk, Labbayk la sharika laka Labbayk, inna al-hamd wal-nimata laka wal-mulk, la sharika lak.""**"
"5362","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that after Ismail عليه السلام people remained upon Islam, but Satan kept suggesting things to turn them from Islam until he introduced into their talbiyah Labbayk Allahumma Labbayk Labbayk la sharika lak illa sharikun huwa lak tamlikuhu wa ma malak, and persisted until he expelled them from Islam into shirk."
"5363","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the people of shirk would recite Labbayk Allahumma Labbayk Labbayk la sharika lak illa sharikan huwa lak tamlikuhu wa ma malak, so Allah Most High revealed {Hal lakum mimma malakat aymanukum min shuraka fima razaqnakum fa antum fihi sawa takhafunahum kakhiphatikum anfusakum} [al-Rum: 28]."
"5364","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ would recite talbiyah. > **""Labbayk Allahumma Labbayk Labbayk la sharika lak Labbayk inna al-hamd wal-nimata laka wal-mulk la sharika lak.""**"
"5365","Abdullah ibn Abi Salamah reports that Sad رضي الله عنه heard a man say Labbayk Dha al-Maarij and replied that He is indeed Dhu al-Maarij but when they were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ they would not say that."
"5366","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that the talbiyah of the Prophet ﷺ was. > **""Labbayk, a Hajj in truth, in worship and servitude.""**"
"5367","Abu al-Tufayl رضي الله عنه reports seeing the Prophet ﷺ upon his she-camel al-Qaswa raising the ihram while people pushed one another seeking to look at him."
"5368","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood at Arafat, and when he said Labbayk Allahumma Labbayk he added that true good is the good of the Hereafter. > **""Labbayk Allahumma Labbayk. Verily true good is the good of the Hereafter.""**"
"5369","Amir ibn Rabiah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that no believing reciter of talbiyah spends the forenoon till sunset reciting it but the sun sets with his sins so he returns as his mother bore him. > **""No believing reciter of talbiyah spends the forenoon till the sun sets but it sets with his sins and he returns as his mother bore him.""**"
"5370","Khuzaymah ibn Thabit رضي الله عنه reports that when the Prophet ﷺ finished his talbiyah he would ask Allah عزوجل for His forgiveness and pleasure and manumission from the Fire."
"5371","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that no reciter of tahlil ever recites nor reciter of takbir ever recites but he is given glad tidings, and when asked if of Paradise he affirmed. > **""No reciter of tahlil ever recites nor reciter of takbir ever recites but he is given glad tidings. Yes.""**"
"5372","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that Jibril عليه السلام came to him commanding him to proclaim the talbiyah. > **""Verily Jibril came to me and commanded me to proclaim the talbiyah.""**"
"5373","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that Jibril عليه السلام commanded him to raise the voice in ihlal for it is among the emblems of Hajj. > **""Jibril commanded me to raise the voice in ihlal, for it is among the emblems of Hajj.""**"
"5374","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that they would go out as pilgrims with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and before reaching al-Rawha the next day their throats became hoarse from raising the voice in talbiyah."
"5375","Ibrahim ibn Khallad ibn Suwayd al-Khazraji, of Banu al-Harith ibn al-Khazraj, reports that Jibril عليه السلام came to the Prophet ﷺ and said. > **""O Muhammad, be loud in talbiyah and abundant in sacrifice.""**"
"5376","Khallad ibn Suwayd رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that Jibril عليه السلام came to the Prophet ﷺ saying O Muhammad be ajjaj and thajjaj, meaning by ajj the talbiyah and by thajj the shedding of blood. > **""O Muhammad, be ajjaj thajjaj.""**"
"5377","Al-Saib ibn Khallad رضي الله عنه reports that Jibril عليه السلام came to the Prophet ﷺ saying be ajjaj thajjaj, where ajj is talbiyah and thajj is slaughter of camels. > **""Be ajjaj thajjaj.""**"
"5378","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that the best Hajj is al-ajj and al-thajj, ajj being talbiyah and thajj being slaughter of camels. > **""The best Hajj is al-ajj and al-thajj. As for al-ajj it is talbiyah, and as for al-thajj it is slaughter of camels.""**"
"5379","Ikrimah reports accompanying al-Husayn ibn Ali رضي الله عنهما from Muzdalifah hearing him recite talbiyah continuously until he stoned Jamrat al-Aqabah. When asked he said he had accompanied his father عليه السلام likewise till stoning, and his father had accompanied the Messenger of Allah ﷺ hearing him recite talbiyah continuously until he stoned Jamrat al-Aqabah. Upon informing Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما he confirmed Husayn spoke truthfully."
"5380","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ recited talbiyah in Umrah until touching the Stone and in Hajj until stoning the Jamrah."
"5381","Abu Wail Shaqiq ibn Salamah reports that Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه recited talbiyah until he stoned the Jamrah."
"5382","Hilal ibn Yasar reports performing Hajj with Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه and seeing him stop the talbiyah when descending from the pass upon seeing the houses of Makkah."
"5383","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent sheep as hady to the House."
"5384","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ sent one hundred camels as hady, covered and garlanded."
"5385","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ offered one hundred camels in his Hajj, slaughtering thirty-three with his own hand then ordering Ali عليه السلام to slaughter the remainder, commanding division of their meats, coverings and skins among the people, giving nothing thereof to the butcher, taking a piece from each camel into one pot to eat its meat and sip its broth, which was done. > **""Distribute their meats, coverings and skins among the people and give nothing thereof to the butcher, and take for us a piece of meat from every camel then place it in one pot until we eat of its meat and sip of its broth.""**"
"5386","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ the hady among them consisted of camels and cows."
"5387","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ divided sheep on the Day of Sacrifice among his Companions and commanded them to slaughter for their Umrah as sufficient for them, and Sad ibn Abi Waqqas رضي الله عنه received a billy-goat. > **""Slaughter for your Umrah, for it suffices you.""**"
"5388","Hudhayfah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ shared among the Muslims in his Hajj seven persons in a cow."
"5389","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports seeing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the year of Hudaybiyah share seven of his Companions in one badanah."
"5390","Al-Shabi reports asking Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما whether a camel and a cow suffice for seven, to which he questioned whether it bears seven souls. When told the Companions of Muhammad ﷺ claim the Messenger of Allah ﷺ legislated a camel for seven and a cow for seven, Ibn Umar asked another man for confirmation who affirmed, whereupon Ibn Umar admitted unawareness of this."
"5391","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said. > **""The camel and the cow are for seven.""**"
"5392","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports seeing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the year of Hudaybiyah make seven of his Companions partners in one badanah."
"5393","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that the emaciated and one-eyed are not permissible among sacrificial camels, and warned against the mutilated with cut ears. > **""The emaciated and one-eyed are not permissible among the sacrificial camels, and beware of the mutilated.""**"
"5394","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ passed Dhul-Hulayfah and ordered ishar, meaning of the sacrificial camels."
"5395","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ performed ishar and taqlid of the hady."
"5396","Ali رضي الله عنه was asked whether a man may ride his hady and replied there is no harm, for the Prophet ﷺ would pass men walking and order them to ride his hady, the hady of the Prophet ﷺ, declaring they would follow nothing better than the Sunnah of their Prophet ﷺ."
"5397","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to a man driving a badanah barefoot and commanded him to ride it. When the man protested it was a badanah he repeated the command, so he rode it. > **""Ride it.""** > **""Ride it.""**"
"5398","Jabir ibn Abdillah رضي الله عنهما reports that he was seated with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when the Prophet ﷺ tore his shirt from the collar downward and removed it from his legs while the people looked on, then explained that he had ordered his sacrificial camel which he had dispatched to be garlanded and marked that day, had forgetfully worn a shirt, and could not therefore remove it over his head, having dispatched the animal while himself remaining resident. > **""I was commanded concerning my sacrificial camel which I dispatched that it be garlanded today and marked at such and such place, so I wore a shirt and forgot, and I could not remove my shirt from my head.""**"
"5399","A group of Banu Salamah report through Ata ibn Yasar رحمه الله that the Prophet ﷺ was seated when he tore his garment, explaining that he had promised that a sacrificial animal would be marked that day. > **""I have promised a sacrificial animal to be marked today.""**"
"5400","Amr ibn Kharijah al-Thumali رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ dispatched sacrificial animals with him and instructed him that if any of them became exhausted he should slaughter it, stain its garland-sandal in its blood, strike its side with it, and that neither he nor his travelling companions should eat from it. > **""When any of them breaks down, slaughter it, then strike its sandal in its blood, then strike its side with it, and neither you nor your travelling companions shall eat it.""**"
"5401","Qays ibn Sad رضي الله عنه, the standard-bearer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, reports that when he intended Hajj he had combed one side of his head, then his sacrificial animal was garlanded, so he entered ihram and left the other side uncombed."
"5402","The Ansari keeper of the sacrificial camels of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ reports that after being dispatched he returned and asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ what he commanded concerning whatever among them broke down, and was instructed to slaughter it, dye its sandal in its blood, place it upon its side, and to permit neither himself nor any of his companions to eat from it. > **""Slaughter it, then dye its sandal in its blood, then place it upon its side, and neither you nor any of your travelling companions shall eat from it.""**"
"5403","Sinan ibn Salamah al-Hudhali رحمه الله reports from his father رضي الله عنه, a Companion of the Prophet ﷺ, that the Prophet ﷺ dispatched two sacrificial she-camels with a man and instructed him that if anything befell them he should slaughter them, dip the sandals in their blood, strike their sides so they would be known as sacrificial animals, striking the sides of each, eating nothing from them nor permitting his companions to eat, and leaving them for those who come after. > **""If anything befalls them, slaughter them and dip the sandal in their blood, then strike their sides therewith so it may be known that they are sacrificial animals. The sides of each one. Neither you nor any of your travelling companions shall eat from them, and leave them for those after you.""**"
"5404","Abu Qatadah رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ was asked about a man accompanying a voluntary sacrificial animal that breaks down before reaching its place, and ruled that he should slaughter it, stain its sandal with its blood, strike its side, and that if he eats from it its replacement becomes obligatory upon him. > **""He slaughters it, then stains its sandal with its blood, then strikes its side therewith, and if he eats from it its replacement is obligatory upon him.""**"
"5405","Alqamah رحمه الله reports that Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه dispatched a sacrificial animal with him and instructed him to eat a third with his companions, give a third in charity, and send a third to his brother Utbah. When Sufyan رحمه الله was asked whether it was voluntary, he affirmed that it was."
"5406","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports from the Prophet ﷺ that five creatures, all corrupt, may be killed by the one in ihram and may be killed within the Haram: the mouse, the scorpion, the snake, the vicious dog, and the crow. > **""Five, all of them corrupt, the one in ihram kills them and they are killed in the Haram: the mouse, the scorpion, the snake, the vicious dog, and the crow.""**"
"5407","Wabarah رحمه الله reports that he heard Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما saying that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded the killing of the wolf."
"5408","Abu Rafi رضي الله عنه reports that while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was in prayer he struck something, and it was a scorpion which he struck and killed, and that he commanded the one in ihram to kill the scorpion, the snake, the mouse, and the kite."
"5409","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded the killing of the gecko even in the midst of the Kaaba. > **""Kill the gecko even in the midst of the Kaaba.""**"
"5410","Abdullah ibn al-Harith ibn Nawfal رحمه الله reports that his father al-Harith رضي الله عنه was in charge of an affair of Makkah in the time of Uthman رضي الله عنه when Uthman came toward Makkah, and Abdullah met him with provisions at Qudayd where the people of the water had hunted partridges and cooked them with water and salt as broth for tharid and presented them to Uthman and his companions, who withheld. Uthman argued it was game they had neither hunted nor ordered, hunted by people in hill and offered to them, so what harm was there, and asked who held otherwise. When told it was Ali رضي الله عنه, he sent for him, and Ali came brushing straw from his hands. Uthman repeated his argument, whereupon Ali became angry and adjured by Allah whoever had been present when a wild donkey leg was brought to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and he declared his people in ihram and ordered it fed to those in hill, and twelve Companions testified. Ali then adjured concerning ostrich eggs brought to the Messenger ﷺ with the same ruling, and fewer than twelve testified. Uthman then withdrew his leg from the food and entered his tent, while the people of the water ate it. > **""We are a people in ihram, so feed it to the people in hill. We are a people in ihram, so feed it to the people in hill.""**"
"5411","In another narration concerning the same incident, five ostrich eggs were brought to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was in ihram."
"5412","In another narration, Uthman ibn Affan رضي الله عنه halted at Qudayd and partridge was brought to him in bowls raised upon their legs, so he sent for Ali رضي الله عنه while he was braiding his camel. Ali came with straw falling from his hands, and he and the people withheld. Ali asked who of Ashja was present and whether they knew that a Bedouin had brought ostrich eggs and pieces of wild game to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ who ordered them fed to his family since he was in ihram, and they affirmed. Uthman then sat back upon his couch, descended, and declared it foul for them. > **""Feed them to your family, for we are in ihram.""**"
"5413","Aishah رضي الله عنها reports that dried gazelle meat was gifted to the Prophet ﷺ while he was in ihram, and he returned it."
"5414","Al-Bara ibn Azib رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ halted at Marr al-Zahran when a limb of game was gifted to him, and he returned it to the messenger with instruction to convey peace to the sender and explain that only ihram had caused its return. > **""Convey peace to him and say to him: Were we not in ihram we would not have returned it to you.""**"
"5415","Umayr ibn Salamah al-Damri رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed al-Arj where there was a hamstrung wild ass, and shortly a man of Bahz came saying it was his shot and placing it at their disposal. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded Abu Bakr رضي الله عنه, who divided it among the companies. Then he proceeded until Aqabat al-Uthayah, where there was a gazelle struck by an arrow sheltering in the shade of a rock, so the Prophet ﷺ ordered a man of his Companions to stand there until all companies had passed and none should shoot anything at it. > **""This is my shot, so dispose of it as you wish. Stand here until the companies pass, let none shoot anything at it.""**"
"5416","Abu Said al-Khudri رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Abu Qatadah al-Ansari رضي الله عنه for charity while the Messenger ﷺ and his Companions set out in ihram until they halted at Usfan where there was a wild ass. Abu Qatadah arrived in hill, and the Companions lowered their heads out of dislike that their glances should inform him. Abu Qatadah saw it, mounted his horse, took his spear, and when his spear fell he asked them to hand it to him but they said they would not assist him in it. He then charged and wounded it, and they began roasting from it, then recalled that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was among them, having gone ahead of them. They caught up with him and asked him, and he saw no harm in it, asking whether they had any of it with them, about which Ubaydullah رحمه الله doubted. > **""Hand it to me. Is there anything of it with you?""**"
"5417","Ali ibn Abi Talib رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ permitted game meat for the one in ihram."
"5418","Abu Musa رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared game meat lawful for them so long as they had neither hunted it nor had it hunted for them while in ihram. > **""Game meat is lawful for you so long as you have not hunted it nor had it hunted for you while you are in ihram.""**"
"5419","Musab al-Makki رحمه الله reports that he met Anas ibn Malik, Zayd ibn Arqam, and al-Mughirah ibn Shubah رضي الله عنهم and heard them narrating that the Prophet ﷺ said Allah commanded a tree on the night of the cave to grow before the Prophet ﷺ and cover him, commanded the spider to weave before him and cover him, and commanded two wild doves to alight at the mouth of the cave. Youths of Quraysh from every clan advanced with sticks, clubs, and swords until they were forty cubits from the Prophet ﷺ, when one hastened to look into the cave, saw the two doves at its mouth, returned to his companions, and when asked what was wrong declared he had seen two doves and knew no one was within. The Prophet ﷺ heard his words and knew Allah had warded them off thereby, so he supplicated for the doves, invoked blessing upon them, fixed their recompense, and they were left undisturbed in the Haram. > **""What is the matter with you? I saw two doves at the mouth of the cave, so I knew there was no one in it.""**"
"5420","Umar ibn al-Khattab رضي الله عنه ruled, and the narrator thought he raised it to the Prophet ﷺ, that for a hyena struck by the one in ihram there is a sheep, for a rabbit a young goat, for a jerboa a young ewe, and for a gazelle a ram."
"5421","Qabisah ibn Jabir رحمه الله reports that while in ihram he saw a gazelle, shot it, and struck the base of its horn, and it dashed away, leaving unease in his heart. He came to Umar ibn al-Khattab رضي الله عنه to ask him and found beside him a fair-skinned man of delicate face, Abdur Rahman ibn Awf رضي الله عنه. He asked Umar, who turned to Abdur Rahman and asked whether he considered a sheep sufficient, and when he affirmed, Umar ordered him to slaughter a sheep. When they rose, a companion of his remarked that the Commander of the Believers had not answered well until he had consulted the man. Umar overheard part of his speech and raised the dirrah to strike him, then turned to strike Qabisah, who protested he had said nothing and it was the other who spoke, so Umar left him, remarking that he had intended to kill unlawfully and dispute the fatwa. He then observed that man has ten traits, nine good and one evil which corrupts them, and warned beware lest all ten be evil."
"5422","In another narration of the same incident, Qabisah inclined toward the man beside Umar, and by Allah his face was as if it were a heart in its radiance."
"5423","Aishah رضي الله عنها reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was cupped while he was in ihram."
"5424","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ was cupped while in ihram on account of a pain he suffered, and used the siwak while in ihram."
"5425","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما held that there was no harm in the money belt for the one in ihram, and he narrated that ruling from the Prophet ﷺ."
"5426","Uthman ibn Affan رضي الله عنه held that the one in ihram may enter the garden and smell sweet basil."
"5427","Abu Umamah al-Bahili رضي الله عنه reports from one who saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ setting out for Mina on the Day of Tarwiyah with Bilal رضي الله عنه at his side holding a staff upon which a garment was draped, shading the Messenger of Allah ﷺ therewith."
"5428","In the narration of al-Tabarani from Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ set out from Makkah to Mina on the Day of Tarwiyah with his procession ahead, while at his side was Bilal رضي الله عنه with a garment tied upon a staff shielding him from the heat of the sun."
"5429","Kurayb, the freedman of Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما, reports that he asked Abu Abbas concerning his ruling that no man who performs Hajj without driving the sacrifice then circumambulates the House but exits into Umrah, while no pilgrim who circumambulates it having driven the sacrifice but combines Hajj and Umrah, though the people deny this. Ibn Abbas replied with disapproval that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had set out with Companions mentioning only Hajj, then commanded whoever had no sacrifice to circumambulate the House and exit into Umrah. When one of them asked whether it was Hajj, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ clarified that it was not Hajj but Umrah. > **""Woe to you. It is not Hajj, but it is Umrah.""**"
"5430","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ arrived at Makkah with his Companions chanting the talbiyah for Hajj, and the Messenger ﷺ declared that whoever wished might make it into Umrah except whoever had the sacrifice with him. When they asked whether one of them would go to Mina with semen dripping, he affirmed. Perfume censers were lit. Ali رضي الله عنه arrived from Yemen, and the Messenger ﷺ asked with what he had entered ihram. When he replied with the same ihram as the Messenger ﷺ, he was told to proceed, for he had a share in the sacrifice with them. Humayd رحمه الله narrated it to Tawus رحمه الله, who affirmed the people had done so. > **""Whoever wishes may make it into Umrah except whoever has the sacrifice with him. Yes. With what did you enter ihram? Proceed, for you have a sacrifice with us. Make it into Umrah.""**"
"5431","Al-Bara رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and his Companions set out in ihram for Hajj, and when they arrived at Makkah he commanded them to make their Hajj into Umrah. When some asked how they could make into Umrah what they had entered as Hajj, he instructed them to consider what he commanded and do it. When they repeated their objection he became angry and went to Aishah رضي الله عنها in anger, whose face showed his anger. When she asked who had angered him that she might be angry with him, he explained he could only be angry while commanding a matter that was not followed. > **""Make your Hajj into Umrah. Consider what I command you and do it. Who has angered me that he may incur anger? Why should I not be angry while I command a matter and it is not followed?""**"
"5432","Maqil ibn Yasar رضي الله عنه reports that they performed Hajj with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and found Aishah رضي الله عنها laying aside her garments. When asked what was wrong, she said she had been told he had exited ihram and released his family. He clarified that only one without sacrifice had exited, while they had not exited since they had camels with them, until reaching Arafat. > **""What is the matter with you? Only one without sacrifice has exited, but as for us we have not exited, for we have sacrificial camels with us, until we reach Arafat.""**"
"5433","Sahl ibn Hunayf رضي الله عنه reports that they set out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ as pilgrims in ihram for Hajj, and when they arrived at Makkah he commanded them to make it into Umrah."
"5434","Urwah ibn al-Zubayr رحمه الله reports that he came to Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما and accused him of long misleading the people by ruling that a man who sets out in ihram for Hajj or Umrah and circumambulates has exited, though Abu Bakr and Umar رضي الله عنهما forbade it. Ibn Abbas retorted whether those two were weightier with him than what is in the Book of Allah and what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ established among his Companions and his nation. Urwah replied that they were more knowledgeable of the Book of Allah and the Sunnah of the Messenger ﷺ than he and Ibn Abbas. Ibn Abi Mulaykah رحمه الله observed that Urwah thereby contended with him."
"5435","Abdullah ibn Hilal al-Muzani رضي الله عنه, a Companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, held that after their generation no one may enter ihram for Hajj then annul his Hajj into Umrah."
"5436","Tariq ibn Shihab رحمه الله reports that a woman among them intended Hajj and wished to join Umrah with her Hajj, so she asked Abdullah رضي الله عنه. He replied that he found it only in the months of Hajj, for Allah عزوجل says Hajj is in well-known months."
"5437","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ declared there is no sarurah in Islam, that is, no remaining without ever performing Hajj. > **""There is no sarurah in Islam.""**"
"5438","Al-Qasim رحمه الله reports that Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه said none should say I am sarurah, for the Muslim is not sarurah, none should say I am a pilgrim, for the pilgrim is the one in ihram, but one should say I intend Makkah."
"5439","Kab ibn Ujrah رضي الله عنه reports that he suffered an ailment in his head and asked the Prophet ﷺ by what rite he should atone. He was commanded to provide a sacrificial animal, garland it, drive it until he halts it at Arafah with the people, then send it forward with the people, as is done with the sacrifice."
"5440","Kab ibn Ujrah رضي الله عنه reports that the vermin of his head tormented him, so he came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and asked about it, whereupon Allah revealed the verse that whoever among you is ill or has an ailment of the head then expiation by fasting, charity, or sacrifice. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then summoned him and asked whether he had a faraq, being three sa, to divide among six poor, or a rite of a sheep, or fasting three days. When Kab asked him to choose for him, he commanded feeding six poor. > **""Is there with you a faraq to divide among six poor, and the faraq is three sa, or a rite: a sheep, or fasting three days? Feed six poor.""**"
"5441","Al-Hirmas رضي الله عنه reports that he was riding behind his father when he saw the Prophet ﷺ upon a camel declaring his presence for Hajj and Umrah together. > **""Labbayk with a Hajj and an Umrah together.""**"
"5442","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports that they set out raising their voices with Hajj, and when they arrived at Makkah the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded them to make it into Umrah. He declared that had he known before what he came to know after he would have made it Umrah, but since he had driven the sacrifice he had joined Hajj and Umrah. > **""Make it into Umrah. Had I known before in my affair what I know now I would have made it Umrah, but I have driven the sacrifice and joined Hajj and Umrah.""**"
"5443","Suraqah رضي الله عنه reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declaring that Umrah has entered into Hajj until the Day of Resurrection, and that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ joined Hajj and Umrah in the Farewell Hajj. > **""Umrah has entered into Hajj until the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"5444","Abu Imran Aslam رحمه الله reports that he performed Hajj with his patrons and entered upon Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها, wife of the Prophet ﷺ, asking whether he should perform Umrah before Hajj. She replied he might do so before or after Hajj. When he objected that people say one who is sarurah should not perform Umrah before Hajj, and he had asked the Mothers of the Believers رضي الله عنهن who answered likewise, he returned and informed her of their reply. She confirmed it and offered further healing, having heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanding the household to chant Umrah within Hajj, elsewhere narrated as commanding the nation of Muhammad ﷺ with Hajj and Umrah. > **""Enter ihram, O family of Muhammad, with an Umrah in Hajj. Enter ihram, O nation of Muhammad, with a Hajj and an Umrah.""**"
"5445","Amr ibn Shuayb رحمه الله reports from his father from his grandfather رضي الله عنه that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ only joined Hajj and Umrah from fear of being barred from the House, saying that if it were not Hajj it would be Umrah. > **""If it is not a Hajj then it is an Umrah.""**"
"5446","Ibn Abi Awfa رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ only combined Hajj and Umrah because he knew he would not perform Hajj after that."
"5447","Zayd ibn Arqam رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed only one Hajj after migrating, and he did not perform Hajj after it: the Farewell Hajj."
"5448","Al-Hasan رحمه الله reports that Umar ibn al-Khattab رضي الله عنه intended to forbid tamattu in Hajj, whereupon Ubayy رضي الله عنه told him it was not his to forbid since they had performed tamattu with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and Umar fell silent."
"5449","Abu Shaykh al-Hunai رحمه الله reports that Muawiyah رضي الله عنه asked a group of Companions of the Prophet ﷺ whether they knew the Prophet ﷺ had forbidden tamattu, meaning tamattu of Hajj, and they replied that they did not."
"5450","Abdullah ibn Sharik al-Amiri رحمه الله reports that he heard Abdullah ibn Umar, Abdullah ibn Abbas, and Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهم asked about Umrah before Hajj in tamattu, and they replied affirmatively that it is the Sunnah of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: one arrives, circumambulates the House and between Safa and Marwa, then exits, and a day before Arafah enters ihram for Hajj, thus combining Umrah and Hajj, or Allah combines for him Umrah and Hajj."
"5451","Amir ibn Rabiah رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ performed Hajj alone without joining Umrah."
"5452","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ arrived and joined Hajj and Umrah, having driven the sacrifice, and declared that whoever had not garlanded the sacrifice should make it Umrah. > **""Whoever has not garlanded the sacrifice, let him make it Umrah.""**"
"5453","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded his wives, so they performed tamattu, and he ordered cows on their behalf."
"5454","Abu Dawud رضي الله عنه reports that they set out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and when they reached Dhu al-Hulayfah the Messenger ﷺ entered the mosque, prayed two rakahs, then entered ihram after the prayer for Hajj and Umrah together."
"5455","Aishah رضي الله عنها reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said during the Farewell Hajj that had he not driven the sacrifice he would have exited ihram, while he had entered ihram for Umrah and Hajj. > **""Had I not driven the sacrifice I would have exited.""**"
"5456","Al-Bara ibn Azib رضي الله عنه reports that he was with Ali رضي الله عنه when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ appointed him over Yemen, and he obtained some ounces of silver with him. When Ali came to the Messenger ﷺ, Fatimah رضي الله عنها said she had perfumed the house and asked what was wrong with him since the Messenger ﷺ had commanded his Companions and they had exited. Ali replied he had entered ihram with the ihram of the Prophet ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ explained he had driven the sacrifice and joined Hajj and Umrah, telling his Companions that had he known before what he knew after he would have done as they did, but he had driven the sacrifice and joined. He then instructed Ali to slaughter sixty-seven or sixty-six of the camels, keeping thirty-three or thirty-four for himself, retaining a piece from every camel. > **""I have driven the sacrifice and joined. Had I known before in my affair what I know now I would have done as you have done, but I have driven the sacrifice and joined. Slaughter sixty-seven or sixty-six of the camels, and keep for yourself thirty-three or thirty-four, and keep from every camel a piece.""**"
"5457","Ali ibn Abi Talib رضي الله عنه reports that to his knowledge they set out as pilgrims chanting Hajj, and neither the Messenger of Allah ﷺ nor Umar رضي الله عنه exited ihram until they had circumambulated the House and between Safa and Marwa."
"5458","Aishah رضي الله عنها reports from the Prophet ﷺ that whoever fasts the days in Hajj while performing tamattu and finds no sacrifice, its time is from the ihram of one of them until the Day of Arafah, which is the last of them. > **""Whoever fasts the days in Hajj and finds no sacrifice while performing tamattu, it is from the ihram of one of you until the Day of Arafah, and that is the last of them.""**"
"5459","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ named the Farewell Hajj the Hajj of Islam. > **""The Farewell Hajj is the Hajj of Islam.""**"
"5460","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ changed both garments of ihram at al-Tanim when entering Makkah."
"5461","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports from the Prophet ﷺ that hands are raised only in seven places: when opening prayer, when entering the Sacred Mosque and looking at the House, when standing upon Safa, when standing upon Marwa, when standing with the people on the evening of Arafah and at Muzdalifah and the two stations, and when stoning the jamrah. > **""Hands are raised only in seven places: when opening prayer, when entering the Sacred Mosque and looking at the House, when standing upon Safa, when standing upon Marwa, when standing with the people on the evening of Arafah and at Muzdalifah and the two stations, and when stoning the jamrah.""**"
"5462","Hudhayfah ibn Usayd رضي الله عنه reports that when the Prophet ﷺ looked at the House he supplicated for its increase in honour, veneration, nobility, righteousness, and awe. > **""O Allah, increase this House of Yours in honour, veneration, nobility, righteousness, and awe.""**"
"5463","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered with them through the abode of Banu Abd Manaf, which the people call the Gate of Banu Shaybah, and they exited with him toward Madinah through the Gate of al-Harurah, which is the Gate of al-Hannatin."
"5464","Abu Bakr al-Siddiq رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ sent him with the declaration of disavowal to the people of Makkah: no idolater shall perform Hajj after this year, no naked person shall circumambulate the House, none shall enter Paradise but a Muslim soul, whoever has a treaty with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ then its term extends to its term, and Allah and His Messenger are free of the idolaters. After he had journeyed three days, the Prophet ﷺ told Ali عليه السلام to catch up with him, return Abu Bakr to him, and convey it himself. When Abu Bakr came to the Prophet ﷺ weeping and asked whether anything had come concerning him, he was told only good had come, but command had been given that none should convey it but he himself or a man from him. > **""No idolater shall perform Hajj after this year, no naked person shall circumambulate the House, none shall enter Paradise but a Muslim soul, and whoever has a treaty with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ then its term is to its term. Allah is free of the idolaters and His Messenger. Catch up with him, return Abu Bakr to me, and convey it yourself. Nothing has come concerning you but good, but I was commanded that none convey it but I or a man from me.""**"
"5465","Nafi رحمه الله reports that when Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما entered the nearest part of the Haram he ceased the talbiyah, and when he reached Dhu Tuwa he spent the night there until morning, then prayed the dawn prayer, bathed, and narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to do so. He then entered Makkah in the forenoon, came to the House, touched the Stone saying in the name of Allah with Allah being greatest, performed raml in three circuits while walking between the two Rukns, and when he came upon the Stone he touched it and pronounced takbir, completing four circuits walking. He then came to the Maqam, prayed two rakahs, returned to the Stone and touched it, exited to Safa through the great gate, stood upon it pronouncing takbir seven times, thrice with takbir, then declaring there is no god but Allah alone without partner, to Him belongs dominion and praise, and He has power over all things."
"5466","Abu al-Tufayl رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ performed raml from the Stone to the Stone."
"5467","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked in the year of Hajj about raml and replied that Allah had prescribed hastening upon them so they should hasten. > **""Verily Allah has prescribed hastening upon you, so hasten.""**"
"5468","Sahl ibn Hunayf رضي الله عنه reports that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed Umrah while on the road, he wished they might find a fat camel to slaughter and eat so the people would see their strength. Umar ibn al-Khattab رضي الله عنه suggested calling for the provisions of the people and supplicating over them, for Allah would bless them, and the Messenger ﷺ did so. The Messenger ﷺ then instructed that upon arrival they should perform raml in the first three circuits to display their strength, and on that day he commanded giving glad tidings that whoever declares there is no god but Allah, Paradise becomes obligatory for him. > **""Were we to find a fat camel we would slaughter it and eat it so they see our strength. When you arrive, perform raml in the first three circuits so you display your strength. Give people glad tidings that whoever says: There is no god but Allah, Paradise becomes obligatory for him.""**"
"5469","Hilal ibn Zayd رحمه الله reports that he saw Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه in the going about the House walking between the Yemeni Rukn and the Black Rukn during the three circuits in Hajj and Umrah. He then heard Anas saying he had seen the Messenger of Allah ﷺ doing so."
"5470","Ali رضي الله عنه, when he touched the Stone, would say with faith in Allah, affirmation of His Book, and adherence to the Sunnah of His Prophet ﷺ that he did so."
"5471","Nafi رحمه الله reports that when Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما touched the Stone he would say with faith in Allah, affirmation of His Book and the Sunnah of His Prophet, and then send blessings upon the Prophet ﷺ."
"5472","Yala ibn Umayyah رضي الله عنه reports that he circumambulated with Umar ibn al-Khattab رضي الله عنه, and when at the Rukn facing the door toward the Stone he took his hand that he might touch it. Umar asked whether he had circumambulated with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and when he affirmed asked whether he had seen him touching it. When he replied he had not, Umar told him to pass on, for in the Messenger of Allah ﷺ there is a fine example for him."
"5473","Ya'la رضي الله عنه reports performing tawaf with Uthman رضي الله عنه while touching the corner, with Ya'la positioned toward the House. When they reached the western corner adjoining the Black Stone, Ya'la pulled Uthman's hand so he would touch it, and Uthman questioned his purpose and whether he should touch, then reminded him of tawaf with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, of never seeing him touch the two western corners, of his excellent example, and commanded him to move on."
"5474","Abu al-Tufayl رضي الله عنه reports that Mu'awiyah رضي الله عنه and Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما arrived, and Ibn Abbas touched all the corners. Mu'awiyah remarked that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had touched only the two Yemeni corners, while Ibn Abbas replied that none of its corners is to be abandoned."
"5475","Abdullah ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr reports hearing his father ask Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما why he touched only the Black Stone and the Yemeni Corner. Ibn Umar explained that he had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promise expiation of sins through their touching, the reward of freeing a slave for carefully counting seven circuits followed by two rak'ahs, and ten good deeds recorded with ten sins erased and ten degrees raised for every foot lifted and set down. > **""Indeed touching them both wipes away sins.""** > **""Whoever performs seven circuits counting them and prays two rak'ahs shall have the like of freeing a slave.""** > **""No man lifts a foot nor sets it down except ten good deeds are written for him, ten evil deeds are removed from him, and ten degrees are raised for him.""**"
"5476","Umar ibn al-Khattab رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed him as a strong man and commanded him not to jostle at the Stone lest he harm the weak, to touch it only when finding it empty, and otherwise to face it with tahlil and takbir. > **""O Umar, you are a strong man. Do not crowd at the Stone lest you harm the weak. If you find it empty then touch it, otherwise face it, declare la ilaha illa Allah, and magnify Allah.""**"
"5477","Abu Ya'fur al-Abdi reports hearing a man returning from Hajj from Makkah state that Umar رضي الله عنه used to press through the crowd at the Corner."
"5478","Amir ibn Rabi'ah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would not touch among the corners except the Yemeni Corner and the Black Stone."
"5479","Abdur Rahman ibn Awf رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked how he had acted concerning touching the two corners. When he answered that he had done both, touching at times and leaving at times, the Prophet ﷺ approved his conduct. > **""How did you act concerning touching the two corners? You have done right.""**"
"5480","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports seeing Umar ibn al-Khattab رضي الله عنه kiss the Stone and place his forehead upon it, then return again to kiss it and prostrate upon it, explaining that he had seen the Messenger of Allah ﷺ act in that manner."
"5481","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would kiss the Yemeni Corner and place his cheek upon it."
"5482","Sa'd ibn Tariq reports from his father that he saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ performing tawaf of the House, touching the Stone with a crooked staff in his hand whenever the people crowded upon it."
"5483","Zayd ibn Jubayr reports that a man asked Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما about the Stone and its wiping when prevented from reaching it and unable to wipe it. Abdullah replied that they used to strike toward it with sticks when unable to wipe it."
"5484","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما enjoins tawaf of this House and touching of this Stone, explaining that they were two stones brought down from Paradise, one of which has been raised while the other shall soon be raised, and swearing by his own disgrace at his grave if his word proves untrue."
"5485","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reports in another narration that Jibril عليه السلام descended with this Stone from Paradise, urging enjoyment of it while it remains among them as a source of good, before it soon returns to whence it came."
"5486","Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As رضي الله عنهما reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ describing the Corner coming on the Day of Resurrection greater than Abu Qubays, possessing a tongue and two lips, bearing witness for whoever touched it in truth as the right hand of Allah عزوجل with which He shakes the hands of His creation. > **""The Corner shall come on the Day of Resurrection greater than Abu Qubays, having a tongue and two lips.""** > **""It shall bear witness for whoever touched it in truth, and it is the right hand of Allah عزوجل with which He shakes the hands of His creation.""**"
"5487","Aishah رضي الله عنها reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanding that good be testified for this Stone, for on the Day of Resurrection it shall intercede with accepted intercession, possessing a tongue and two lips with which it testifies for whoever touched it. > **""Bear witness of good for this Stone, for on the Day of Resurrection it shall be an intercessor whose intercession is accepted, having a tongue and two lips, bearing witness for whoever touched it.""**"
"5488","Anas رضي الله عنه reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that the Black Stone is among the stones of Paradise. > **""The Black Stone is among the stones of Paradise.""**"
"5489","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declaring that Allah shall raise the Black Stone and the Yemeni Corner on the Day of Resurrection with two eyes, a tongue and two lips, testifying for whoever touched them faithfully. > **""Allah shall raise the Black Stone and the Yemeni Corner on the Day of Resurrection, having two eyes, a tongue and two lips, testifying for whoever touched them faithfully.""**"
"5490","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports from the Prophet ﷺ that the Black Stone is from the stones of Paradise with nothing else on earth from Paradise besides it, that it was white as a wild cow, and that were it not for contact with the filth of Jahiliyya no afflicted person touching it would fail to be healed. > **""The Black Stone is from the stones of Paradise, and there is nothing on earth from Paradise besides it. It was white as a wild cow, and were it not for what touched it of the filth of Jahiliyya, no afflicted person touching it would fail to be healed.""**"
"5491","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declaring that were it not for contamination of the Corner by the impurities and filth of Jahiliyya and the hands of oppressors and sinners, every diseased person would seek healing through it. > **""Were it not for what has affected the Corner of the impurities of Jahiliyya and its filth, and the hands of oppressors and sinners, whoever suffers disease would seek healing through it.""**"
"5492","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports the Prophet ﷺ declaring that were it not for contamination by the impurities of Jahiliyya and the hands of oppressors and sinners, every afflicted person would be healed through it and it would remain today as Allah created it. Its blackness was changed lest the people of the Fire gaze upon the adornment of Paradise and incline toward it. It is a ruby among the rubies of Paradise which Allah placed at the site of the Ka'bah when He sent down Adam عليه السلام before the Ka'bah existed, while the earth was pure without disobedience or defiling inhabitants. A rank of angels stood at the boundaries of the Sanctuary guarding it from the dwellers of the earth, then the jinn, who were not permitted to look upon it since it is from Paradise and whoever looks upon Paradise enters it, except one for whom Paradise is due. The angels repelled them from every side while stationed at the boundaries, for which reason it was named the Sanctuary, as they intervened between them and it. > **""Were it not for what has affected the Corner of the impurities of Jahiliyya and its filth, and the hands of oppressors and sinners, whoever suffers affliction would be healed through it, and it would be found today as it was the day Allah created it. It was changed to black lest the people of the Fire look upon the adornment of Paradise and turn toward it. It is a ruby among the rubies of Paradise which Allah placed at the site of the Ka'bah when He sent down Adam, before the Ka'bah existed, while the earth was pure, with nothing of disobedience wrought therein and no people to defile it. A rank of angels was placed at the boundaries of the Sanctuary guarding it from the dwellers of the earth, whose dwellers then were the jinn. It was not proper for them to look upon it, for it is something from Paradise, and whoever looks upon something from Paradise enters it, so it was not proper that any look upon it except one for whom Paradise is due. The angels drove them from it while standing at the boundaries of the Sanctuary, casting back whoever came from every side, and for that it was named the Sanctuary, for they intervened between them and it.""**"
"5493","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reports that the Black Corner descended from heaven and was placed upon Abu Qubays like a white wild cow, remaining there forty years before being set upon the foundations of Ibrahim عليه السلام."
"5494","Qudamah ibn Abdullah رضي الله عنه reports seeing the Prophet ﷺ upon a she-camel touching the Corner with his crooked staff."
"5495","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed tawaf upon his mount on the day of the conquest of Makkah, touching the corners with a staff that was with him."
"5496","Abu Rafi' رضي الله عنه reports seeing the Prophet ﷺ perform tawaf of the House upon his mount, touching the Corner with his staff."
"5497","Abdullah ibn Hanzalah رضي الله عنه reports seeing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ perform tawaf of the House upon his mount, touching the Corner with his staff."
"5498","Abu Malik al-Ashja'i reports from his father that the Prophet ﷺ performed tawaf of the House upon his mount, touching the Corner with his staff."
"5499","Aishah رضي الله عنها reports that the Prophet ﷺ performed tawaf on a camel on the day of the conquest with a staff, touching the Corner therewith out of dislike that the people be struck away from him."
"5500","Amir ibn Rabi'ah رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ was performing tawaf of the House when the strap of his sandal broke. A man produced a strap from his own sandal to fasten it upon the Prophet's ﷺ sandal, but he pulled it away, declaring it preferment which he does not love. > **""This is preferment, and I do not love preferment.""**"
"5501","Jabir ibn Abdullah رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ performed tawaf of the House in his Hajj upon his Jad'a she-camel, with Abdullah ibn Umm Maktum رضي الله عنه holding its halter and chanting rajaz."
"5502","Abdullah ibn Amir ibn Rabi'ah reports seeing Abdur Rahman ibn Awf رضي الله عنه perform tawaf of the House while chanting hida and wearing leather socks. Umar رضي الله عنه expressed wonder whether his chanting around the House or his tawaf in socks was stranger, and Abdur Rahman replied that he had done so in the time of one better than Umar, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who had not censured him."
"5503","Nusayr ibn Dhuluq reports seeing Ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما perform tawaf wrapped in a cloak of his."
"5504","Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir reports from his father that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ likened seven circuits of the House performed without idle speech to freeing a slave. > **""Whoever performs seven circuits of the House without idle speech therein shall be like freeing a slave.""**"
"5505","Abu al-Zubayr reports asking Jabir رضي الله عنه about tawaf of the Ka'bah. He explained that they would perform tawaf wiping the Corner at the beginning and the end, and would not perform tawaf after dawn prayer until sunrise nor after Asr until sunset, having heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ describe sunrise in the horn of Satan. > **""The sun rises in the horn of Satan.""**"
"5506","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ an address to Banu Abd Manaf forbidding them to prevent anyone from performing tawaf of this House and praying at any hour of night or day. > **""O Banu Abd Manaf, do not prevent anyone performing tawaf of this House at any hour of night or day, and praying.""**"
"5507","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ warning Banu Abd Manaf never to be found preventing anyone from performing tawaf of this House for an hour of night or day. > **""O Banu Abd Manaf, let me never find you preventing anyone performing tawaf of this House for an hour of night or day.""**"
"5508","Amr ibn Dinar reports seeing Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما perform seven circuits after Asr followed by two rak'ahs, then explaining that dislike applies only at sunrise because the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared the sun rises between the two horns of Satan. > **""The sun rises between the two horns of Satan.""**"
"5509","Abu Shu'bah reports seeing al-Hasan and al-Husayn رضي الله عنهما perform tawaf after Asr and pray two rak'ahs."
"5510","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promising forgiveness however great the sins for two tawafs: tawaf after dawn prayer concluding at sunrise, and tawaf after Asr concluding at sunset. When asked about performance shortly before or after those limits, he ruled it joined thereto. > **""Two tawafs whose performer shall be forgiven his sins however great they be: tawaf after dawn prayer concluding at sunrise, and tawaf after Asr concluding at sunset. It is joined thereto.""**"
"5511","Al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was performing tawaf of the House and sought drink while circumambulating."
"5512","Jabir رضي الله عنه, Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما and Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما report that the Prophet ﷺ, he and his Companions, performed only a single tawaf for their Umrah and their Hajj."
"5513","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports his disagreement with Zayd ibn Thabit رضي الله عنه concerning qiran."
"5514","Sa'd ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports performing tawaf with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, with some performing seven circuits, some eight, and some more, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared there was no harm. > **""No harm.""**"
"5515","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ recited before dawn, then prayed six rak'ahs glancing right and left after every two rak'ahs, leading them to suppose two rak'ahs for every seven circuits, without giving salams."
"5516","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports from the Prophet ﷺ that what lies between the Corner and the Maqam is the Multazam, where no afflicted person supplicates but is healed. > **""What lies between the Corner and the Maqam is the Multazam. No afflicted person supplicates therein but is healed.""**"
"5517","Al-Mughirah ibn Abi Hakim reports sitting with Abdullah ibn Sa'd ibn Khaythamah رضي الله عنه when a man performed tawaf, prayed two rak'ahs in the courtyard of the House, then stood and clung to the House. Abdullah declared it an innovation they had never practiced and remarked he would not be satisfied until striking it with his posterior. Then another man reaching the mosque gate raised his hands facing the House as if supplicating, and Abdullah again declared it an innovation they had never practiced. When asked whether he had witnessed Badr, Abdullah affirmed he had, along with al-Aqabah with his father."
"5518","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ never performed tawaf around anything except that it belongs to the House."
"5519","Aishah رضي الله عنها states she makes no distinction between praying in the Hijr and praying inside the House."
"5520","Ali ibn Abi Talib رضي الله عنه reports seeing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with his garment lifted until it reached his knees."
"5521","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ walked in one year and hastened in another year."
"5522","Habibah bint Abi Tajrah رضي الله عنها reports seeing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ moving between Safa and Marwa with the people ahead of him and him behind them, hastening so intensely that she saw his knees with his izar whirling about him, while commanding hastening because Allah عزوجل had prescribed sa'y. > **""Hasten, for Allah عزوجل has prescribed sa'y upon you.""**"
"5523","Safiyyah bint Shaybah رضي الله عنها reports that a woman informed her she had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ between Safa and Marwa commanding sa'y as prescribed. > **""Sa'y has been prescribed upon you, so perform sa'y.""**"
"5524","Tamlik رضي الله عنها reports observing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ from her chamber between Safa and Marwa while he declared that Allah عزوجل had prescribed sa'y and commanded its performance. > **""Indeed Allah عزوجل has prescribed sa'y upon you, so perform sa'y.""**"
"5525","Umm Walad Shaybah reports seeing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ hastening between Safa and Marwa and declaring that the valley floor is crossed only at a run. > **""The valley floor is crossed only at a run.""**"
"5526","Safiyyah bint Shaybah رضي الله عنها reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanding hastening because Allah عزوجل had prescribed sa'y. > **""Hasten, for Allah عزوجل has prescribed sa'y upon you.""**"
"5527","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was questioned and affirmed that Allah had prescribed sa'y and commanded its performance. > **""Indeed Allah has prescribed sa'y upon you, so perform sa'y.""**"
"5528","Alqamah reports that Abdullah رضي الله عنه stood upon Safa at a cleft therein and swore by Him besides whom there is no god that there stood the station of the one upon whom Surah al-Baqarah was revealed ﷺ."
"5529","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out from the mosque toward Safa through the gate of Banu Makhzum."
"5530","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Ansar رضي الله عنهم deemed sa'y between Safa and Marwa a matter of Jahiliyya, whereupon Allah عزوجل revealed that Safa and Marwa are among the symbols of Allah, so whoever performs Hajj to the House or Umrah bears no sin in circumambulating them. > **""Indeed Safa and Marwa are among the symbols of Allah, so whoever performs Hajj to the House or Umrah, there is no sin upon him in circumambulating them.""**"
"5531","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما explains concerning circumambulation of Safa and Marwa that it is voluntary with no sin upon its performer, and no harm upon whoever leaves it."
"5532","Abu al-Tufayl reports asking Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما whether his people truthfully claim the Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed sa'y between Safa and Marwa as a Sunnah. Ibn Abbas confirmed their truthfulness, explaining that when Ibrahim عليه السلام was commanded the rites, Satan confronted him at the place of sa'y and raced him, but Ibrahim outran him."
"5533","Ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه reports that when the Prophet ﷺ hastened in the belly of the valley he supplicated for forgiveness and mercy through the Mightiest and Most Noble. > **""O Allah, forgive and have mercy, and You are the Mightiest, Most Noble.""**"
"5534","Abdur Rahman ibn Tariq ibn Alqamah reports from his uncle that when the Prophet ﷺ reached a place in the abode of Ya'la he faced the House and supplicated."
"5535","Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Thaqafi reports attending the sermon of Ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما at the season one day before Tarwiya while in ihram, appearing as a handsome middle-aged man in two white garments. He ascended the minbar, greeted the people who returned his greeting, recited the finest talbiyah ever heard, praised Allah and extolled Him, then addressed the pilgrims come as delegations to Allah from distant horizons, affirming Allah's honoring of His delegation, the unfailing hope of seekers of what is with Allah, confirmation of words by deeds, care for intention and hearts, and fear of Allah in these days of forgiveness, undertaken neither for trade, wealth nor worldly gain. He repeated talbiyah with the people and spoke at length, then expounded the known months of Hajj as Shawwal, Dhu al-Qa'dah and ten of Dhu al-Hijjah, with no intercourse, no abuse and no disputing therein, Allah's knowledge of all good, provision of piety as the best provision, permissibility of seeking bounty through trade, the standing at Arafat until sunset followed by pouring forth, and remembrance of Allah at the Sacred Monument of Muzdalifa as He guided them."
"5536","Abdullah ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما held it commendable to pray Zuhr at Mina on Tarwiya day when able, because the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had prayed Zuhr at Mina on Tarwiya day."
"5537","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ announcing one day before Tarwiya that their morrow's abode, God willing, would be at the right side of Khayf where the polytheists had sworn upon disbelief. > **""Our abode tomorrow, God willing, shall be at the right side of Khayf, where the polytheists apportioned upon disbelief.""**"
"5538","Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما describes the Sunna of the pilgrim as praying Zuhr, Asr, Maghrib, Isha and Fajr of Tarwiya day at Mina, then proceeding as Allah decreed, addressing the people after the sun declines, alighting to join Zuhr and Asr, standing at Arafah and departing at sunset, praying Maghrib where Allah decreed, standing at Muzdalifa and praying Fajr at dawn, departing in the morning, and becoming lawful in all prohibitions except women after stoning the Jamrah until performing tawaf of the House."
"5539","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reports that Jibril عليه السلام brought Ibrahim عليه السلام to Mina where he prayed Zuhr, Asr, Maghrib, Isha and Fajr, then to Arafat where he joined two prayers and stood until sunset, then to Muzdalifa where he lodged the night and prayed with the utmost haste of any Muslim, then to Mina where he stoned, sacrificed and shaved. Allah عزوجل then revealed to Muhammad ﷺ to follow the upright religion of Ibrahim who was not among the polytheists. In another version a weakened man of mounts and family asked about hastening, and Abdullah recounted Ibrahim's tawaf of the House, sa'y between Safa and Marwa, and Zuhr at Mina in like manner."
"5540","Jubayr ibn Mut'im رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ that all of Arafat is a standing place apart from the belly of Uranah, all of Muzdalifa is a standing place apart from Muhassir, all the passes of Mina are slaughter places, and all the days of Tashriq are for slaughter, with a variant naming all the passes of Makkah as slaughter places. > **""All of Arafat is a standing place, but keep above the belly of Uranah. All of Muzdalifa is a standing place, but keep above Muhassir. All the passes of Mina are slaughter places, and all the days of Tashriq are for slaughter.""**"
"5541","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports from the Prophet ﷺ that all of Arafah is a standing place and all of Mina is a slaughter place. > **""All of Arafah is a standing place, and all of Mina is a slaughter place.""**"
"5542","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declaring all of Muzdalifa a sacred monument while keeping above the belly of Uranah, and all of Arafat a standing place while keeping above the valley of Muhassir. > **""All of Muzdalifa is a sacred monument, but keep above the belly of Uranah. All of Arafat is a standing place, but keep above the valley of Muhassir.""**"
"5543","Mujahid reports from Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما, as conveying from the Prophet ﷺ, that Hajj is Arafat. > **""Hajj is Arafat.""**"
"5544","Rabi'ah ibn Abbad reports from his father that he saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ standing with the polytheists at Arafat before his mission, then saw him after his mission standing in that same station, and thus knew Allah عزوجل had guided him thereto."
"5545","Abdul Aziz ibn Qays al-Abdi reports hearing Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما describe a youth riding behind the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on Arafah day gazing at women. The Messenger ﷺ repeatedly turned his face away with his hand from behind while the youth kept gazing, then instructed him as his nephew that whoever controls hearing, sight and tongue on that day shall be forgiven. > **""O son of my brother, this is a day in which whoever controls his hearing, his sight and his tongue shall be forgiven.""**"
"5546","Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As رضي الله عنهما reports the Prophet ﷺ declaring that Allah عزوجل boasts to His angels of the people of Arafah on the evening of Arafah, bidding them look upon servants come disheveled and dusty. > **""Indeed Allah عزوجل boasts to His angels of the people of Arafah on the evening of Arafah, saying: Look upon My servants come to Me disheveled and dusty.""**"
"5547","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declaring that Allah boasts to the angels of the people of Arafat, bidding them look upon disheveled dusty servants. > **""Indeed Allah boasts to the angels of the people of Arafat, saying: Look upon My servants disheveled and dusty.""**"
"5548","Abdullah ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ a promise for one who repeats ten phrases of glorification a thousand times on the eve of Arafah, that Allah grants whatever he asks except severance of kinship or sin. > **""Whoever says on the eve of Arafah these ten phrases a thousand times, he shall not ask Allah for anything but He shall give it to him, except severance of kinship or sin: Glory be to Him Whose Throne is in the heaven, glory be to Him Whose authority is in the earth, glory be to Him Whose path is in the sea, glory be to Him Whose dominion is in the Fire, glory be to Him Whose mercy is in Paradise, glory be to Him Whose decree is in the graves, glory be to Him Whose spirit is in the air, glory be to Him Who raised the heaven, glory be to Him Who spread the earth, glory be to Him from Whom there is no refuge except to Him.""**"
"5549","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports among the supplications of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the Farewell Hajj a humble plea of the needy sinner seeking mercy. > **""O Allah, You hear my speech and see my place and know my secret and my open, nothing of my affair is hidden from You. I am the wretched, the poor, the seeker of aid, the seeker of refuge, the fearful, the acknowledging confessor of his sin. I ask You the asking of the destitute, and I beseech You the beseeching of the sinful lowly, and I call upon You the supplication of the fearful blind, whose neck is humbled to You, whose eyes overflow to You, whose body is abased, whose nose is dusted for You. O Allah, do not make me wretched by my supplication to You, and be to me Kind, Merciful, O Best of those asked and O Best of givers.""**"
"5550","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reports that the most frequent supplication of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the Day of Arafah was the declaration of divine unity and power. > **""There is no god but Allah alone, without partner. To Him belongs dominion and to Him belongs praise, and He is over all things Powerful.""**"
"5551","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promising forgiveness on the evening of Arafah for every heart containing even mustard-seed faith, and when asked whether it was specific to the people of Arafah, he declared it general for Muslims. > **""When it is the evening of Arafah, there remains none in whose heart is even a mustard-seed weight of faith but he is forgiven. Rather, for the Muslims generally.""**"
"5552","Talib ibn Salma ibn Asim ibn al-Hakam reports from a family member who heard his grandfather state that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ announced on that day Allah's regard for the gathering, acceptance of its righteous, intercession for its wrongdoers, and pardon of all. > **""Verily Allah looked upon this gathering and accepted from their righteous and interceded their righteous for their wrongdoers, and pardoned them all.""**"
"5553","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declaring no days better to Allah than the ten days of Dhu al-Hijjah, better than equal jihad except one whose face is dusted in the soil, and no day better than Arafah, when Allah descends to the lowest heaven, boasts of earth-dwellers to heaven-dwellers, and frees most from the Fire. > **""There are no days better to Allah than the days of the ten of Dhu al-Hijjah. They are better than their number in jihad in the path of Allah, except a dusted one who dusts his face in the dust. There is no day better to Allah than the Day of Arafah. Allah descends to the lowest heaven and boasts of the people of the earth to the people of the heaven, saying: Look at My servants, disheveled, dusty, chanting aloud, come from every deep pass, though they have not seen My mercy nor seen My punishment. I have never seen a day with more freed from the Fire than the Day of Arafah.""**"
"5554","Abdur Rahman ibn Yazid reports bathing with Ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه on the Day of Arafah beneath an arak tree."
"5555","Abdul Majid al-Uqayli reports travelling for Hajj when Yazid ibn al-Muhallab came forth, then after completing rites coming to a water in the highlands, finding elders with dyed beards by a well, being directed to the house of the aged reclining Companion al-Ad-da ibn Khalid al-Kilabi رضي الله عنه, who asked about Yazid's call to Allah's Book and the Prophet's ﷺ sunnah, advised sitting back thrice for success and guidance, and testified seeing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on Arafah standing in both stirrups calling at highest voice about the sacred day, month, land, and inviolability of blood and wealth until meeting the Lord, raising hands to heaven invoking Allah's witness repeatedly. > **""O people, what day is this day of yours? What month is this month of yours? What land is this land of yours? Your day is a sacred day and your month is a sacred month. Verily your blood and your wealth are sacred upon you, like the sacredness of this day of yours, in this month of yours, in this land of yours, until the day you meet your Lord, Blessed and Exalted, when He shall ask you about your deeds. O Allah, bear witness over them.""**"
"5556","Urwah ibn Mudarris ibn Aws ibn Harithah ibn Lam رضي الله عنه reports performing Hajj in the Prophet's ﷺ time, reaching the people only by night at Jam', proceeding to Arafat and returning to Jam', then complaining of wearing himself and his mount, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ defined completion of Hajj by joining Fajr and the departure after prior standing at Arafat by night or day. > **""Whoever prays with us the dawn prayer at Jam' and stands with us until we depart, having departed before that from Arafat by night or by day, then his Hajj is complete and he has fulfilled his tafath.""**"
"5557","Urwah ibn Mudarris رضي الله عنه reports coming to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at Jam' before departure, complaining of folding the two mountains and meeting hardship, whereupon the Messenger ﷺ assured him, with Abdullah ibn Ahmad's addition bidding him end anxiety, that catching the departure is catching Hajj. > **""Whoever catches our departure has caught the Hajj. Calm your terror; whoever catches this departure of ours has indeed caught the Hajj.""**"
"5558","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ defining catching Hajj by reaching Arafah before dawn. > **""Whoever catches Arafah before the rising of dawn has caught the Hajj.""**"
"5559","Miswar ibn Makhramah رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressing at Arafat after praise of Allah, contrasting the polytheists' early departure when the sun stood over mountain tops like men's turbans, and from al-Mash'ar al-Haram while sunlight spread, with his own departure after setting. > **""As for what follows, the people of polytheism and idols used to depart from this place when the sun stood over the mountain tops as though turbans of men upon their faces, whereas we depart after it has set.""**"
"5560","Abu Bakr al-Siddiq رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, when the sun set at Arafah, departed, and departed from Muzdalifah before sunrise."
"5561","Maysarah al-Ashja'i reports performing Hajj with Abdullah ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما until standing at Arafat, being told to lean on the mountain, then upon people's departure being restrained to a measured pace between paces, continuing past the mountain despite requesting dismount, reaching Jam' where Ibn Umar called adhan then iqamah for Maghrib then iqamah for Isha, repeating at dawn as at the first Mash'ar, then explaining that polytheists would not leave Arafat until the sun turbaned mountain tops like turbans on faces whereas the Messenger ﷺ would not leave until sunset, and would not leave Jam' until they cried let Thabir shine and the sun stood like turbans whereas the Messenger ﷺ would leave before sunrise."
"5562","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbidding departure on Arafah day before the imam departs. > **""Do not depart on the Day of Arafah until the imam departs.""**"
"5563","Abdur Rahman ibn Yazid reports performing Hajj with Ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه in Uthman's رضي الله عنه caliphate, asking at Arafah whether the sun had set, Ibn Mas'ud judging departure by Amir al-Mu'minin then correct, Uthman departing almost simultaneously, people hastening while Ibn Mas'ud kept a measured pace until Jam'."
"5564","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports hastening began from Bedouins standing at people's edges with hanging sticks, quivers and bowls whose clatter rushed the people, while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was seen with his she-camel's temple touching its hump commanding calm. > **""O people, upon you is tranquility. O people, upon you is tranquility.""**"
"5565","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ departing from Arafat chanting a rajaz verse of hastening to Allah in weariness upon a mount whose faith opposes the Christians. > **""To You it hastens early, restless, its girth heaving, its religion opposing the religion of the Christians.""**"
"5566","Abdur Rahman ibn Yazid reports departing with Ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه from Arafah, Uthman رضي الله عنه standing at Muzdalifah until daylight, Ibn Mas'ud judging immediate departure correct, and Uthman departing before Abdullah finished speaking."
"5567","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ standing at Jam' and departing when all things grew bright before sunrise."
"5568","Ubadah ibn al-Samit رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ announcing on Arafah Allah's favor, forgiveness except mutual claims, gifting the wrongdoer to the righteous and granting the righteous their requests, commanding departure in Allah's name, then announcing at Jam' forgiveness of the righteous, intercession for the corrupt, enveloping mercy, forgiveness spreading to every repentant guardian of tongue and hand, while Iblis and his troops on Arafat's mountain, witnessing Allah's dealing, cry woe at forgiveness covering those he lured for ages, dispersing in ruin. > **""O people, Allah Almighty has favored you this day and forgiven you except mutual claims among you, and gifted your wrongdoer to your righteous, and given your righteous what he asked, so depart in the name of Allah. Allah has forgiven your righteous and interceded your righteous for your corrupt. Mercy descends and envelops them, then forgiveness is spread in the earth and falls upon every repentant who guarded his tongue and his hand. Iblis and his troops are upon the mountain of Arafat looking at what Allah does with them, and when forgiveness descends he and his troops call for woe, saying: I lured them for ages of time, then forgiveness came and covered them? So they disperse calling for woe and destruction.""**"
"5569","Anas رضي الله عنه reports hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ describe Allah favoring Arafat's people, boasting to angels over disheveled dusty servants from every deep pass, witnessing answered supplication, accepted intercession, gifting wrongdoer to righteous and granting requests except mutual claims, then at Jam' renewing the witness with guarantee of mutual claims. > **""Allah favored the people of Arafat, boasting of them to the angels, saying: O My angels, look at My servants, disheveled, dusty, advancing toward Me from every deep pass. I make you witness that I have answered their supplication, interceded their subjects, gifted their wrongdoer to their righteous, and given their righteous all they asked Me, except mutual claims among them. When the people depart to Jam' and stand renewing desire and seeking to Allah, He says: O My angels, My servants stood and renewed desire and seeking. I make you witness that I have answered their supplication, interceded their subjects, gifted their wrongdoer to their righteous, given their righteous all they asked Me, and guaranteed from them the mutual claims among them.""**"
"5570","Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sending her forward among his weak family on Muzdalifah night, whereupon she stoned the Jamrah by night, proceeded to Mecca praying Fajr there, then returned to Mina."
"5571","Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas رضي الله عنه reports seeing the Prophet ﷺ hasten in the valley of Muhassir."
"5572","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, upon reaching Muhassir, urging his mount and commanding chickpea-sized pebbles. > **""Upon you is the pebbles of khadhf.""**"
"5573","Anas رضي الله عنه reports lodging with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with some uttering takbir and some uttering tahlil, neither party faulting the other."
"5574","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports the Prophet ﷺ sending him with his family to Mina on sacrifice day to stone the Jamrah at dawn."
"5575","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports not knowing with how many pebbles the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stoned."
"5576","Harmalah ibn Amr, Abu Abdur Rahman رضي الله عنه, reports performing the Farewell Hajj riding behind his uncle Sinan ibn Sannah, seeing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at Arafat placing one finger upon another, and his uncle explaining the command to stone the Jamrah with pebbles like khadhf. > **""Stone the Jamrah with the like of the pebbles of khadhf.""**"
"5577","Hamzah ibn Amr al-Aslami رضي الله عنه reports seeing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at Arafah while riding behind his uncle with fingers one upon another, and his uncle explaining the command to stone the Jamarat with pebbles like khadhf. > **""Stone the Jamarat with the like of the pebbles of khadhf.""**"
"5578","Hirmas ibn Ziyad رضي الله عنه reports seeing the Prophet ﷺ while riding behind his father upon the she-camel al-Adba on sacrifice day with people around, and his father explaining the command to stone the Jamarat with pebbles like khadhf. > **""Stone the Jamarat with the like of the pebbles of khadhf.""**"
"5579","Asma bint Abi Bakr رضي الله عنها reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanding stoning of the Jamarat of Mudar, whereas each tribe used to stone a Jamrah. > **""Stone the Jamarat of Mudar.""**"
"5580","Abdur Rahman ibn Uthman al-Taymi رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanding them in the Farewell Hajj to stone the Jamarat with pebbles like khadhf."
"5581","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reports seeing the Prophet ﷺ stand longer at the second Jamrah than at the first, then come to Jamrat al-Aqabah, stone it, and not stand there."
"5582","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports the Prophet ﷺ would not stone until the sun had declined."
"5583","Abu al-Tufayl reports asking Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما whether his people's claim that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ran between Safa and Marwah as sunnah was true, and he affirmed it, then narrated that when Ibrahim عليه السلام was commanded the rites Satan confronted him at the Mas'a and raced him but Ibrahim outran him, then Jibril عليه السلام took him to Jamrat al-Aqabah where Satan appeared and he stoned him with seven until he left, then at the middle Jamrah he stoned him with seven, then laid him for the forehead while Ismail عليه السلام wore a white shirt and asked his father to remove it for shrouding as he owned no other garment, and while removing it he was called from behind that he had fulfilled the vision, whereupon Ibrahim turned to a white horned dark-eyed ram, a breed Ibn Abbas said they used to seek, then Jibril took him to the farthest Jamrah where he stoned Satan with seven until he left, then to Mina as the camping place of people, then to Jam' as al-Mash'ar al-Haram, then to Arafah, where Jibril asked had he recognized and he answered yes, hence its naming, then explained that when Ibrahim was commanded to call people to Hajj the mountains lowered heads and villages were raised for him so he called mankind to Hajj."
"5584","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ describing Jibril taking Ibrahim عليه السلام to Jamrat al-Aqabah where Satan appeared and he stoned him with seven until he sank, then to the middle Jamrah where he stoned him with seven until he sank, then to the farthest Jamrah where he stoned him with seven until he sank, then when Ibrahim intended to slaughter his son and his son asked to be bound against trembling lest blood sprinkle his father, he bound him, sharpened the blade, and was called from behind that he had fulfilled the vision. > **""Jibril took Ibrahim to Jamrat al-Aqabah, and Satan appeared to him, so he stoned him with seven pebbles and he sank. Then he came to the middle Jamrah and Satan appeared to him, so he stoned him with seven pebbles and he sank. Then he came to the farthest Jamrah and stoned him with seven pebbles and he sank. When Ibrahim intended to slaughter his son, his son said to his father: O father, bind me lest I shake and my blood sprinkle upon you when you slaughter me. So he bound him, and when he sharpened the blade and intended to slaughter him, he was called from behind: O Ibrahim, you have fulfilled the vision.""**"
"5585","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports Jibril عليه السلام coming to the Prophet ﷺ to show him the rites, Thabir parting, entering Mina showing the Jamarat, then showing Jam' and Arafat, and at the Jamrah Iblis springing forth whereupon he stoned him with seven until he sank, repeating until Jamrat al-Aqabah where he sank and departed."
"5586","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports Jibril عليه السلام taking the Prophet ﷺ to show the rites, bringing him to Jamrat al-Aqabah where Iblis stood and commanding him whereupon he stoned him with seven until he sank in the earth, then to the middle Jamrah likewise, then to the third likewise, then to Jam', then calling Labbayk from Arafat."
"5587","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports a man asking the Prophet ﷺ about stoning the Jamarat and its reward, and hearing him promise finding it with the Lord when most needed. > **""You shall find that with your Lord when you are most in need of it.""**"
"5588","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promising the stoning of the Jamarat as light on the Day of Resurrection. > **""When you stone the Jamarat, it shall be for you a light on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"5589","Abu Sa'id رضي الله عنه reports asking the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about the Jamarat stoned yearly supposing they diminish, whereupon he explained that accepted pebbles are raised, else they would appear like mountains. > **""What is accepted thereof is lifted, and were it not so you would have seen them like mountains.""**"
"5590","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ permitting herdsmen to stone by night."
"5591","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ permitting camel herdsmen to stone by night."
"5592","Abu Ubaydah ibn Abdillah ibn Zam'ah reports Umm Qays bint Mihsan رضي الله عنها, their neighbor, narrating that Ukashah ibn Mihsan رضي الله عنه went out with Banu Asad wearing shirts on sacrifice evening, returning at night carrying shirts in hands, and upon questioning explained the concession that stoning the Jamrah rendered lawful all ihram prohibitions except women until circumambulating the House, but evening arrival without tawaf returned them to sacred state as before stoning until tawaf, hence their shirts as seen."
"5593","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ defining lawfulness after stoning the Aqabah Jamrah with seven, departing, slaughtering hady, and shaving, rendering lawful what Hajj had forbidden. > **""Whoever stones the Jamrah with seven pebbles, the Jamrah at al-Aqabah, then departs and slaughters a hady then shaves, then what was forbidden to him of Hajj has become lawful for him.""**"
"5594","Ata reports the Prophet ﷺ, when he stoned the Jamrah, slaughtered and shaved, became lawful for everything except women."
"5595","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ restricting ritual shaving of forelocks to Hajj or Umrah. > **""Forelocks are not laid down except in Hajj or Umrah.""**"
"5596","Ma'mar ibn Abdillah رضي الله عنه reports saddling for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the Farewell Hajj, being asked one night about disturbance in the girths, swearing he had tied as usual but one envying his place had loosened them for replacement, being reassured of retention, then after the Messenger ﷺ slaughtered his hady at Mina being commanded to shave him, standing at his head with razor whereupon the Prophet ﷺ reminded him of the trust of his earlobe in his hand, his grateful acknowledgment, the Prophet's confirmation, and his shaving of the Messenger ﷺ. > **""O Ma'mar, this night I found disturbance in my girths? I shall not do so. O Ma'mar, the Messenger of Allah has enabled you of his earlobe with the razor in your hand. Then I affirm it for you.""**"
"5597","Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها reports Ma'mar ibn Abdillah al-Adawi رضي الله عنه shaving the Messenger of Allah's ﷺ head on sacrifice day."
"5598","Hubshi ibn Junadah رضي الله عنه, among those witnessing the Farewell Hajj, reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ thrice supplicating forgiveness for shavers before including shorteners upon repeated asking. > **""O Allah, forgive the shavers. O Allah, forgive the shavers. And the shorteners.""**"
"5599","Malik ibn Rabi'ah رضي الله عنه reports hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ repeatedly supplicating forgiveness for shavers, then upon a man's interjection including shorteners at the third or fourth, and adding that he himself was shaven that day valuing it beyond red camels or mighty prize. > **""O Allah, forgive the shavers. O Allah, forgive the shavers. And the shorteners.""**"
"5600","Qarib رضي الله عنه reports hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ supplicate forgiveness for shavers, and upon a man's interjection including shorteners at the fourth, with Sufyan gesturing by hand its lesser rank as though scraping. > **""O Allah, forgive the shavers. And the shorteners.""**"
"5601","Yahya ibn Husayn reports from his grandmother that she heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ thrice invoking mercy upon shavers, then upon asking at the third including shorteners. > **""May Allah have mercy on the shavers. May Allah have mercy on the shavers. May Allah have mercy on the shavers. And the shorteners.""**"
"5602","Abu Sa'id al-Khudri رضي الله عنه reports the Prophet ﷺ and his Companions shaving at Hudaybiyah except Abu Qatadah and Uthman رضي الله عنهما, and his thrice invoking mercy upon shavers before including shorteners upon repeated asking. > **""May Allah have mercy on the shavers. May Allah have mercy on the shavers. And the shorteners.""**"
"5603","Abu Sa'id al-Khudri رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and his Companions entering ihram in Hudaybiyah year except Uthman and Abu Qatadah رضي الله عنهما, and the Messenger ﷺ seeking forgiveness thrice for shavers and once for shorteners."
"5604","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ invoking mercy upon shavers, and upon repeated asking including shorteners at the third or fourth. > **""May Allah have mercy on the shavers. May Allah have mercy on the shavers. And the shorteners.""**"
"5605","Azraq ibn Qays reports sitting with Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما when a man asked about entering ihram while gathering hair, Ibn Umar recalling Umar's رضي الله عنه caliphate ruling that whoever braids or felts his head must shave, and upon the man's distinction of mere gathering, Ibn Umar equating them as goat and billy, billy and goat."
"5606","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports Muawiyah رضي الله عنه informing him that he saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ shorten his hair with a broad arrowhead."
"5607","Uthman رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbidding a woman shaving her head."
"5608","Aishah رضي الله عنها reports the Prophet ﷺ forbidding a woman shaving her head."
"5609","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that in the Hajj he performed, standing between the two Jamarat on sacrifice day, he declared it the Day of the Greater Hajj. > **""This is the Day of the Greater Hajj.""**"
"5610","Ibn Abi Awfa رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declaring sacrifice day the Day of the Greater Hajj. > **""The Day of Sacrifice is the Day of the Greater Hajj.""**"
"5611","Fadl ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports the Prophet ﷺ slaughtering at Jamrat al-Aqabah and declaring his spot with all Mina as slaughter place, bidding sacrifice in dwellings. > **""I have slaughtered here, and all Mina is a slaughter place, so slaughter in your dwellings.""**"
"5612","Urwah ibn Mudarris رضي الله عنه reports coming to the Prophet ﷺ at Mina and being bidden to calm terror. > **""Calm your terror, O Urwah.""**"
"5613","Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanding her to join Fajr prayer on sacrifice day at Mecca."
"5614","Shurayh ibn Abraha رضي الله عنه reports seeing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ utter takbir in the days of Tashriq until leaving Mina, uttering after every prayer."
"5615","Shurayh ibn Abraha رضي الله عنه reports in a version that the Prophet ﷺ uttered takbir in the days of Tashriq from Zuhr prayer of sacrifice day until leaving Mina."
"5616","Abu Ishaq reports the companions of Abdullah reporting from Abdullah رضي الله عنه that he used to utter takbir after Fajr of Arafah day, ceasing after Asr of sacrifice day while uttering after Asr prayer, saying Allah is Greatest, there is no god but Allah, and Allah is Greatest, and praise be to Allah."
"5617","Abu al-Darda رضي الله عنه reports asking the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about Mina's wonder, narrow yet expanding when people lodge, whereupon he likened it to the womb, narrow yet Allah expands it upon bearing. > **""Mina is only like the womb: it is narrow, and when it bears Allah expands it.""**"
"5618","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports Rabi'ah coming to the Prophet ﷺ seeking permission to depart in the first departure, whereupon Jibril عليه السلام came saying Allah greets him with peace and bids tell Rabi'ah not to depart in the first departure lest affection diminish. > **""O Muhammad, Allah Blessed and Exalted greets you with peace and says to you: Tell Rabi'ah not to depart in the first departure, lest I lessen you of beloved.""**"
"5619","Aishah رضي الله عنها and Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما report the Prophet ﷺ visiting the House by night."
"5620","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports permission for water providers and gatekeepers, meaning Abbas رضي الله عنه and Al Shaybah, to spend the Mina nights at Mecca."
"5621","Abu Hurrah al-Raqashi reports from his uncle that while holding the Messenger of Allah's ﷺ camel rein in mid Tashriq days keeping people off, the Prophet ﷺ asked about the month, day and land, affirmed their sacredness, declared blood, wealth and honors sacred likewise until meeting Him, bade listening for life, thrice warned against wrongdoing, forbade Muslim wealth without willing consent, laid all Jahiliyyah blood, distinction and wealth beneath his feet till Resurrection with Rabi'ah ibn al-Harith ibn Abd al-Muttalib nursed among Banu Layth killed by Hudhayl as first blood laid, laid all Jahiliyyah usury with Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib's usury as first laid while capitals remain without wronging or wronged, announced time's return to creation-day state reciting the verse of twelve months with four sacred, warned against returning to disbelief in mutual killing, announced Satan's despair of worship by pray-ers yet incitement among them, enjoined Allah-consciousness toward captive women owning nothing with mutual rights of bed and home, discipline by admonition, forsaking beds and non-severe striking, their kind provision and clothing, their taking by Allah's trust and lawful private by Allah's word, trust's return to its entruster, then spreading hands asked twice whether he conveyed and bade the witness convey to the absent as the conveyed may be happier than the hearer, a saying Hasan affirmed as fulfilled. > **""O people, do you know in what month you are, in what day you are, and in what land you are? Your blood, your wealth and your honors are sacred upon you, like the sacredness of this day of yours, in this month of yours, in this land of yours, until the day you meet Him. Listen to me and you shall live. Beware, do not wrong, beware, do not wrong, beware, do not wrong. The wealth of a Muslim is not lawful except with his willing consent. Every blood, distinction and wealth of Jahiliyyah is beneath these feet of mine until the Day of Resurrection. The first blood laid is the blood of Rabi'ah ibn al-Harith ibn Abd al-Muttalib, nursed among Banu Layth whom Hudhayl killed. Every usury of Jahiliyyah is laid, and Allah Almighty decreed the first usury laid is the usury of al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib. Yours is your capitals; you shall not wrong nor be wronged. Time has turned as it was the day Allah created the heavens and the earth. Verily the number of months with Allah is twelve months in Allah's Book the day He created the heavens and the earth, of them four are sacred; that is the upright religion, so wrong not yourselves therein. Do not return after me disbelievers striking one another's necks. Satan has despaired that the pray-ers worship him, but in incitement among you. Fear Allah concerning women, for they are captives with you owning nothing for themselves. They have a right upon you and you have a right upon them: that they admit none to your beds besides you, and permit none in your houses whom you dislike. If you fear their disobedience, admonish them, forsake them in beds, and strike them a non-severe striking. Theirs is their provision and clothing in kindness. You took them by Allah's trust and made their private lawful by Allah's word. Whoever has a trust let him return it to who entrusted him. Have I not conveyed? Have I not conveyed? Let the witness convey to the absent, for many a conveyed is happier than a hearer.""**"
"5622","Abu Nadrah reports from one who heard the Prophet's ﷺ sermon in mid Tashriq days declaring one Lord and one father, no merit of Arab over non-Arab, non-Arab over Arab, black over red, nor red over black except by piety, seeking acknowledgment of conveyance, then affirming sacred day, month, land and sacredness of blood and wealth likewise, again seeking acknowledgment and bidding the witness convey to the absent. > **""O people, your Lord is One and your father is one. There is no merit for an Arab over a non-Arab, nor for a non-Arab over an Arab, nor for black over red, nor for red over black, except by piety. Have I conveyed? This day, what day is it? What month is this? What land is this? Allah Almighty has made sacred among you your blood and your wealth like the sacredness of this day of yours, in this month of yours, in this land of yours. Have I conveyed? Let the witness convey to the absent.""**"
"5623","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that a surah was revealed to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was at Mina in the middle of the days of Tashriq, whereupon he recognized it as news of death. He ordered his she-camel al-Qaswa to be saddled, mounted her, halted for the people at al-Aqabah, and those whom Allah willed of the Muslims gathered to him. He praised Allah and extolled Him as He deserves, then spoke at length concerning the annulment of pre-Islamic blood and usury, the turning of time, the sacred months and postponement, trusts, Satan's despair, the rights of women, the unlawfulness of a brother's wealth, adherence to the Book of Allah, and the sanctity of blood, wealth and honour. > **""To proceed, O people, every bloodshed of the Jahiliyyah is void, and the first of your bloods that I void is the blood of Rabi'ah ibn al-Harith, who was being suckled among Banu Layth and Hudhayl killed him. And every usury of the Jahiliyyah is abolished, and the first of your usury that I abolish is the usury of al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib.""** > **""O people, time has turned to its form on the day Allah created the heavens and the earth. The number of months is twelve, of which four are sacred: Rajab of Mudar which is between Jumada and Sha'ban, Dhu al-Qa'dah, Dhu al-Hijjah, and al-Muharram. That is the upright religion, so wrong not yourselves therein. Postponement is an increase in disbelief by which the disbelievers are led astray, making it lawful one year and unlawful one year to adjust the number Allah has forbidden. They would make Safar lawful one year and make al-Muharram unlawful one year, and that is postponement.""** > **""O you people, whoever has a trust with him, let him return it to the one who entrusted him. O people, Satan has despaired of being worshipped in your lands at the end of time, yet he is pleased with petty deeds from you, so beware for your religion against petty deeds. O people, women with you are captives; you took them by Allah's trust and made their private parts lawful by Allah's word. You have a right over them and they have a right over you. Among your rights over them is that they admit none besides you to your beds and do not disobey you in what is good. If they do that, then you have no way against them, while theirs is their provision and clothing in a fitting manner. If you strike, then strike without severity. It is not lawful for a man from his brother's wealth except what his soul willingly permits. O people, I have left among you what if you hold fast to it you shall never stray: the Book of Allah, so act by it. O people, what day is this? What land is this? What month is this? Verily Allah the Blessed and Exalted has forbidden your blood, your wealth and your honour like the sanctity of this day, this month and this land. Let your witness convey to your absent: no prophet after me and no ummah after you. O Allah, bear witness.""**"
"5624","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the turning of time to its state on the day Allah created the heavens and the earth, and recited the verse concerning the twelve months of which four are sacred, three successive with Rajab of Mudar between Jumada and Sha'ban. > **""Verily time has turned to its form on the day Allah created the heavens and the earth. Verily the number of months with Allah is twelve months in the Book of Allah, of which four are sacred. Three are successive, and Rajab of Mudar which is between Jumada and Sha'ban.""**"
"5625","Fadalah ibn Ubayd al-Ansari رضي الله عنه reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that during the Farewell Hajj he declared the day and the land sacred, forbade blood, wealth and honour until the meeting with Allah, even the thrust by which a Muslim repels another intending harm, and defined the Muslim, the believer, the emigrant and the struggler. > **""This is a sacred day, and a sacred land, so your blood, your wealth and your honour are sacred upon you, like this day and this day until the day you meet Him, even a thrust with which a Muslim repels a Muslim intending harm thereby. I shall inform you who the Muslim is. The Muslim is he from whose tongue and hand people are safe. The believer is he whom people trust over their wealth and selves. The emigrant is he who abandons errors and sins. The struggler is he who struggles against himself in obedience to Allah.""**"
"5626","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed them at Mina on the Day of Sacrifice with words like the hadith of Abu Bakrah رضي الله عنه."
"5627","Abu Malik al-Ash'ari رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed the people during the days of sacrifice in the Farewell Hajj, affirmed the sanctity of the day, declared mutual sanctity until the Resurrection, defined the Muslim, the believer and the emigrant, and detailed the inviolability of a believer's flesh, honour, face, blood, wealth and safety from harm. > **""Is not this the sacred day? Verily the sanctity of what is between you until the Day of Resurrection is like the sanctity of this day. I inform you who the Muslim is? He from whose tongue and hand the Muslims are safe. I inform you who the believer is? He whom people trust over their wealth and selves. I inform you who the emigrant is? He who abandons evil deeds. The believer is sacred upon the believer like the sanctity of this day: his flesh is sacred upon him to eat through backbiting, his honour sacred to violate, his face sacred to strike, his blood sacred to shed, his wealth sacred to wrongfully take, and harming him sacred to thrust him away.""**"
"5628","In a version it is stated that he said that in the middle of the days of sacrifice, adding a prohibition of a thrust that causes hardship. > **""And it is unlawful for him to thrust him with a thrust that puts him to hardship.""**"
"5629","Ammar ibn Yasir رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed them, asked about the day, the month and the land, and declared blood, wealth and honour sacred like that day in that month in that land, commanding the witness to convey to the absent. > **""What day is this? What month is this? What land is this? Verily your blood, your wealth and your honour are sacred, like the sanctity of this day of yours, in this month of yours, in this land of yours. Let the witness convey to the absent.""**"
"5630","Al-Harith ibn Amr رضي الله عنه reports coming to the Prophet ﷺ at Mina or at Arafat while Bedouins came and said upon seeing his face that it was a blessed face. He thrice asked for supplication for forgiveness and the Prophet ﷺ answered with supplication for all, then caught his spittle in his hand and wiped it upon his sandal disliking that it touch anyone around him. He then asked about the day and month, declared blood and wealth sacred, commanded conveyance and charity as he might not be seen after that day, and fixed Yalamlam for Yemen and Dhat Irq for Iraq or the East as places of ihram. > **""O Allah, forgive us. O you people, what day is this, and what month is this? Verily your blood and your wealth are sacred upon you, like the sanctity of this day of yours, in this month of yours, in this land of yours. O Allah, have I conveyed? Let the witness convey to the absent. Give charity, for I do not know, perhaps you shall not see me after this day of mine.""**"
"5631","Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed the people in the Farewell Hajj, praised Allah and extolled Him, then commanded them to learn their rites as he might not perform Hajj after that year. > **""O you people, take your rites, for I do not know, perhaps I shall not perform Hajj after this year of mine.""**"
"5632","Wabisah ibn Ma'bad al-Juhani رضي الله عنه reports witnessing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the Farewell Hajj while he addressed the people, asking which month, day and land is most sacred, declaring blood, wealth and honour inviolable until meeting the Lord, raising his hands to heaven in testimony, and commanding the witness to convey. Wabisah then said they witnessed while others were absent and conveyed as commanded. > **""O you people, which month is most sacred? Which day is most sacred? Which land is greatest with Allah in sanctity? Verily your blood, your wealth and your honour are forbidden upon you like the sanctity of this day of yours, in this month of yours, in this land of yours, until the day you meet your Lord. Have I not conveyed? O Allah, bear witness. Let the witness among you convey to the absent.""**"
"5633","Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked in the Farewell Hajj which land, month and day is most sacred, was answered Makkah, Dhu al-Hijjah and the Day of Sacrifice which is the Day of the Greater Hajj, then declared blood and wealth sacred until meeting the Lord and disapproved shedding blood in Allah's sanctuary. > **""Which land is most sacred? Which month is most sacred? Which day is most sacred? Your blood and your wealth are sacred upon you until you meet your Lord, like the sanctity of this day of yours, in this month of yours, in this land of yours. I do not deem it right that blood be shed in Allah's sanctuary.""**"
"5634","Ubadah ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr reports that Rabi'ah ibn Umayyah ibn Khalaf al-Jumahi, a loud-voiced man, would repeat aloud beneath the throat of the she-camel of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the Day of Arafah whatever he was told to proclaim. He thrice called concerning the month, the land and the day, and the Prophet ﷺ thrice declared blood and wealth sacred until meeting Allah. > **""Cry out. O you people, do you know what month this is? Verily Allah Almighty has forbidden upon you your blood and your wealth until you meet your Lord, like the sanctity of this month of yours. Cry out: do you know what land this is? Verily your blood and your wealth are sacred upon you until the day you meet Him, like the sanctity of this land of yours. Cry out: what day is this? Verily Allah Almighty has forbidden upon you your blood and your wealth until the day you meet Him, like the sanctity of this day of yours.""**"
"5635","Hujayr رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet of Allah ﷺ addressed in the Farewell Hajj, asked which land and month this is, declared blood, wealth and honour sacred like that day, month and land, commanded the witness to convey, and forbade returning after him as disbelievers striking one another's necks. > **""O you people, what land is this? What month is this? Verily your blood, your wealth and your honour are sacred upon you like the sanctity of this day of yours, like this month of yours, like the sanctity of this land of yours. Let your witness convey to your absent. Do not return after me as disbelievers striking one another's necks.""**"
"5636","Abu Umamah Sudayy ibn Ajlan al-Bahili رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ came in the Farewell Hajj upon a she-camel until he halted amid the people on the Day of Arafah, asked about the day, month and land, declared wealth, honour and blood sacred, then said every prophet's supplication had passed except his own which he stored with his Lord for the Resurrection, and that prophets boast in numbers so they should not disgrace him as he waits for them at the gate of the Pond. > **""What day is this? What month? What land is this? Verily your wealth, your honour and your blood are sacred upon you like this day of yours, in this month of yours, in this land of yours. Verily every prophet's supplication has passed except my supplication, for I have stored it with my Lord until the Day of Resurrection. To proceed, prophets vie in numbers, so do not disgrace me, for I shall sit for you at the gate of the Pond.""**"
"5637","In a version from Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه, he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the Day of the Farewell Hajj upon his she-camel al-Jad'a, with his feet in the stirrups and a hand upon the front and back of the saddle stretching thereby, calling the people to listen as they might not see him after that year, followed by words like what preceded. > **""O you people, be silent, for perhaps you shall not see me after this year of yours.""**"
"5638","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه reports hearing the Prophet ﷺ while mounted upon al-Jad'a with al-Fadl ibn al-Abbas رضي الله عنهما behind him, warning against imposing oaths upon Allah. > **""Do not impose oaths upon Allah, for whoever imposes an oath upon Allah, Allah belies him.""**"
"5639","Al-Bara ibn Azib رضي الله عنه and Zayd ibn Arqam رضي الله عنه report hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declare blood and wealth sacred like that day in that land. > **""Verily your blood and your wealth are sacred upon you, like the sanctity of this day of yours in this land of yours.""**"
"5640","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ distributed sheep among his Companions on that day, and Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas رضي الله عنه received a he-goat and slaughtered it. When the Prophet ﷺ halted at Arafah he ordered Rabi'ah ibn Umayyah ibn Khalaf, a loud-voiced man, to stand beneath the throat of his she-camel and proclaim thrice concerning the month, land and day, declaring blood and wealth sacred. The Prophet ﷺ then completed his Hajj, defined the standing at Arafah and at Quzah with all of Arafah and Muzdalifah as standing places. > **""Cry out: O you people, do you know what month this is? Cry out: do you know what land this is? Cry out: do you know what day this is? Cry out and say: Verily the Messenger of Allah ﷺ has forbidden upon you your blood and your wealth like the sanctity of this month of yours, like the sanctity of this land of yours, and like the sanctity of this day of yours. This is the standing place, and all of Arafah is a standing place. This is the standing place, and all of Muzdalifah is a standing place.""**"
"5641","Shu'ayb ibn Umar reports going to Makkah until at al-Dariyyah a brother told him of a man with companionship of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at a pitched tent. They came, greeted, learned they were folk of Basrah, and he affirmed companionship, having sat beneath his pulpit on the Day of the Farewell Hajj while he ascended, praised Allah, and recited the verse of creation from male and female into peoples and tribes with nobility only by piety, denying superiority of Arab over non-Arab, black over white or white over black except by piety, and warning Quraysh not to come with the world upon their necks while people come with the Hereafter. Asked his name, he said he was al-Ad-da ibn Khalid ibn Amr ibn Amir, the horseman of al-Dahya in the Jahiliyyah. > **""Verily Allah says: O mankind, We created you from male and female and made you peoples and tribes that you may know one another. The most noble of you with Allah is the most God-fearing. There is no superiority of an Arab over a non-Arab, nor of a non-Arab over an Arab, nor of a black over a white, nor of a white over a black, except by piety. O company of Quraysh, do not come with the world carried upon your necks while people come with the Hereafter, for I shall not avail you against Allah in anything.""**"
"5642","Ka'b ibn Asim al-Ash'ari رضي الله عنه reports hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ address in the Farewell Hajj in the middle of the days of Tashriq, affirming the sanctity of the day, declaring mutual sanctity, defining the Muslim, the believer and the emigrant, and detailing the inviolability of a believer's flesh, honour, face and freedom from harm and oppressive thrusting. > **""Is this day sacred? Verily your sanctity among yourselves is like the sanctity of this day of yours. I inform you who the Muslim is? The Muslim is he from whose tongue and hand the Muslims are safe. I inform you who the believer is? The believer is he whom the Muslims trust over their selves and wealth. I inform you who the emigrant is? The emigrant is he who abandons evil deeds of what Allah has forbidden him. The believer is inviolable upon the believer like the sanctity of this day: his flesh is inviolable to eat through backbiting, his honour inviolable to violate, his face inviolable to strike, harming him inviolable, and thrusting him with a thrust that overburdens him inviolable.""**"
"5643","Kulthum ibn Jubayr reports being with Anbasah ibn Sa'id when he rode to al-Hajjaj and a man called Abu Ghadiyah al-Juhani came. Abd al-A'la ibn Abdillah ordered them to receive him, he alighted and entered, asked for drink, refused water in a glass cup then drank from another vessel. He said he pledged to the Prophet ﷺ while returning wealth to his family. Rashid ibn Unayf asked whether with that right hand, and Abd al-A'la rebuked him asking with his left. He said he witnessed his sermon on the Day of Aqabah declaring blood and wealth sacred and forbidding return to disbelief. Then when Uthman رضي الله عنه was besieged he heard a man say shall this not be killed, looked and it was Ammar رضي الله عنه, and were it not for his companions behind him he would have trampled his belly, praying to meet him. On the Day of Siffin he saw a tall man leading a battalion on foot, his armour uncovered from his knee, stabbed him, and he was Ammar. > **""Verily your blood and your wealth are sacred upon you like the sanctity of this day of yours, in this month of yours, in this land of yours. Verily do not return after me as disbelievers striking one another's necks.""**"
"5644","In a version from him it is said that Ammar ibn Yasir رضي الله عنهما was among their best, with words like the above, adding that a freedman of theirs said which hand withheld him, and he never saw a man clearer in misguidance in his view, having heard from the Prophet ﷺ what he heard then killing Ammar. > **""Which hand withheld him?""**"
"5645","Sarra bint Nabhan رضي الله عنها, a mistress of a household in the Jahiliyyah, reports hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the Farewell Hajj ask what day and what land this is, on the day called the Day of Heads, identifying it as the middle of the days of Tashriq and the Sacred Precinct. He said he might not meet them after that year, declared blood, wealth and honour sacred until meeting the Lord for reckoning, commanded the farthest to convey to the nearest, and asked whether he conveyed. When they returned to Madinah only a little remained before he ﷺ passed away. > **""Do you know what day this is? This is the middle of the days of Tashriq. Do you know what land this is? This is the Sacred Precinct. I may not meet you after this year of mine. Verily your blood, your wealth and your honour are sacred upon you, like the sanctity of this day of yours, in this month of yours, in this land of yours, until you meet your Lord and He questions you about your deeds. Let your farthest convey to your nearest. Have I conveyed?""**"
"5646","Jamrah bint Quhafah رضي الله عنها reports being with Umm Salamah, Mother of the Believers رضي الله عنها, in the Farewell Hajj and hearing the Prophet ﷺ say whether he conveyed. Her young son asked why he called his mother, and she explained he meant his ummah, while he declared honour, wealth and blood sacred like that day in that land in that month. > **""O my ummah, have I conveyed to you? Verily your honour, your wealth and your blood are sacred upon you, like the sanctity of this day of yours, in this land of yours, in this month of yours.""**"
"5647","Abu Qubaylah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood among the people in the Farewell Hajj and commanded worship, the five prayers, fasting the month, obedience to rulers, with entry to the Garden of the Lord. > **""There is no prophet after me, and no ummah after you, so worship your Lord, establish your five, fast your month, obey your rulers, then enter the Garden of your Lord.""**"
"5648","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports sitting with the Prophet ﷺ in the mosque of Mina when a man of the Ansar and a man of Thaqif came, greeted, and said they came to ask. He offered to inform them of their questions or to withhold, they chose information, the Thaqifi yielded to the Ansari, and the Prophet ﷺ listed questions about leaving home for the Sacred House, two rak'ahs after tawaf, circuits between al-Safa and al-Marwah, standing on the evening of Arafah, stoning the pillars, sacrifice, shaving, and tawaf with ifadah. The man swore that was his question, and the Prophet ﷺ detailed a good deed and effacement for each step of the mount, manumission for the rak'ahs and seventy necks for sa'y, divine descent and boasting with forgiveness as numerous as sand, raindrops and sea foam, expiation of grave mortal sins for each pebble, stored sacrifice, a good deed and effacement for each hair shaved, and sinless tawaf with an angel placing hands between his shoulders announcing forgiveness of the past and renewal of deeds. > **""If you wish I inform you of what you came to ask me about, and if you wish I withhold? You came to ask me about your leaving your house intending the Sacred House and what is yours therein, about your two rak'ahs after tawaf and what is yours therein, about your circuits between al-Safa and al-Marwah and what is yours therein, about your standing on the evening of Arafah and what is yours therein, about your stoning the pillars and what is yours therein, about your sacrifice and what is yours therein, about your shaving your head and what is yours therein, and about your tawaf of the House thereafter with ifadah and what is yours therein?""** > **""When you leave your house intending the Sacred House, your she-camel does not place nor lift a foot but Allah writes for you thereby a good deed and effaces from you a sin. Your two rak'ahs after tawaf are like freeing a neck of the children of Isma'il عليه السلام. Your circuits of al-Safa and al-Marwah thereafter are like freeing seventy necks. Your standing on the evening of Arafah: Allah the Blessed and Exalted descends to the lowest heaven and boasts of you to the angels, saying: My servants came to Me dishevelled from every deep pass hoping for My Garden; were your sins as numerous as sand, as raindrops, or as sea foam, He forgives them. Pour forth, My servants, forgiven for you and for whom you intercede. Your stoning the pillars: for every pebble you threw, a grave mortal sin. Your sacrifice is stored for you with your Lord. Your shaving your head: for every hair you shaved a good deed, and a sin effaced thereby. Your tawaf of the House thereafter: you circumambulate without sin; an angel comes until he places his hands between your shoulders and says: Act anew, for what has passed is forgiven.""**"
"5649","In a version with a different opening two men came, one Ansari and one Thaqifi, the Ansari preceding, the Prophet ﷺ noting his precedence, the Ansari yielding, and the Thaqifi preferring to be informed. The Prophet ﷺ said he came about prayer, bowing, prostration and fasting, which he affirmed, then enjoined prayer at the beginning and end of the day with sleep in the middle, proper bowing with hands upon knees and fingers spread until every limb returns to its joint, firm prostration of the forehead without pecking, and fasting the white days. > **""O brother of Thaqif, ask about your need, and if you wish I inform you of what you came to ask about. You ask me about your prayer, your bowing, your prostration and your fasting, saying: what is mine therein? Pray at the first of the day and its end, and sleep its middle. When you stand for prayer then bow, place your hands upon your knees, spread between your fingers, then raise your head until every limb returns to its joint. When you prostrate, settle your forehead upon the earth and do not peck. Fast the white nights: thirteen, fourteen and fifteen.""** > **""Then you are you.""**"
"5650","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports sitting with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the mosque of Mina when a man of the Ansar and a man of Thaqif came, greeted with fine supplication, and sought to ask. He offered to inform or to remain silent while they asked, they chose information for increase in faith, the Ansari yielded to the Thaqifi who yielded back, and the Ansari asked. The Prophet ﷺ listed questions about leaving home, tawaf, two rak'ahs, sa'y, standing at Arafah, stoning, sacrifice, shaving and tawaf al-ifadah, which the man affirmed, then detailed elevation by a degree with each step beside good deeds and effacement, divine boasting with forgiveness as sand, raindrops and sea foam, expiation of grievous mortal sins for each pebble, stored sacrifice, a good deed and effacement for each hair with surplus stored as good deeds, and sinless ifadah tawaf with an angel announcing forgiveness and renewal. The Thaqifi then asked about prayer, which the Prophet ﷺ affirmed and explained through perfect ablution with sins falling from nostrils, eyes, nails, head and feet, recitation, tranquil bowing and prostration without pecking, and prayer at the beginning and end of the day. > **""If you wish I inform you of what you came to ask me about, and if you wish I am silent and you ask me? You came to ask me about your leaving your house intending the Sacred House and what is yours therein, about your tawaf of the House and what is yours therein, about your two rak'ahs after tawaf and what is yours therein, about your circuits of al-Safa and al-Marwah and what is yours therein, about your standing on the evening of Arafah and what is yours therein, about your stoning the pillars and what is yours therein, about your sacrifice and what is yours therein, about your shaving your head and what is yours therein, and about your tawaf of the House thereafter, meaning tawaf al-ifadah?""** > **""When you leave your house intending the Sacred House, your she-camel does not place nor lift a foot but Allah writes for you thereby a good deed, removes thereby a sin, and raises you a degree. Your two rak'ahs after tawaf are like freeing a neck of the children of Isma'il عليه السلام. Your tawaf between al-Safa and al-Marwah thereafter is like freeing seventy necks. Your standing on the evening of Arafah: Allah the Blessed and Exalted descends to the lowest heaven, boasts of you to the angels, saying: These are My servants, come dishevelled as intercessors from every deep pass, hoping for My mercy and forgiveness; were your sins as numerous as sand, as numerous as drops, and as sea foam, I have forgiven them. Pour forth, My servants, forgiven for you and for whom you intercede. Your stoning the pillars: for every pebble you throw, expiation of a grave mortal sin. Your sacrifice is stored for you with your Lord. Your shaving your head: for every hair you shaved a good deed, and a sin thereby effaced. If sins are fewer than that, it is stored for you among your good deeds. Your tawaf of the House thereafter, meaning ifadah: you circumambulate without sin; an angel comes until he places his hands between your shoulders, then says: Act anew, for what has passed is forgiven.""** > **""You came to ask me about prayer. When you stand for prayer, perfect ablution: when you rinse, sins fall from your nostrils; when you wash your face, sins fall from the hair of your eyes; when you wash your hands, sins fall from your fingernails; when you wipe your head, sins fall from your head; when you wash your feet, sins fall from your toenails. Then when you stand for prayer, recite what you wish of the Qur'an. When you bow, settle your hands upon your knees, spread between your fingers until tranquil bowing. When you prostrate, settle your face fully in prostration until tranquil prostrating, and do not peck. Pray at the first of the day and its end. Then you are you.""**"
"5651","Ubadah ibn al-Samit رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ led them in prayer, then two men, one Ansari and one Thaqifi, stepped forward, the Ansari preceding. The Prophet ﷺ noted his precedence, the Ansari permitted the other being in haste, the Thaqifi asked about prayer and was informed, then the Ansari chose to be informed about his reward for intending the Ancient House, standing at Arafah, stoning, shaving and farewell to the House, which he affirmed. The Prophet ﷺ detailed a good deed and raised degree for every foot of man and beast, divine witnessing of forgiveness as days, drops and sands of Alij, hidden delight of eyes for stoning with the verse of al-Sajdah, light on the Resurrection for every fallen hair, and emergence from sins as the day his mother bore him upon farewell. > **""The Ansari has preceded you in asking. If you wish I inform you of what you came to ask about, and if you wish you ask me and I inform you. You came to ask me what reward is yours when you intend the Ancient House, what reward in your standing at Arafah, what reward in your stoning the pillars, what reward in your shaving your head, and what reward when you bid the House farewell?""** > **""When you intend the Ancient House, you and your beast do not lift nor place a foot but a good deed is written for you and a degree raised for you. Your standing at Arafah: Allah Almighty says to His angels: O My angels, what has brought My servants? They say: They came seeking Your pleasure and Paradise. Allah Almighty says: I call Myself and My creation to witness that I have forgiven them to the number of days of time, the number of drops, and the number of sands of Alij. Your stoning the pillars: Allah Almighty says: No soul knows what is hidden for them of delight of eyes as recompense for what they did. Your shaving your head: no hair of yours falls to earth but it shall be light for you on the Day of Resurrection. When you bid the House farewell, you emerge from your sins as the day your mother bore you.""**"
"5652","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports hearing the Prophet ﷺ speak of the people of the Gathering and what they attain. > **""Were the people of the Gathering to know with Whom they have alighted, they would rejoice with bounty after forgiveness.""**"
"5653","Hisl of Banu Amir ibn Lu'ayy reports that the Prophet ﷺ passed in his Hajj with them beside a man who had finished his Hajj and asked whether his Hajj was sound, and upon affirmation commanded renewal of deeds. > **""Is your Hajj sound for you? Begin deeds anew.""**"
"5654","Amir ibn Rabi'ah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded succession between Hajj and Umrah, for succession between them removes poverty and sins as the bellows removes dross of iron, with increase in life and provision in another wording. > **""Follow between Hajj and Umrah, for succession between them removes poverty and sins as the bellows removes dross of iron. Succession between them increases life and provision, and they remove poverty and sins as the bellows removes dross of iron.""**"
"5655","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded succession between Hajj and Umrah for they remove poverty and sins as the bellows removes dross of iron. > **""Follow between Hajj and Umrah, for they remove poverty and sins as the bellows removes dross of iron.""**"
"5656","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded succession between Hajj and Umrah for they remove sins as the bellows removes dross of iron. > **""Follow between Hajj and Umrah, for they remove sins as the bellows removes dross of iron.""**"
"5657","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded constancy in Hajj and Umrah for they remove poverty and sins as the bellows removes dross of iron. > **""Maintain Hajj and Umrah, for they remove poverty and sins as the bellows removes dross of iron.""**"
"5658","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded constancy in Hajj and Umrah for they remove poverty and sins as the bellows removes dross of iron. > **""Maintain Hajj and Umrah, for they remove poverty and sins as the bellows removes dross of iron.""**"
"5659","Jubayr ibn Mut'im رضي الله عنه reports seeing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ shorten his hair upon al-Marwah with an arrowhead, declaring the entry of Umrah into Hajj until the Resurrection, with an addition that there is no celibate abstention from pilgrimage. > **""Umrah has entered into Hajj until the Day of Resurrection. There is no Sarurah.""**"
"5660","Amir ibn Rabi'ah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described Umrah to Umrah as expiation for sins and errors between them, and the accepted Hajj as rewarded only by Paradise. > **""Umrah to Umrah is expiation for what is between them of sins and errors, and the accepted Hajj has no recompense except Paradise.""**"
"5661","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ performed three Umrahs, all in Dhu al-Qa'dah, reciting talbiyah until touching the Stone."
"5662","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ alighted at Marr al-Zahran in his Umrah, word reached his Companions that Quraysh said they would not be roused from emaciation. His Companions suggested slaughtering from their mounts to eat meat and sip broth for strength upon entering before the people, but he forbade it and ordered gathering of provisions. They spread leather mats, ate until satisfied, each filling his bag, then the Prophet ﷺ entered the mosque while Quraysh sat toward al-Hijr. He tucked his cloak, commanded that the people see no weakness in them, touched the Corner, walked until hidden by the Yemeni Corner then hastened to the Black Corner, whereupon Quraysh said they were not content with walking but leapt as gazelles. He did that for three circuits and it became sunnah, and Abu al-Tufayl reports Ibn Abbas informing that the Prophet ﷺ did that also in the Farewell Hajj. > **""Do not do so, but gather for me from your provisions. The people shall see no weakness in you.""**"
"5663","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ performed three Umrahs, all in Dhu al-Qa'dah: one at the time of al-Hudaybiyah, another at the truce with Quraysh, and another upon return from Ta'if at the time of Hunayn from al-Ji'ranah."
"5664","Umar ibn al-Khattab رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed three Umrahs before his Hajj in Dhu al-Qa'dah."
"5665","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that Umar رضي الله عنه sought the Prophet's ﷺ permission for Umrah, he permitted him and asked for inclusion in righteous supplication. > **""O my brother, include us in your righteous supplication.""**"
"5666","Al-Bara رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed Umrah before performing Hajj."
"5667","Abu Bakrah رضي الله عنه reports going with the Prophet ﷺ in one of his Umrahs, and he did not cease talbiyah until touching the Stone."
"5668","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned from Ta'if he alighted at al-Ji'ranah, distributed spoils there, then performed Umrah from it with two nights remaining of Shawwal."
"5669","Khalid ibn Abd al-Uzza ibn Salamah reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ alighted upon him at al-Ji'ranah, accepted his slaughtered hospitality, remained with him by day and night, then called to Umrah. The Prophet ﷺ descended with Muharrish to the valley until a place called Ashqab, apportioned its water to al-Kuddah, al-Kuddah's water to Khalid, and the rest of the valley to Muharrish. The Prophet ﷺ then struck the mound with his hand, water gushed and he drank, called again to Umrah, and Khalid sent to his companion Muharrish ibn Abdillah while the Prophet ﷺ feared entering Makkah, so he conducted him by a road avoiding those feared until he completed his rites and they returned to Khalid, where Muharrish released him from ihram by shaving. > **""O Muharrish, the water of this place is to al-Kuddah, the water of al-Kuddah is for Khalid, and what remains of the valley is yours, O Muharrish.""**"
"5670","Ali ibn Abi Talib رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ equated Umrah in Ramadan with Hajj. > **""An Umrah in Ramadan equals a Hajj.""**"
"5671","Abu Taliq رضي الله عنه reports that his wife, while he owned a camel and a she-camel, asked for his camel to perform Hajj. He said it was confined in Allah's path, she replied Hajj upon it is in Allah's path, asked for the she-camel while he performed Hajj upon his camel, but he refused to prefer anyone over himself. She asked from his expenditure, he said he had no surplus beyond his going and leaving for them though he would give had he possessed it. She then asked him to convey her greeting to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and inform him. When he met him ﷺ he conveyed her greeting and her words, and the Prophet ﷺ affirmed her truth, said giving the camel would have been in Allah's path and giving from expenditure would be replaced by Allah, and when asked what equals Hajj with him, answered Umrah in Ramadan. > **""Umm Taliq has spoken truly. Had you given her your camel it would have been in Allah's path, and had you given her from your expenditure Allah would have replaced it for you. An Umrah in Ramadan.""**"
"5672","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما and Ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما report that the Prophet ﷺ equated Umrah in Ramadan with Hajj. > **""An Umrah in Ramadan equals a Hajj.""**"
"5673","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ performed Umrah in Ramadan."
"5674","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ liken Umrah in Ramadan to Hajj with him. > **""An Umrah in Ramadan is like a Hajj with me.""**"
"5675","Urwah al-Bariqi رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ equated Umrah in Ramadan with Hajj. > **""An Umrah in Ramadan equals a Hajj.""**"
"5676","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to al-Marwah that it is a place of sacrifice with all passes and roads of Makkah as places of sacrifice. > **""This is the place of sacrifice, and all passes of Makkah and its roads are places of sacrifice.""**"
"5677","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described two leaders who are not free leaders: a woman performing Hajj with the people who menstruates before tawaf al-ziyarah, whose companions may not depart until consulting her, and a man following a funeral who prays over it and may not return until consulting its people. > **""Two leaders yet not leaders: a woman performing Hajj with the people who menstruates before circumambulating the House the tawaf of visitation; her companions may not depart until they consult her. And a man following a funeral who prays over it; he may not return until he consults the people of the funeral.""**"
"5678","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports hearing Umar ibn al-Khattab رضي الله عنه proclaim at Mina that departure is on the morrow and none should depart until circumambulating the House, for the last rite is tawaf."
"5679","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ was informed that Safiyyah رضي الله عنها menstruated and thought she would detain them, but when told she had performed ifadah on the Day of Sacrifice he permitted departure. > **""I see her but detaining us. Let her depart.""**"
"5680","Aishah رضي الله عنها and Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها report that Safiyyah bint Huyayy رضي الله عنها menstruated before departure, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered upon her while she wept, asked whether she would detain them and whether she had performed ifadah on the Day of Sacrifice, and upon affirmation permitted departure. > **""Are you detaining us? Had you performed ifadah on the Day of Sacrifice? Then depart.""**"
"5681","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that Umm Sulaym رضي الله عنها menstruated after performing ifadah, and the Prophet ﷺ commanded her to depart."
"5682","Umar ibn al-Khattab رضي الله عنه reports that alighting at al-Abtah on the evening of departure is from the sunnah."
"5683","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports a man coming to the Prophet ﷺ saying his father died without performing the Hajj of Islam, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ likened it to a debt upon his father to be fulfilled. > **""Tell me, were a debt upon your father, would you settle it for him? It is a debt upon him, so settle it.""**"
"5684","Uqbah ibn Amir رضي الله عنه reports a woman coming to the Prophet ﷺ asking whether she may perform Hajj for her deceased mother, and he likened it to a debt settled with acceptance, commanding her to perform Hajj for her. Another woman asked whether she may take her suckling or small son for Hajj, and he affirmed. > **""Tell me, were a debt upon your mother and you settled it, would it not be accepted from you? Perform Hajj for her. Yes.""**"
"5685","Zayd ibn Arqam رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ruled that Hajj performed for one's father or mother suffices for him and for them. > **""Whoever performs Hajj for his father or his mother, that suffices for him and for them.""**"
"5686","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised one who performs Hajj for a deceased the like of his reward, one who breaks a faster's fast the like of his reward, and one who calls to good the like of its doer's reward. > **""Whoever performs Hajj for a deceased, the one performed for has the like of his reward. Whoever breaks a faster's fast has the like of his reward. Whoever calls to good has the like of the reward of its doer.""**"
"5687","Sawdah رضي الله عنها reports a man coming to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying his father was an aged elder unable to perform Hajj, and the Prophet ﷺ likened it to a debt settled with acceptance, declaring Allah more merciful and commanding Hajj for his father. > **""Tell me, were a debt upon your father and you settled it for him, would it be accepted from you? Allah is more merciful. Perform Hajj for your father.""**"
"5688","Aishah رضي الله عنها reports that the Prophet ﷺ heard a man reciting talbiyah for Shubrumah, asked what Shubrumah was, was told of kinship, asked whether he had performed Hajj for himself, and upon denial commanded Hajj for himself first then for Shubrumah. > **""What is Shubrumah? Have you performed Hajj for yourself? Perform Hajj for yourself, then perform Hajj for Shubrumah.""**"
"5689","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ heard a man say labbayk for Shubrumah, asked whether he had performed Hajj for himself, and upon denial commanded Hajj for himself then for Shubrumah. > **""Have you performed Hajj for yourself? Perform Hajj for yourself, then perform Hajj for Shubrumah.""**"
"5690","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports that while the Prophet ﷺ journeyed, a woman with her young son came asking whether there is Hajj for him, he affirmed with reward for her, and when asked its reward upon standing at Arafah, answered his parents are written good deeds to the number of all standing equal to hairs of their heads. > **""Yes, and for you is reward. There is written for his parents thereby, to the number of all who stood at the standing place equal to hairs of their heads, good deeds.""**"
"5691","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ departed from Makkah he swore he would depart knowing it the most beloved and most honoured land of Allah to him, and were its people not expelling him he would not leave. He addressed Banu Abd Manaf that if authority after him came to them they should prevent none from circumambulating Allah's House at any hour of night or day, said were Quraysh not to transgress he would inform them of their standing with Allah, and supplicated that as their beginning tasted affliction their end taste favour. > **""Indeed, by Allah, I shall depart from you, and I know you are the most beloved of Allah's lands to me and the most honoured to Allah. Were your people not expelling me I would not depart. O Banu Abd Manaf, if you are custodians of this affair after me, prevent none circumambulating Allah's House at any hour he wishes of night or day. Were Quraysh not to transgress I would inform them of what is theirs with Allah. O Allah, You made their beginning taste affliction, so make their end taste favour.""**"
"5692","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ halted at al-Hazwarah and declared knowledge that it is the most beloved land of Allah to Him, and were his people not expelling him he would not depart. > **""I know you are the most beloved land of Allah to Him, and were my people not expelling me from you I would not depart.""**"
"5693","In a version from him also, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ halted at al-Hajun and swore it the best and most beloved land of Allah to Allah, and were he not expelled he would not depart, with the hadith in full. > **""By Allah, you are the best land of Allah and the most beloved land of Allah to Allah Exalted. Were I not expelled from you I would not depart.""**"
"5694","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports from the Prophet ﷺ that Allah has forbidden His sanctuary until the Resurrection: its trees not cut, its grass not gathered, its lost property not lifted except for announcement. > **""Verily Allah has forbidden His sanctuary until the Day of Resurrection: its trees shall not be cut, its grass shall not be gathered, and its lost property shall not be lifted except for announcement.""**"
"5695","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say Makkah was permitted to him for an hour of the day and is not permitted to any after him, remaining sacred by Allah's sanctity until the Resurrection with trees uncut, herbage ungathered, game unstartled and lost property untouched except for its announcer. When asked except idhkhir for their smiths and houses, he excepted idhkhir. > **""Makkah was permitted to me for an hour of the day and is not permitted to any after me. It is sacred by Allah's sanctity until the Day of Resurrection: its trees shall not be cut, its herbage shall not be gathered, its game shall not be startled, and its lost property shall not be picked except for its announcer. Except idhkhir.""**"
"5696","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports from the Prophet ﷺ that Allah sanctified this House the day He created the heavens and the earth and fashioned it when He fashioned the sun and moon with what surrounds it of heaven as sacred. It is not lawful for any after him, permitted to him only for an hour of the day then returning as before. When told Khalid ibn al-Walid رضي الله عنه was killing, he ordered a man to command Khalid to lift his hand from killing. The man instead told Khalid the Prophet of Allah ﷺ says kill whom you can, so he killed seventy. The Prophet ﷺ summoned Khalid asking did he not forbid killing, Khalid said the man commanded killing whom he could, the Prophet summoned the man asking did he not command Khalid to kill none, the man said you intended a matter and Allah intended a matter with Allah's command above his, unable except what occurred, and the Prophet of Allah ﷺ fell silent without reply. > **""Verily Allah sanctified this House the day He created the heavens and the earth, and fashioned it when He fashioned the sun and the moon, with what surrounds it of heaven as sacred. It is not lawful for any after me, permitted to me only for an hour of the day, then returning as before. Rise, O man, go to Khalid ibn al-Walid and tell him to lift his hand from killing. Did I not forbid you from killing? Did I not command Khalid to kill no one?""**"
"5697","Muti' ibn al-Aswad رضي الله عنه, whose name was al-Asi and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named him Muti', reports hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when he commanded killing of those men at Makkah declare Makkah shall never be raided after this year. > **""Makkah shall never be raided after this year ever.""**"
"5698","Abdullah ibn Hubshi رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ warned against cutting the lote tree, with the narrator explaining the reference to be the lote of the Haram. > **""Whoever cuts a lote tree, Allah will plunge his head into the Fire.""**"
"5699","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما testifies by Allah that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declare that its sanctity shall be violated by a Qurayshi man whose sins would outweigh those of men and jinn. > **""It shall be made lawful, and a man of Quraysh shall violate it; were his sins weighed against the sins of the two heavy hosts they would outweigh them.""**"
"5700","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ foretold a man committing deviation in Makkah bearing that name, upon whom shall be half the punishment of the world. > **""A man shall commit ilhad in Makkah called Abdullah; upon him shall be half the punishment of the world.""**"
"5701","Sa'id ibn Amr reports that Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما came to Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما as he sat in the Hijr and warned him against deviation in Allah's sanctuary, citing what he had heard from the Prophet ﷺ. Ibn al-Zubayr replied that he should consider lest he himself be that man, given his reading of scriptures and companionship of the Prophet ﷺ, whereupon Ibn Amr declared his departure to Sham as a warrior. > **""It shall be made lawful, and a man of Quraysh shall violate it; were his sins weighed against the sins of the two heavy hosts they would outweigh them.""**"
"5702","Sa'id ibn Amr reports that Abdullah ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما came to Ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنه and warned him against ilhad in Allah's sanctuary, citing the Prophet ﷺ concerning a Qurayshi man whose sins would preponderate over those of men and jinn. Ibn al-Zubayr told him to consider lest he be that man. > **""There shall commit ilhad therein a man of Quraysh; were his sins weighed against the sins of the two heavy hosts they would preponderate.""**"
"5703","Ibn Abza reports that when Uthman رضي الله عنه was besieged, Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما offered prepared mounts to移 move to Makkah where supporters could join him. Uthman declined, citing the Prophet ﷺ concerning a ram-like leader of Quraysh named Abdullah committing ilhad in Makkah, bearing half the burdens of mankind. > **""A ram of Quraysh shall commit ilhad in Makkah named Abdullah; upon him shall be the like of half the sins of mankind.""**"
"5704","Aishah رضي الله عنها, wife of the Prophet ﷺ, states that she saw the guide of the elephant and its driver blinded, crippled, and begging for food in Makkah."
"5705","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ that the Devil has despaired of being worshipped in this land yet contents himself with deeds people deem insignificant. > **""Verily Shaytan has despaired of being worshipped in this land of yours, but he is content with what you deem insignificant.""**"
"5706","Abu Mahdhurah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ assigned the adhan to him and his freedmen, the siqayah to Banu Hashim, and the hijabah to Banu Abd al-Dar."
"5707","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ granted the custodianship of the Kaaba to Banu Talhah as an everlasting inheritance to be taken only by a wrongdoer. > **""Take it, O Banu Talhah, eternally inherited; none shall take it from you except a wrongdoer.""**"
"5708","Abu al-Tufayl reports that when Ali رضي الله عنه disputed with al-Abbas رضي الله عنه over the siqayah, Talhah ibn Ubaydillah, Amir ibn Makhramah ibn Nawfal, and Azhar ibn Abd Awf رضي الله عنهم testified that the Prophet ﷺ had entrusted it to al-Abbas on the day of the conquest."
"5709","Abdullah ibn Zarir reports that Ali رضي الله عنه told al-Abbas رضي الله عنه to ask the Prophet ﷺ for the treasury custodianship. When al-Abbas asked, the Prophet ﷺ promised what was better for them, neither diminishing them nor diminished by them, and granted the siqayah. > **""I shall give you what is better for you than that, which neither diminishes you nor is diminished by you.""**"
"5710","Abdullah ibn Abi Razin reports from his father from Ali رضي الله عنه that he asked al-Abbas to request the hijabah from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who instead granted the siqayah with its undiminished benefit. When Ali further asked that al-Abbas be employed over the charities, the Prophet ﷺ refused on account of their nature. > **""I shall give you the siqayah, which diminishes you not nor is diminished by you.""** > **""I would never employ you over the washings of the sins of the people.""**"
"5711","Abu Dharr رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described Zamzam as sustenance and healing. > **""Zamzam is the food of hunger and the cure of sickness.""**"
"5712","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared Zamzam the best water on earth, containing nourishment and cure, and the water of the valley of Barhut in Hadramawt the worst, like the legs of locusts among vermin, flowing in the morning and waterless by evening. > **""The best water upon the face of the earth is the water of Zamzam; therein is food of hunger and cure of sickness. And the worst water upon the face of the earth is water in the valley of Barhut in Hadramawt, like the leg of the locust among vermin; in the morning it flows forth, and in the evening there is no moisture therein.""**"
"5713","Abu al-Tufayl reports he heard Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما say they used to call Zamzam Shubaah, finding it an excellent aid for dependents."
"5714","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared the wayfarer the first drinker, meaning of Zamzam. > **""The wayfarer is the first drinker.""**"
"5715","Al-Sa'ib used to say to drink from the siqayah of al-Abbas رضي الله عنه, for it is from the Sunnah."
"5716","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ requested Zamzam water as a gift from Suhayl ibn Amr."
"5717","Abu al-Tufayl رضي الله عنه reports that he saw the Prophet ﷺ come to Zamzam."
"5718","Habib ibn Abi Thabit reports that he asked Ata whether he might carry Zamzam water, and Ata replied that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had carried it, as had al-Hasan and al-Husayn رضي الله عنهما."
"5719","Abu al-Tufayl رضي الله عنه reports that he saw the Prophet ﷺ come to Zamzam and command drawing and serving water, declaring he would himself draw were it not feared they would be overpowered over it. > **""Draw and give drink; were it not that I fear you would be overpowered over it, I would draw.""**"
"5720","Uthman ibn Affan رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ came to Zamzam and commanded drawing water, declaring he would draw were it not feared they would be overpowered over it. > **""Draw; were it not that you would be overpowered over it, I would draw.""**"
"5721","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that Abu Talib was repairing Zamzam while the Prophet ﷺ as a youth carried stones."
"5722","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ delivered a sermon with his back toward al-Multazam."
"5723","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ supplicated for blessing in the sa', the mudd, and in Makkah. > **""O Allah, bless for us in our sa' and our mudd, and bless for us in our Makkah.""**"
"5724","Mu'adh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described how when Allah sent Adam down to earth he wept over Paradise a hundred autumns, then marvelled at the earth's expanse and asked whether any other would inhabit it. Allah revealed that houses wherein His name is remembered shall be raised, and that He would settle Adam in a House singled out with His honor and might, named His House, though He dwells not in houses nor is contained, being upon His Throne and Kursi, with nothing escaping His grasp and power. Adam was commanded to inhabit it in his life, then generations after him, nation after nation, until a descendant named Ibrahim would be made among its builders and dwellers. > **""Yea, there shall be raised houses wherein My name is remembered, and I shall settle you in a House thereof which I single out with My honor, invest with My might, name My House, and endow with My greatness. I dwell not therein, nor does it befit Me to dwell in houses nor to be contained, but I am upon My Throne and the Kursi of My greatness, and nothing of what I created may escape My grasp nor My power. Inhabit it, O Adam, as long as you live, then generations after you shall inhabit it, nation after nation, age after age, until it reaches a son of your sons called Ibrahim, whom I shall make among its builders and dwellers.""**"
"5725","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reports that when Allah sent Adam down from Paradise He declared He was sending down with him a House to be circumambulated as His Throne is circumambulated and prayed at as around His Throne. At the Flood it was raised; the prophets performed its pilgrimage without knowing its place, until it was shown to Ibrahim عليه السلام, who built it from five mountains — Hira, Thubayr, Lubnan, Mount Tur, and Mount al-Khayr — and people were urged to benefit from it as much as they could."
"5726","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reports that when Allah sent Adam down in the land of India he had seedlings from Paradise which he planted. His head reached the sky and his feet the earth, and he heard the speech of angels, which lightened his solitude. After a constriction he was lowered to seventy cubits, and Allah revealed He would send down a House to be circumambulated as angels circumambulate His Throne and prayed at as angels pray around His Throne. He set out toward the House with each footstep spanning a village and the distance between his feet a wilderness, until he reached Makkah, entered by the gate of al-Safa, circumambulated and prayed, then departed to Sham where he died."
"5727","Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As رضي الله عنهما reports that the House was placed two thousand years before the earth, being a white eminence while the Throne was upon the water, with the earth beneath it as a thin scum spread out from it."
"5728","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reports that the sanctuary was placed two thousand years before the earth and the earth was spread from beneath it. Mujahid explained concerning the verse about making hearts incline to them that had the wording been all hearts, Persia and Rome would have crowded upon it."
"5729","Abu al-Tufayl رضي الله عنه reports that in Jahiliyyah the Kaaba was built of piled stones, low enough for a young goat to mount, roofless, with garments laid and draped over it, the Black Corner set upon its wall in reverence, having two corners like a ring. A Roman ship bound for Abyssinia wrecked near Jeddah; Quraysh took its timber from a Roman carpenter aboard and resolved to build their Lord's House. When they sought to demolish it, a serpent like a water-skin piece, black-backed and white-bellied, barred each approach with open mouth. Quraysh gathered at the Maqam, implored Allah that they intended only honor and adornment, and asked Him to do as He willed if pleased. They heard a rumble in the sky, and a bird black-backed, white-bellied and legged, greater than a man, gripped the serpent's head with its talons and dragged it with its great tail toward Ajnad. Quraysh then demolished and rebuilt with valley stones carried upon their necks, raising it twenty cubits in the sky. The Prophet ﷺ carried stones from Ajnad wearing a small namirah which constricted him; when he placed it upon his shoulder his nakedness showed from its smallness, whereupon he was called to cover it, and was never afterward seen naked. > **""Our Lord, we intend no harm; we intend only the honor of Your House and its adornment, so if You are pleased therewith, do as seems to You.""** > **""O Muhammad, cover your nakedness.""**"
"5730","In a narration the Roman is called Bul'um, with the call to Muhammad ﷺ to cover his nakedness being the first call to him, and Abu al-Tufayl adds that Quraysh utilized some of the timber. > **""O Muhammad, cover your nakedness.""**"
"5731","Al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib رضي الله عنه reports that when Quraysh built the House they carried stones to it two by two upon their necks while women carried water for mortar. He and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ carried stones with cloaks beneath them, donning waist-wrappers when people approached. While walking with Muhammad ﷺ ahead without a wrapper, he fell prostrate gazing toward the sky above. When asked his condition he rose, took his wrapper, and declared he had been forbidden to walk naked. Al-Abbas concealed this fearing people would call him mad, until Allah manifested his prophethood. > **""I have been forbidden to walk naked.""**"
"5732","Marthad ibn Shurahbil, who was present, reports that Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما sent nobles of Quraysh to Aishah رضي الله عنها, who informed them that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had said were it not for his people's recent polytheism he would rebuild the House upon the foundations of Ibrahim and Isma'il عليهما السلام, explaining they fell short for lack of funds. When the Kaaba weakened after burning by the Syrians, Ibn al-Zubayr demolished it while the narrator was in Makkah, uncovering in the Hijr an interlocked foundation left exposed three days for witness, like the hump of camels of five stones — one face a stone, one face a stone, one face a stone, and one face two stones — so that inserting a crowbar at one corner shook the other. He built upon that foundation with two doors level to the ground, eastern and western. After Ibn al-Zubayr was killed, al-Hajjaj demolished the Hijr side and restored it as before, and Abd al-Malik wrote he wished Ibn al-Zubayr's work had been left. Marthad heard Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما say had he authority he would include the whole Hijr in the House, for why would it be circumambulated if not of the House. > **""Were it not for your people's recent polytheism, I would rebuild the House upon the foundations of Ibrahim and Isma'il. Do you know why they fell short of the foundations of Ibrahim and Isma'il? Expenditure fell short of them.""**"
"5733","Urwah reports that when the Kaaba burned and weakened, Ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما declared no man would leave his own dwelling so and resolved to demolish and rebuild, consulting the people. Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما advised leaving it as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ left it, but Ibn al-Zubayr attributed this to reluctance to spend and offered his own wealth. He demolished it with a black Abyssinian freedman while people fled Makkah, levelling it to the ground by sunset with the people's help. He inquired whence its stones were carried in Jahiliyyah, was directed to that mountain, and carried what he needed. He testified he heard Aishah رضي الله عنها report the Prophet ﷺ saying were it not for her people's recent Jahiliyyah he would demolish the Kaaba, level it to the ground since they raised it to admit only whom they wished, make for it a western door with entry from one and exit from the other, and join it to Ibrahim's foundation, for they shortened it leaving part in the Hijr. He dug to Ibrahim's foundation, inserting a crowbar at one side shook all sides, built including the Hijr addition, though its height shortened after adding; its length at demolition was eighteen cubits, and by his son's counsel he added nine cubits and three pillars. When Abd al-Malik ruled after Ibn al-Zubayr's killing, al-Hajjaj wrote concerning blocking its door. > **""O Aishah, were it not that your people are recent in Jahiliyyah, I would demolish the Kaaba and level it to the ground, for your people raised it only that none enter but whom they wish, and I would make for it a western door to be entered from this and exited from this, and I would join it to the foundation of Ibrahim, for your people shortened its measure and left part of it in the Hijr.""**"
"5734","Ikrimah reports that Ibn al-Zubayr and Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهم passed the mosque while the Syrians pelted it with stones by catapult from Mount Abu Qubays. Allah sent a thunderbolt burning their engine and forty-four men beneath it. Men of Banu Umayyah said not to be dismayed for it is a land of thunderbolts, then Allah sent another burning their engine and forty men beneath it. While so, news of the death of Yazid ibn Muawiyah came and the Syrians dispersed."
"5735","Mujahid reports from his master who was among those building the Kaaba in Jahiliyyah that he owned a stone he carved by hand and worshipped besides Allah, bringing thick milk he preferred for himself to pour over it, until a dog came, licked it, raised its leg and urinated upon it. When they built to the place of the Stone, none could see it amid their stones save a space like a man's head barely showing a man's face. One clan of Quraysh claimed the setting, others claimed it, and they agreed to arbitrate by the first man emerging from the pass. The Prophet ﷺ came, they declared the Trustworthy had come, and he placed it in a garment, summoned their clans to grasp its sides with him, then set it himself ﷺ."
"5736","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ looked at the Kaaba and declared its purity, fragrance, and sanctity, yet the sanctity of the believer greater, for Allah made it inviolable and made inviolable the believer's wealth, blood, honor, and even entertaining an evil thought of him. > **""There is no god but Allah, how pure you are, how pure your scent, how great your sanctity; yet the believer is greater in sanctity than you. Verily Allah made you inviolable, and made inviolable of the believer his wealth, his blood, his honor, and that we think of him an evil thought.""**"
"5737","Huwaytib ibn Abd al-Uzza رضي الله عنه reports that while sitting in the courtyard of the Kaaba in Jahiliyyah, a woman came seeking refuge of the House from her husband. When he extended his hand to her it withered, and the narrator saw him in Islam still paralyzed."
"5738","Jubayr ibn Mut'im رضي الله عنه reports he heard the Prophet ﷺ say to Uthman ibn Talhah رضي الله عنه when handing him the key of the Kaaba to conceal it, hence the key is kept concealed. > **""Here, conceal it.""**"
"5739","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared Allah sends down one hundred twenty mercies each day and night upon this House — sixty for those circumambulating, forty for those praying, and twenty for those gazing — with a variant wording upon the mosque of Makkah and a variant of forty for those in retreat. > **""Verily Allah sends down in every day and night one hundred twenty mercies upon this House: sixty for those circumambulating, forty for those praying, and twenty for those gazing.""**"
"5740","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared whoever enters the House enters into goodness and exits from evil forgiven. > **""Whoever enters the House enters into goodness and exits from evil forgiven.""**"
"5741","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ did not enter the House in the year of the conquest but entered during the Hajj. When he descended he prayed four — or two — rak'ahs between the Stone and the door facing the House, declaring it the qiblah. > **""This is the qiblah.""**"
"5742","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ entered the House accompanied by al-Fadl رضي الله عنه while Bilal رضي الله عنه stood at the door."
"5743","Aishah رضي الله عنها asked why all the Prophet's ﷺ family had entered the House except her. He told her to send to Shaybah to open the door for her, but Shaybah replied they could not open it by night in Jahiliyyah nor Islam. The Prophet ﷺ then told her to pray in al-Hijr, for her people had shortened the building of the House when they built it. > **""Send to Shaybah that he open the door for you.""** > **""Pray in al-Hijr, for your people fell short in the building of the House when they built it.""**"
"5744","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that al-Fadl ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما, who was with the Prophet ﷺ when he entered it, informed him that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not pray in the Kaaba but when he entered fell prostrate between the two pillars then sat supplicating."
"5745","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that al-Fadl ibn al-Abbas رضي الله عنه informed him he entered with the Prophet ﷺ, who did not pray inside when he entered, but when he exited and descended he bowed two rak'ahs at the door of the House."
"5746","Al-Fadl ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ stood in the Kaaba glorifying, magnifying, supplicating Allah عزوجل, and seeking forgiveness, without bowing or prostrating."
"5747","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما used to say he did not love to pray in the Kaaba, for whoever prays therein leaves part of it behind him. His brother informed him that when the Prophet ﷺ entered he fell between the two pillars prostrate, then sat and supplicated without praying."
"5748","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ entered the Kaaba and prayed two rak'ahs between the two pillars, then exited and prayed between the door and the Stone, declaring it the qiblah. He entered another time and stood supplicating without praying. > **""This is the qiblah.""**"
"5749","Uthman ibn Talhah رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ prayed two rak'ahs in the House, facing upon entering between the two pillars as Hasan narrated in his hadith."
"5750","Abu al-Sha'tha reports he set out for Hajj and entered the House, advancing at the two pillars to cling to the wall. Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما came, stood beside him, and prayed four. When asked where the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had prayed, he said Usamah ibn Zayd رضي الله عنه informed him it was here. When asked how many he prayed, he blamed himself for accompanying Usamah a lifetime without asking how many. The following year the narrator returned and stood in that spot, whereupon Ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما crowded him until expelling him, then prayed four therein."
"5751","Ibn Abi Mulaykah reports that Muawiyah رضي الله عنه came to Makkah, entered the Kaaba, and sent to Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما asking where the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had prayed. He replied between the two pillars opposite the door. Ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما then shook the door violently until opened and told Muawiyah he knew as he knew but had been envied."
"5752","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that on the day of the conquest the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent to the mother of Uthman ibn Talhah for the key of the Kaaba. She swore by al-Lat and al-Uzza she would not send it, and when force was suggested, her son Uthman رضي الله عنه said she was recent in disbelief and asked to be sent to her, warning her she would be killed if she withheld it. She produced it, he hastened with it, stumbled near the Prophet ﷺ so the key slipped from his hand, and the Prophet ﷺ knelt covering it with his garment and took it. He came to the door, opened it, stood at the corners and environs of the House supplicating, then prayed two rak'ahs between the two pillars."
"5753","Abd al-Rahman ibn Safwan رضي الله عنه reports that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conquered Makkah he resolved to don his garments as his house was upon the road. When the Prophet ﷺ exited, he asked those with him where the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had prayed, and was told two rak'ahs at the middle pillar on its right."
"5754","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered the Kaaba with Uthman ibn Shaybah and Bilal رضي الله عنهما. He crowded through until reaching the door as they exited and asked them what had been done; they said he had prayed two rak'ahs between the two pillars."
"5755","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه was asked where the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had prayed when he entered the House, and replied between the two pillars."
"5756","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ entered the Kaaba with Bilal, Usamah, and Uthman رضي الله عنهم, the door closed upon them. He sat upon the ground waiting a while until the Messenger of Allah ﷺ exited, then ascended the stairs, entered the House, and asked where the Prophet ﷺ had prayed. They said here, but he forgot to ask how many he had prayed."
"5757","Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Zajjaj reports he asked Shaybah ibn Uthman رضي الله عنه whether people claim the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered the Kaaba without praying therein. He declared they lied, for he prayed two rak'ahs between the two pillars then pressed his belly and back against them."
"5758","Musafi' ibn Shaybah reports from his father Shaybah رضي الله عنه that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered the Kaaba, prayed two rak'ahs, saw pictures therein, and commanded Shaybah to remove them. This weighed heavily upon Shaybah, so a Persian man offered to efface them smeared with saffron, and did so. > **""O Shaybah, spare me these pictures.""**"
"5759","Musafi' ibn Shaybah reports his father from his grandfather that he saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pray two rak'ahs behind the middle pillar of the House, there being three pillars in the House."
"5760","Misma' al-Ijli al-Ramm reports a Hijabah elder named Misma' saw him praying behind the middle pillar of the House and informed him his father from his grandfather had seen the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pray two rak'ahs behind it."
"5761","Abd al-Rahman ibn Safwan رضي الله عنه reports he saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and his companions, entered between two of them, and asked what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had done when he entered the House. He was told he had prayed two rak'ahs between the two pillars on the right of the House."
"5762","The umm walad of Shaybah رضي الله عنه, who had pledged the Prophet ﷺ, reports that the Prophet ﷺ summoned Shaybah, who opened the House. When he entered he bowed and struck his forehead."
"5763","Aishah رضي الله عنها states they always heard that Isaf and Na'ilah, a man and woman of Jurhum, committed adultery in the Kaaba and were transformed into two stones."
"5764","Aishah رضي الله عنها reports that the Prophet ﷺ declared Isaf and Na'ilah, a man and woman, committed adultery in the Kaaba so Allah transformed them into two stones remaining in Makkah. > **""Isaf and Na'ilah, a man and a woman, committed adultery in the Kaaba, so Allah transformed them into two stones.""**"
"5765","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed a group of Quraysh sitting in the courtyard of the Kaaba and warned them to consider their deeds therein, for it shall be questioned concerning them and report of them and their deeds, and reminded that its dweller is he who consumes no usury nor carries slander. > **""Consider what you do therein, for it shall be questioned concerning you and shall report of you and your deeds. And remember that its dweller is he who eats no usury and carries no slander.""**"
"5766","Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ explained the House was named al-Atiq because freed from tyrants, never attained — or overpowered — by a tyrant. > **""It was named al-Bayt al-Atiq because it was freed from tyrants; no tyrant ever attains it.""**"
"5767","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade letting its hire and its dwellings, meaning Makkah. > **""Its hire and its dwellings are not lawful.""**"
"5768","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared seventy prophets prayed in Masjid al-Khayf, among them Musa عليه السلام, whom he beheld wearing two Qatwani cloaks, in ihram upon a camel of Shan'uah bridled with fiber, wearing two braids. > **""Seventy prophets prayed in Masjid al-Khayf, among them Musa. As if I look at him, upon him two Qatwani cloaks, in ihram upon a camel of the camels of Shan'uah, bridled with a bridle of fiber, upon him two braids.""**"
"5769","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ declared seventy prophets are buried in Masjid al-Khayf. > **""In Masjid al-Khayf are buried seventy prophets.""**"
"5770","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that Abu Bakr al-Siddiq رضي الله عنه told his son that should a new calamity befall the people, he should resort to the cave wherein he and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had hidden and remain therein, for his provision would come to him therein morning and evening."
"5771","Al-Aswad ibn Khalaf رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ commanded him to renew the markers of the Haram."
"5772","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that when the Prophet ﷺ overlooked the graveyard upon his first route, he pointed with his hand beyond al-Dafirah — or al-Dafir, with doubt — declaring it an excellent graveyard. When asked whether he specified the ravine, the informant affirmed thus, and they heard the Prophet ﷺ specified the ravine facing the House. > **""What an excellent graveyard is this.""**"
"5773","Umar ibn al-Khattab رضي الله عنه reports hearing the Prophet ﷺ foretell the desertion and rebuilding of Makkah and its final abandonment. > **""The people of Makkah shall go forth from it, and none shall inhabit it but few. Then it shall be inhabited and filled and built up. Then they shall go forth from it and never return to it again.""**"
"5774","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reports hearing the Prophet ﷺ describe the Abyssinian assailant of the Kaaba with vivid physical detail. > **""Dhul-Suwayqatayn from Abyssinia shall ruin the Kaaba, strip it of its ornament and divest it of its covering. I see him even now, bald in front and bow-legged, striking it with his spade and his pickaxe.""**"
"5775","Sa'id ibn Sam'an reports hearing Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه inform Abu Qatadah رضي الله عنه of the Prophet's ﷺ account of the pledge, the violation of the House by its own folk, and the Abyssinian ruin thereafter. > **""A man shall be pledged between the Corner and the Station, and none shall violate the House except its own people. When they violate it, ask not of the ruin of the Arabs. Then the Abyssinians shall come and ruin it with a ruin after which it shall never be inhabited again, and they are those who shall extract its treasure.""**"
"5776","Aishah رضي الله عنها reports from the Prophet ﷺ the distinction between the conquest of other lands and that of Madinah. > **""The lands were opened by the sword, and Madinah was opened by the Quran.""**"
"5777","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports the Prophet ﷺ exalting Madinah with five noble descriptions. > **""Madinah is the dome of Islam, the abode of faith, the land of migration, and the resting place of the lawful and the unlawful.""**"
"5778","Rafi' ibn Khadij رضي الله عنه, seated aged near the pulpit of Marwan ibn al-Hakam in Makkah, rose in objection when Marwan extolled Makkah alone and omitted Madinah, testifying to what he had heard from the Prophet ﷺ. > **""Madinah is better than Makkah.""**"
"5779","Dhu Mikhbar رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ that Allah عزوجل looked upon Madinah when it was an open plain without mud-brick or hair-cloth dwelling, addressed its folk, and promised them every fruit upon three conditions. > **""O people of Yathrib, I impose upon you three conditions, and I shall drive to you of every fruit: disobey not, practise not excess, and be not arrogant. If you do any of that, I shall leave you like the scorching wind, none withheld from devouring it.""**"
"5780","Al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib رضي الله عنه reports setting out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ from Madinah, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ turned toward it and declared its absolution. > **""Indeed Allah has absolved this peninsula of shirk.""**"
"5781","In another wording of the report of al-Abbas رضي الله عنه, the Messenger ﷺ qualified the purification of the town with a single exception. > **""Indeed Allah has purified this town of shirk, unless the stars mislead them.""**"
"5782","Ali ibn Abi Talib رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ announcing the despair of the devils regarding worship in his city and the Arabian Peninsula, alongside their remaining weapon. > **""Indeed the devils have despaired of being worshipped in this land of mine — meaning Madinah — and in the Arabian Peninsula, but only incitement among them.""**"
"5783","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ likening the final refuge of faith to a serpent returning to its den. > **""Indeed faith shall retreat to Madinah as the serpent retreats to its hole.""**"
"5784","Al-Bara ibn Azib رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanding repentance from the old name and affirming the new one twice. > **""Whoever names Madinah Yathrib, let him seek forgiveness from Allah عزوجل. It is Tabah, it is Tabah.""**"
"5785","Budhayh reports that Abdullah ibn Ja'far, arriving as an envoy before Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan in the presence of Yahya ibn Abd al-Hakam, rebuked Yahya for asking after Khaybah when referring to Madinah. > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ named it Taybah, yet you call it Khaybah?!""**"
"5786","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ foretelling a time when people abandon Madinah for distant prosperity and carry off their families, while affirming where true good lies. > **""There shall come upon the people of Madinah a time when people depart from it to the horizons seeking ease, and they find ease. Then they come and carry off their families to that ease, while Madinah is better for them had they known.""**"
"5787","Aflah, freedman of Abu Ayyub al-Ansari رضي الله عنه, reports passing Zayd ibn Thabit and Abu Ayyub رضي الله عنهما seated at the mosque of the funerals, where one reminded the other of a narration heard from the Messenger ﷺ in that very mosque concerning Madinah, future conquests, departing and remaining folk, and the recompense of the steadfast until death. > **""There shall come upon the people a time when the conquests of the earth are opened, and men go forth to them finding ease, comfort and food. Then they pass by brothers of theirs performing Hajj or Umrah and say: What keeps you in hardship of life and severity of hunger? Going and staying — going and staying. And Madinah is better for them. None remains firm in it, enduring its hardship and severity until he dies, except I shall be for him on the Day of Resurrection a witness or an intercessor.""**"
"5788","Abu Usayd al-Sa'idi رضي الله عنه reports standing with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at the bier of Hamzah ibn Abd al-Muttalib رضي الله عنه, where a striped cloakalternately exposed face and feet until the Prophet ﷺ ordered its placement and the covering of the feet with tree branches. Lifting his head to find his Companions weeping, the Prophet ﷺ foretold the migration to fertile lands. > **""Place it upon his face, and place upon his feet some of this tree.""** > **""There shall come upon the people a time when they go forth to the countryside and find therein food, clothing and mounts. Then they write to their families: Come to us, for you are in the land of Hijaz, barren. And Madinah is better for them had they known.""**"
"5789","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ forbade the demolition of the fortified stone houses of Madinah."
"5790","Sahl ibn Sa'd رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ enjoining those with a stake in Madinah to cling to it and those without to acquire one, foretelling a time when the lack of it renders a man a mere passer-through. > **""Whoever has a root in Madinah, let him hold fast to it, and whoever has no root in it, let him make for himself a root in it. For there shall come upon the people a time when he who has no root in it shall be like one departing from it, passing through to other than it.""**"
"5791","Bilal ibn al-Harith رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ magnifying a single Ramadan and a single Friday observed in Madinah over a thousand elsewhere. > **""A Ramadan in Madinah is better than a thousand Ramadans in other lands, and a Friday in Madinah is better than a thousand Fridays in other lands.""**"
"5792","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declaring Madinah his sanctuary and supplicating for its inviolability in four particulars. > **""For every prophet there is a sanctuary, and my sanctuary is Madinah. O Allah, I declare it sacred by Your sacredness: that no innovator be sheltered therein, its grass not be cut, its thorn not be lopped, and its lost property not be taken except for one who announces it.""**"
"5793","Abu Juhayfah رضي الله عنه reports entering upon Ali رضي الله عنه, who called for his sword and drew forth an Arabian hide inscribed with the Prophet's ﷺ testament that nothing remained besides the revealed Book of Allah save this writing. > **""In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful. Muhammad, the Messenger of Allah, said: For every prophet there is a sanctuary, and my sanctuary is Madinah.""**"
"5794","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ compare Madinah to the blacksmith's bellows, parallel its sanctity with Makkah between the two lava tracts and its preserve, exempt plague and the Dajjal by Allah's will, post angels over its passes and gates, and forbid the bearing of arms for combat therein. > **""The likeness of Madinah is the likeness of the bellows. Ibrahim عليه السلام declared Makkah sacred, and I declare Madinah sacred, and it is, like Makkah, sacred — what is between its two lava tracts — and all its preserve. No tree thereof shall be cut except that a man feed his beast therefrom, and neither plague nor the Dajjal shall approach it, Allah willing, and the angels guard it at its passes and gates.""** > **""It is not lawful for anyone to carry weapons therein for fighting.""**"
"5795","Anas رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declaring Madinah sacred, with Humayd transmitting the additional prohibition of arms. > **""Madinah is sacred.""** > **""And no weapon shall be carried therein for fighting.""**"
"5796","Abu al-Yasar رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ declared the ground between the two lava tracts of Madinah sacred."
"5797","Yusayr ibn Amr reports asking Sahl ibn Hunayf رضي الله عنه whether he had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say anything concerning Madinah, and receiving the twofold affirmation. > **""Indeed it is sacred and secure. Indeed it is sacred and secure.""**"
"5798","Ka'b ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared the acacia within a barid on every side of Madinah sacred ground, then dispatched him to raise boundary marks upon the heights of Dhat al-Hays, Sharib, the heights of Mahis, and Nubayt."
"5799","Ka'b ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports in the longer narration that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent him forth solely to set the marks upon the boundaries of the Sanctuary."
"5800","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared Madinah sacred to the extent of a barid from every direction."
"5801","Al-Harith ibn Rafi' ibn Makith al-Juhani رضي الله عنه reports asking Jabir ibn Abdillah رضي الله عنهما about flocks and shepherd boys at Tharir beating the hablah fruit of the acacia for their sheep, and receiving the ruling of gentle shaking rather than stripping or lopping, together with the Prophet's ﷺ prohibition of cutting even palm-fibre rigging. > **""The preserve of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ shall neither be beaten for leaves nor lopped, but shake gently.""** > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would forbid cutting even the masad.""**"
"5802","Shurahbil ibn Sa'd reports seizing a bird at al-Aswaf, whereupon Zayd ibn Thabit رضي الله عنه took it from him, released it, and questioned his knowledge of the sanctuary. > **""Knew you not that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared what is between its two lava tracts sacred?""**"
"5803","In another wording, Zayd ibn Thabit رضي الله عنه came upon folk in their garden setting snares, drove them off with a shout, and questioned their knowledge of the hunting ban. > **""Knew you not that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared its game unlawful?!""**"
"5804","Zayd ibn Thabit رضي الله عنه, finding boys who had driven a fox into a corner, drove them away from it, with Malik reporting his further exclamation. > **""Such is done in the sanctuary of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?!""**"
"5805","Abdullah ibn Abbad al-Zuraqi reports snaring sparrows at the well of Ihab when Ubadah ibn al-Samit رضي الله عنه saw him, wrested the seized sparrow from his grasp, and admonished him with the parallel to Makkah. > **""O my son, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared what is between its two lava tracts sacred, as Ibrahim declared Makkah sacred.""**"
"5806","Yahya ibn Umarah reports from his grandfather Abu Hasan that, having entered al-Aswaf and seized two dubsiyy birds while their mother fluttered over them, Abu Hasan came upon him, struck him with a staff until a woman of the household named Maryam protested, and questioned his knowledge of the sanctuary. > **""Knew you not that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared what is between the two lava tracts of Madinah sacred?!""**"
"5807","Abdullah ibn Salam رضي الله عنه declares the ground between Kuda and Uhud sacred by the declaration of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, vowing never to cut its tree nor slay its bird."
"5808","Ibrahim ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf reports hunting a bird at Qunbulah in Madinah, whereupon his father Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf رضي الله عنه seized his ear, wrung it, released the bird, and cited the hunting ban. > **""Indeed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared the game between its two lava tracts unlawful.""**"
"5809","Ka'b ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ forbade the hunting of wild beasts between the two lava tracts of Madinah."
"5810","Abu Ayyub رضي الله عنه, finding boys who had driven a fox into a corner, drove them away and marvelled at the deed in the divine sanctuary. > **""In the sanctuary of Allah is this done?!""**"
"5811","Amr ibn Awf رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ permitted the cutting of the masad, the two posts, and wood taken as a staff for the beast."
"5812","Abu Qatadah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed ablution, prayed on the land of Sa'd at the foot of the lava tract near the goat pens, and supplicated for Madinah with the parallel to Ibrahim's supplication for Makkah, for love of the city, removal of its fever, and affirmation of its sanctity. > **""O Allah, Ibrahim, Your friend and servant, supplicated You for the people of Makkah, and I, Muhammad, Your servant and Messenger, supplicate You for the people of Madinah with the like of what Ibrahim supplicated You for Makkah. We ask You to bless them in their sa', their mudd and their fruits. O Allah, make Madinah beloved to us as You made Makkah beloved to us, and transfer its fever to Khumm. O Allah, I declare what is between its two lava tracts sacred, as You declared the Sanctuary sacred upon the tongue of Ibrahim.""**"
"5813","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports seeing the Prophet ﷺ one day gaze toward Sham, then Iraq, then every horizon, supplicating for hearts, provision and measure. > **""O Allah, turn their hearts. O Allah, grant us provision of the fruits of the earth, and bless for us our mudd and our sa'.""**"
"5814","Sufyan ibn Abi Zuhayr رضي الله عنه reports his horse tiring at al-Aqiq during an expedition dispatched by the Messenger ﷺ, returning to request a mount, the Prophet ﷺ accompanying him to seek a camel found only with Abu Jahm ibn Hudhayfah al-Adawi رضي الله عنه who gifted rather than sold it, and the Prophet ﷺ speaking at the well of Ihab of approaching buildings, the conquests of Sham and Iraq, the allure of their plenty, and the supplication for measure. > **""Buildings shall soon reach this place. Sham shall soon be opened, and men of this town shall come to it, and its yield and ease shall please them, while Madinah is better for them had they known. Then Iraq shall be opened, and folk shall come hastening, carrying off their families and those who obey them, while Madinah is better for them had they known. Ibrahim supplicated for the people of Makkah, and I ask Allah to bless for us our sa' and bless for us our mudd as He blessed the people of Makkah.""**"
"5815","Ali ibn Abi Talib رضي الله عنه reports halting with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at al-Suqya belonging to Sa'd, where the Prophet ﷺ supplicated for Madinah with the parallel to Ibrahim's supplication and a twofold increase. > **""O Allah, Ibrahim, Your servant and friend, supplicated You for the people of Makkah with blessing, and I, Muhammad, Your servant and Messenger, supplicate You for the people of Madinah, that You bless them in their sa' and their mudd as You blessed the people of Makkah, and make the blessing two blessings.""**"
"5816","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ supplicating after Fajr for Madinah, its measures, Sham and Yemen, and answering a man's plea for Iraq with warning. > **""O Allah, bless for us our Madinah, bless for us our mudd and our sa'. O Allah, bless for us our Sham and our Yemen.""** > **""From there rises the horn of Satan, and tribulations are stirred.""**"
"5817","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports the Prophet ﷺ supplicating for measure, the two sanctuaries, Sham and Yemen, and answering a man's plea for Iraq with warning of the devil's horn, stirring trials and eastern harshness. > **""O Allah, bless for us our sa' and our mudd, bless for us our Makkah and our Madinah, and bless for us our Sham and our Yemen.""** > **""In it is the horn of Satan and the stirring of tribulations, and coarseness is in the East.""**"
"5818","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ recounting a dream of a dark woman with dishevelled hair departing to Mahya'ah, that is Juhfah, and giving its interpretation. > **""I saw in a dream a black woman with dishevelled hair who went forth until she stood at Mahya'ah — that is Juhfah — so I interpreted that the fever of Madinah was transferred to Juhfah.""**"
"5819","Umar رضي الله عنه reports prices soaring in Madinah and hardship tightening, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded patience, announced blessing upon the measures, enjoined common meals, promised intercession to the steadfast, replacement for the averse, and melting punishment for the schemer. > **""Be patient and receive glad tidings, for I have blessed your mudd and your sa'. Eat together and do not separate, for the food of one suffices two, the food of two suffices four, and the food of four suffices five and six. And blessing is in the company. Whoever endures its hardship and severity, I shall be for him an intercessor or a witness on the Day of Resurrection. Whoever leaves it out of aversion to what is therein, Allah shall replace him therein with one better than he. And whoever intends it with evil, Allah shall melt him as salt melts in water.""**"
"5820","Subay'ah al-Aslamiyyah رضي الله عنها reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ enjoining whoever is able to die in Madinah to do so, with the promise of intercession. > **""Whoever among you is able to die in Madinah, let him die there, for none dies therein except I shall be for him an intercessor or a witness on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"5821","An orphan woman of Thaqif in the household of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ reports to Safiyyah bint Abi Ubayd the Prophet's ﷺ injunction to die in Madinah and its promised recompense. > **""Whoever among you is able to die in Madinah, let him die there, for whoever dies therein, I shall be for him a witness or an intercessor on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"5822","Jabir ibn Abdillah رضي الله عنهما, his sight gone, reports being led between his two sons past a commander of the time of tribulation newly arrived in Madinah, being jostled, and invoking ruin upon whoever frightens the Messenger ﷺ. Asked how one could frighten him after his death, he cited the Prophet's ﷺ identification of himself with his city's folk. > **""Perish he who frightened the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.""** > **""Whoever frightens the people of Madinah has frightened what is between my two sides.""**"
"5823","Ubadah ibn al-Samit رضي الله عنه reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ a supplication against whoever wrongs and frightens the people of Madinah, with curse and rejection of deeds. > **""O Allah, whoever wrongs the people of Madinah and frightens them, frighten him. Upon him is the curse of Allah, the angels and all mankind. Neither obligatory nor voluntary deed shall be accepted from him.""**"
"5824","Khalid ibn Khallad ibn al-Sa'ib reports from his father from his grandfather that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ threatened whoever frightens the people of Madinah with fright, curse, wrath and rejected deeds. > **""Whoever frightens the people of Madinah, Allah shall frighten him on the Day of Resurrection, curse him, be angry with him, and accept from him neither obligatory nor voluntary deed.""**"
"5825","Al-Sa'ib ibn Khallad رضي الله عنه reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ the supplication against whoever wrongs and frightens the people of Madinah, with universal curse and rejection of deeds. > **""O Allah, whoever wrongs the people of Madinah and frightens them, frighten him. Upon him is the curse of Allah, the angels and all mankind. Accept from him neither obligatory nor voluntary deed.""**"
"5826","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ warning that harm dealt to the people of Madinah returns upon its doer with divine harm, curse and rejected deeds. > **""Whoever harms the people of Madinah, Allah shall harm him. Upon him is the curse of Allah, the angels and all mankind. Allah shall accept from him neither obligatory nor voluntary deed.""**"
"5827","Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ supplicating for divine sufficiency against whoever assails the people of Madinah with violence, and warning of the melting punishment of ill-intenders. > **""O Allah, suffice them against whoever assails them with violence — meaning the people of Madinah — and none intends it with evil except Allah shall melt him as salt melts in water.""**"
"5828","Abu Umamah ibn Tha'labah رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ invoking the universal curse and rejection of deeds upon three offenders: the claimant of false masters, the false swearer upon the minbar to devour Muslim property, and the innovator or shelterer of innovators in his city. > **""Whoever attaches himself to other than his masters, upon him is the curse of Allah, the angels and all mankind. Neither obligatory nor voluntary deed shall be accepted from him. Whoever swears upon this minbar of mine a false oath by which he takes the property of a Muslim man without right, upon him is the curse of Allah, the angels and all mankind. Neither obligatory nor voluntary deed shall be accepted from him. And whoever innovates in this city of mine an innovation or shelters an innovator, upon him is the curse of Allah, the angels and all mankind. Neither obligatory nor voluntary deed shall be accepted from him.""**"
"5829","Jabir ibn Abdillah رضي الله عنهما reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ overlooking a cleft of the lava tract in their company, praising Madinah, describing the angelic blockade of the Dajjal, the three quakes, the exodus of hypocrites led by women, the day of purification, the Jewish host and the tent at the confluence of torrents. He then magnified the trial of the Dajjal above all trials, distinguished his own warning, and placed his hand upon his eye. > **""Madinah is the best of lands. When the Dajjal emerges, upon every pass thereof shall be an angel, and he shall not enter it. When that is so, Madinah shall shake its people with three quakes, and no hypocrite man or woman shall remain except he goes forth to him — and most of those who go forth to him shall be women — and that is the day of purification, when Madinah expels its filth as the bellows expels the dross of iron. With him shall be seventy thousand Jews, upon each man a green cloak and an adorned sword, and he shall pitch his tent at this lava bed where the torrents meet.""** > **""There has been no trial, nor shall there be until the Hour is established, greater than the trial of the Dajjal. There is no prophet but warned his nation, and I shall inform you of what no prophet informed his nation. Indeed Allah عزوجل is not one-eyed.""**"
"5830","In the wording of al-Tabarani, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ bade the people of Madinah remember the day of deliverance, and, asked its meaning, described the descent of the Dajjal at Dhubab, the exodus of every unbelieving and sinful soul, and the purification of the believers. > **""O people of Madinah, remember the day of deliverance.""** > **""The Dajjal shall advance until he alights at Dhubab, and none shall remain in Madinah — no polytheist man or woman, no disbeliever man or woman, no hypocrite man or woman, no sinner man or woman — except he goes forth to him, and the believers shall be purified. That is the day of deliverance.""**"
"5831","Mihjan ibn al-Adra' رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ thrice repeating the question of the day of deliverance in an address, then, asked its meaning, describing the Dajjal's ascent of Uhud, his remark upon the white palace, the angelic blockade, the salt-flat of Jurf, and the three quakes. > **""The day of deliverance — and what is the day of deliverance? The day of deliverance — and what is the day of deliverance? The day of deliverance — and what is the day of deliverance?""** > **""The Dajjal shall come and ascend Uhud and say to his companions: See you this white palace? This is the mosque of Ahmad. Then he shall come to Madinah and find at every pass an angel with unsheathed sword. Then he shall come to the salt-flat of Jurf and pitch his pavilion. Then Madinah shall shake three times, and no hypocrite man or woman, no sinner man or woman shall remain except he goes forth to him. That is the day of deliverance.""**"
"5832","Raja reports that Buraydah was at the mosque door when Mihjan passed and Sakbah prayed, and Buraydah jestingly urged Mihjan to pray like him. Mihjan answered with the Prophet's ﷺ grasp of his hand, the prospect over Madinah, the guarded gates barring the Dajjal, the inquiry in the mosque, and the counsel in the chamber of one of the wives. > **""Woe to it as a town — its people shall leave it in its best state. The Dajjal shall come to it and find upon every gate an angel with unsheathed wing, and he shall not enter it.""** > **""Who is this? Be silent, let him not hear, lest you destroy him.""** > **""Indeed the best of your religion is the easiest of it. Indeed the best of your religion is the easiest of it.""**"
"5833","Abu Abdillah al-Qarraz reports hearing Sa'd ibn Malik and Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنهما quote the Messenger of Allah ﷺ supplicating for Madinah, its measures and its folk with the parallel to Ibrahim's supplication, describing its angelic interlacing, the two guarding angels at every pass, the exclusion of plague and the Dajjal, and the melting punishment of ill-intenders. > **""O Allah, bless the people of Madinah in their city, bless them in their sa', and bless them in their mudd. O Allah, Ibrahim is Your servant and friend, and I am Your servant and Messenger. Ibrahim asked You for the people of Makkah, and I ask You for the people of Madinah as Ibrahim asked You for Makkah, and the like thereof with it. Indeed Madinah is interlaced with angels; upon every pass thereof are two angels guarding it. Neither plague nor the Dajjal shall enter it. Whoever intends it with evil, Allah shall melt him as salt melts in water.""**"
"5834","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ describing Makkah and Madinah enfolded by angels with a guardian at every pass barring the Dajjal and the plague. > **""Madinah and Makkah are encompassed by angels. Upon every pass thereof is an angel. Neither the Dajjal nor the plague shall enter them.""**"
"5835","Iyad, cousin of Usamah ibn Zayd whose daughter was his wife, reports mention before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ of a man from the countryside seized by fever on the road near Madinah, alarming the people, and the Prophet's ﷺ hopeful remark concerning its passes. > **""I hope it shall not ascend upon us its mountain pass — meaning Madinah.""**"
"5836","Tamim al-Dari رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ identifying Taybah as Madinah and describing the sword-bearing angel at every pass barring the Dajjal forever. > **""Taybah is Madinah, and there is no pass among its passes but upon it is an angel with bared sword. The Dajjal shall never enter it.""**"
"5837","Abdullah ibn Shaqiq reports walking with Imran ibn Husayn until they reached the mosque of Basrah, where Buraydah sat and Sakbah, a Companion of Muhammad ﷺ from Aslam, stood praying Duha. Buraydah urged Imran to pray like him, Imran fell silent, and later recounted walking with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ past Uhud, the prospect over Madinah, the guarded ravines, the inquiry in the mosque, and the raised hands. > **""Woe to it as a town — its people shall leave it in its finest state. The Dajjal shall come to it but shall be unable to enter it. He shall find upon every ravine an angel with unsheathed sword.""** > **""Who is this? Let him not hear, lest you break his back.""** > **""The best of your religion is the easiest of it.""**"
"5838","Mihjan ibn al-Adra' رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ dispatching him on an errand, meeting him on the road of Madinah, taking his hand up Uhud, facing Madinah with lament over its desertion at ripeness, answering who shall eat its fruit, barring the Dajjal at every pass, then questioning a praying man at the mosque door and commanding silence. > **""Woe to it as a town — its people shall leave it when it is at its ripest. Who shall eat its fruit? The sound ones among birds and beasts. The Dajjal shall not enter it; whenever he intends to enter it, an angel meets him at every pass and repels him.""** > **""Does he say it truthfully? Let him not hear, lest you destroy him.""**"
"5839","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that a three-day absence from Madinah leaves its mark upon the returning heart. > **""Whoever is absent from Madinah three days then comes to it, his heart arrives imbued with aversion.""**"
"5840","Ma'qil ibn Yasar رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ naming Madinah his migration and resting place, binding his nation to honour his neighbours so long as they avoid major sins, and threatening the violator with the drink of Hell. Asked its meaning, he explained the mire of Khabal. > **""Madinah is my migration and my resting place in the earth. It is binding upon my nation to honour my neighbours so long as they avoid the major sins. Whoever of them does not, Allah shall give him to drink of the mire of Khabal.""** > **""The exudate of the people of Hell.""**"
"5841","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports from the Prophet ﷺ the due of intercession for whoever visits his grave. > **""Whoever visits my grave, my intercession becomes due for him.""**"
"5842","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ binding himself to intercede for whoever comes with no need but visitation. > **""Whoever comes to me as a visitor, knowing no need but my visitation, it is binding upon me to be for him an intercessor on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"5843","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports from the Prophet ﷺ that Hajj coupled with visitation of the grave after death equals visitation in life. > **""Whoever performs Hajj then visits my grave after my death is like one who visited me in my life.""**"
"5844","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ equating visitation of the grave after death with visitation in life. > **""Whoever visits my grave after my death is like one who visited me in my life.""**"
"5845","Abu Dawud ibn Abi Salih reports Marwan coming one day upon a man with his face laid upon the grave, demanding to know his deed, then finding him to be Abu Ayyub رضي الله عنه, who affirmed the object of his coming. > **""Yes, I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. I did not come to a stone.""**"
"5846","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbidding the taking of his grave as an idol and cursing the takers of prophetic graves as mosques. > **""Do not make my grave an idol. Allah cursed a people who took the graves of their prophets as mosques.""**"
"5847","Ali ibn al-Husayn reports seeing a man come to a gap near the grave of the Messenger ﷺ, enter it and supplicate, forbidding him and citing a narration heard from his father from his grandfather from the Messenger ﷺ. > **""Take not my grave as a festival, nor your houses as graves. For your greeting reaches me wherever you are.""**"
"5848","Umar ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham reports that Abu Basrah al-Ghifari رضي الله عنه met Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه while he was coming from al-Tur, having prayed therein. He asked from where he had come, and Abu Hurayrah informed him, whereupon Abu Basrah declared that had he met him before departure he would not have departed, for he had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Do not tie saddles except to three mosques: al-Masjid al-Haram, and this mosque of mine, and al-Masjid al-Aqsa.""**"
"5849","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""The best to which mounts are ridden are the mosque of Ibrahim عليه السلام and my mosque.""**"
"5850","Shahr reports hearing Abu Sa'id al-Khudri رضي الله عنه, when al-Tur was mentioned in his presence, convey that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""It is not fitting for a mount to tie its saddle toward a mosque seeking prayer therein except al-Masjid al-Haram and al-Masjid al-Aqsa and this mosque of mine, and it is not fitting for a woman who has entered Islam to leave her house traveling except with a husband or a mahram of hers. And prayer is not fitting in two hours of the day: after Fajr prayer until the sun moves high. And fasting is not fitting in two days of the year: the day of Fitr from Ramadan and the day of Nahr.""**"
"5851","Ali رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: > **""Do not tie saddles except to three mosques: this mosque of mine and al-Masjid al-Haram and al-Masjid al-Aqsa. And let no woman travel beyond two days except with her husband or a mahram, and let not two days in the year be fasted: al-Fitr and al-Adha, and no prayer after two prayers: after Fajr prayer until the sun rises, and after Asr until the sun sets.""**"
"5852","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Do not tie saddles except to three mosques: Masjid al-Khayf and Masjid al-Haram and my mosque.""**"
"5853","Umar رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Do not tie saddles except to three mosques: Masjid al-Haram and this mosque of mine and al-Masjid al-Aqsa.""**"
"5854","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports hearing the Prophet ﷺ say: > **""The best to which mounts are ridden are the mosque of Ibrahim and the mosque of Muhammad صلى الله عليهما.""**"
"5855","Aishah رضي الله عنها reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""I am the Seal of the Prophets and my mosque is the seal of the mosques of the Prophets. The most deserving mosques to be visited and to which mounts are tied are al-Masjid al-Haram and my mosque; a prayer in my mosque is better than a thousand prayers in other than it among the mosques except al-Masjid al-Haram.""**"
"5856","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: > **""Do not tie saddles except to three mosques: Masjid al-Haram and the mosque of Madinah and the mosque of Bayt al-Maqdis.""**"
"5857","Abu al-Ja'd al-Damri رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Do not tie saddles except to three mosques: al-Masjid al-Haram and this mosque of mine and Masjid al-Aqsa.""**"
"5858","Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""A prayer in this mosque of mine is better than a thousand prayers in other than it among the mosques except al-Masjid al-Haram, and a prayer in al-Masjid al-Haram is better than a hundred prayers in this.""**"
"5859","Jubayr ibn Mut'im رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""A prayer in this mosque of mine is better than a thousand prayers in other than it except al-Masjid al-Haram.""**"
"5860","Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""A prayer in this mosque of mine is better than a thousand prayers in other than it except al-Masjid al-Haram.""**"
"5861","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه or Aishah رضي الله عنها reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""A prayer in my mosque is better than a thousand prayers in other than it except al-Masjid al-Aqsa.""**"
"5862","Al-Arqam رضي الله عنه reports coming to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and greeting him, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ asked where he intended. He answered that he intended that direction, pointing with his hand toward Bayt al-Maqdis. The Prophet ﷺ asked what took him there, whether trade, and he replied that he intended prayer therein. The Prophet ﷺ then pointed with his hand toward Makkah and toward Sham and said: > **""Where do you intend? What takes you there, is it trade? Prayer here is better than a thousand prayers there.""**"
"5863","Al-Arqam رضي الله عنه, a Badri in whose house at al-Safa the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had sheltered until the Muslims reached forty men, the last of whom to accept Islam was Umar ibn al-Khattab, after which they went forth against the mushrikun, reports coming to bid farewell to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ intending Bayt al-Maqdis. The Prophet ﷺ asked where he intended and what took him there, whether trade, and he replied that he intended to pray therein, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Where do you intend? What takes you there, is it trade? Prayer here is better than a thousand prayers there.""**"
"5864","Ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما reports hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""A prayer in al-Masjid al-Haram is better than a thousand prayers in other than it among the mosques.""**"
"5865","Abdullah, meaning Ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""A prayer in this mosque of mine is better than a thousand prayers in other than it except al-Masjid al-Haram, and a prayer in al-Masjid al-Haram is better than prayer in my mosque by a thousand prayers.""**"
"5866","Aishah رضي الله عنها reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that he said: > **""A prayer in al-Masjid al-Haram is better than a hundred prayers in other than it.""**"
"5867","Anas رضي الله عنه reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that he said: > **""A prayer in this mosque of mine is better than a thousand prayers in other than it except al-Masjid al-Haram.""**"
"5868","Abu Sa'id al-Khudri رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ bade farewell to a man and asked him where he intended. He answered that he intended Bayt al-Maqdis, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Where do you intend? A prayer in this mosque of mine is better than a hundred prayers in other than it except al-Masjid al-Haram.""**"
"5869","Ali ibn Abi Talib رضي الله عنه and Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه report from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: > **""What is between my grave and my minbar is a garden among the gardens of Paradise, and a prayer in my mosque is better than a thousand prayers in other than it except al-Masjid al-Haram.""**"
"5870","Jubayr ibn Mut'im رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: > **""A prayer in this mosque of mine is better than prayer in other than it except al-Masjid al-Haram.""**"
"5871","Ubaydah ibn Adam reports hearing Umar رضي الله عنه ask Ka'b where he deemed he should pray, and Ka'b advising that if he accepted his counsel he pray behind the Rock so that all of al-Quds would be before him. Umar replied that he had likened it to Judaism, and instead prayed where the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had prayed, advancing toward the qiblah and praying, then spreading his cloak and gathering refuse therein while the people gathered refuse with him."
"5872","Maymunah رضي الله عنها reports asking for a ruling concerning Bayt al-Maqdis, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ described it as the land of gathering and resurrection, commanding them to come to it and pray therein, for a prayer therein equals a thousand prayers. When asked concerning one unable to journey to it, he commanded that whoever cannot come to it gift oil to be lit therein, for whoever gifts oil to it is as one who came to it: > **""The land of gathering and the land of resurrection. Come to it and pray therein, for a prayer therein is as a thousand prayers. Whoever is unable to come to it, let him gift to it oil to be lit therein, for whoever gifts to it oil is as one who came to it.""**"
"5873","Abu al-Darda رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Prayer in al-Masjid al-Haram is a hundred thousand prayers, and prayer in my mosque is a thousand prayers, and prayer in Bayt al-Maqdis is five hundred prayers.""**"
"5874","Abu Dharr رضي الله عنه reports that while with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ they discussed which is more virtuous, the mosque of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ or Bayt al-Maqdis, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""A prayer in this mosque of mine is better than four prayers therein, and it is an excellent place of prayer. And it shall soon be that a bowshot of land from which Bayt al-Maqdis is seen is better for a man than the whole world.""**"
"5875","Dhu al-Asabi' رضي الله عنه reports asking where the Prophet ﷺ would command them if tested with remaining after him, whereupon he commanded adherence to Bayt al-Maqdis, in hope that offspring would arise going morning and evening to that mosque: > **""Upon you is Bayt al-Maqdis, for perhaps there shall arise for you offspring who go morning and evening to that mosque.""**"
"5876","Rafi' ibn Umayr رضي الله عنه reports hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that Allah said to Dawud عليه السلام to build Him a house upon the earth, but Dawud built a house for himself before building the commanded House, whereupon Allah revealed questioning why he had set his own house before His, to which Dawud answered that such is the decree of kingship and precedence. He then began the mosque, but when the wall was completed two-thirds fell, and he complained to Allah عزوجل, who revealed that it was not fitting for him to build His house because of blood upon his hands. When Dawud asked whether that was not in His desire and love, Allah affirmed yet declared them His servants and Himself most merciful to them, which grieved him, so Allah revealed not to grieve for its building would be decreed at the hand of his son Sulayman. When Dawud died Sulayman undertook it, and upon completion offered sacrifices and slaughtered and gathered Banu Isra'il, whereupon Allah revealed that He had seen his joy in building His house and bade him ask to be given. He asked three traits: judgment coinciding with His judgment, kingship befitting none after him, and that whoever comes to this House intending only prayer therein emerge from sins as the day his mother bore him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ added concerning the answer: > **""Allah said to Dawud: Build Me a house upon the earth. O Dawud, you have set your house before My House? Such is what I decreed: whoever possesses gives precedence. It is not fitting that you build Me a house, because of what blood flowed upon your hands. They are My servants and I am most merciful to them. Do not grieve, for I shall decree its building upon the hand of your son Sulayman. I have seen your joy in building My House, so ask Me and I shall give you. As for two, they were given to him, and I hope the third was given to him: judgment coinciding with My judgment, and kingship befitting none after him, and whoever comes to this House intending only prayer therein emerges from his sins as the day his mother bore him.""**"
"5877","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: > **""A prayer in this mosque of mine is better than a thousand prayers in other than it among the mosques except al-Masjid al-Haram, for it is more virtuous.""**"
"5878","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: > **""Whoever prays forty prayers in my mosque, missing no prayer, there is written for him exemption from the Fire and exemption from punishment and exemption from hypocrisy.""**"
"5879","Muslim ibn Aslam ibn Bujrah, brother of al-Harith ibn al-Khazraj, an aged elder, reports entering Madinah and fulfilling his need in the market, then returning to his family. When he laid down his cloak he remembered he had not prayed in the mosque of the Prophet ﷺ, swearing he had not prayed therein, for the Prophet ﷺ had said to them: > **""Whoever of you descends to this town, let him not return to his family until he bows two rak'ahs in this mosque, then returns to his family.""**"
"5880","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه and Abu Sa'id رضي الله عنه report that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""What is between my house and my minbar is a garden among the gardens of Paradise, and my minbar is upon my Hawd.""**"
"5881","Jabir ibn Abdillah رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""What is between my house and my chamber is a garden among the gardens of Paradise, and my minbar is upon a channel among the channels of Paradise.""**"
"5882","Sahl ibn Sa'd رضي الله عنه reports hearing the Prophet ﷺ say: > **""My minbar is upon a channel among the channels of Paradise.""** When asked what al-tur'ah means, Abu al-Abbas answered: the door."
"5883","Abu Bakr al-Siddiq رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""What is between my house and my minbar is a garden among the gardens of Paradise, and my minbar is upon a channel among the channels of Paradise.""**"
"5884","Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""What is between my house and my minbar — or my grave and my minbar — is a garden among the gardens of Paradise.""**"
"5885","Mu'adh ibn al-Harith رضي الله عنه reports hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""My minbar is upon a channel among the channels of Paradise.""**"
"5886","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: > **""What is between my house and my minbar is a garden among the gardens of Paradise, and my minbar is upon my Hawd.""**"
"5887","Abu Waqid al-Laythi رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Verily the legs of my minbar were seen in Paradise.""**"
"5888","Abu Sa'id al-Khudri رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""My minbar is upon a channel among the channels of Paradise, and what is between the minbar and the house of Aishah is a garden among the gardens of Paradise.""**"
"5889","Al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam رضي الله عنه reports hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""What is between my house and my minbar is a garden among the gardens of Paradise, and my minbar is upon my Hawd.""**"
"5890","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""What is between my chamber and my place of prayer is a garden among the gardens of Paradise.""**"
"5891","Aishah رضي الله عنها reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that in the mosque there is a spot before this pillar, such that if people knew its merit they would pray therein only after casting lots for it. A group of sons of the Sahabah and sons of the Muhajirun with her asked where it was, but she withheld it from them. They remained with her then departed, while Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما stayed, so they said she would inform him of the place and bade them watch where he prayed in the mosque. After a while he went forth and prayed at the pillar at which his son Amir ibn Abdillah ibn al-Zubayr had prayed, called the Pillar of Drawing Lots. Atiq explains it is the middle pillar between the grave and the minbar, with two pillars to its right toward the minbar, two between it and the minbar, and two between it and the courtyard: > **""Verily in the mosque there is a spot before this pillar; if people knew it they would not pray therein except that lots fly for them therein.""**"
"5892","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: > **""No mushrik shall enter this mosque of ours after this year of ours except Ahl al-Kitab and their servants.""**"
"5893","Sahl ibn Sa'd رضي الله عنه reports that two men differed in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning the mosque founded upon taqwa. One said it is the mosque of the Messenger ﷺ and the other said it is the mosque of Quba, so they came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and asked him, whereupon he said: > **""It is this mosque of mine.""**"
"5894","In another narration, when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked about the mosque founded upon taqwa, he said: > **""It is my mosque.""**"
"5895","Ubayy ibn Ka'b رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""The mosque founded upon taqwa is this mosque of mine.""**"
"5896","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports setting out toward the mosque of taqwa with Abdullah ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما and Samurah ibn Jundub رضي الله عنه, coming to the Prophet ﷺ, who bade them proceed toward the mosque of taqwa. They proceeded toward it, then met him with his hands upon the shoulders of Abu Bakr رضي الله عنه and Umar رضي الله عنه, hastening toward him, whereupon he asked who they were and Abu Bakr named Abdullah ibn Umar, Abu Hurayrah and Samurah: > **""Proceed toward the mosque of taqwa. Who are these, O Abu Bakr?""**"
"5897","Jabir ibn Samurah رضي الله عنه reports that when the people of Quba asked the Prophet ﷺ to build them a mosque, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that one of them should rise and mount the she-camel. Abu Bakr رضي الله عنه rose and mounted it and moved it but it did not rise, so he returned and sat. Umar رضي الله عنه rose and mounted it and moved it but it did not rise, so he returned and sat. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to his Companions that one of them should rise and mount the she-camel, so Ali رضي الله عنه rose, and when he placed his foot in the stirrup it sprang with him, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Let one of you rise and mount the she-camel. O Ali, loosen its rein and build upon its circuit, for it is commanded.""**"
"5898","Al-Shumus bint al-Nu'man رضي الله عنها reports observing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when he arrived and dismounted and founded this mosque, Masjid Quba. She saw him taking stone or rock until the stone weighed him down, and saw whiteness of dust upon his belly or navel. A man of his Companions came saying may my father and mother be ransom for you, O Messenger of Allah, give it to me to suffice you, but he refused and bade him take a like stone, until he founded it, declaring that Jibril عليه السلام was guiding the Ka'bah, and it was said to be the straightest mosque in qiblah: > **""No, take a stone like it. Verily Jibril is directing the Ka'bah.""**"
"5899","Sahl ibn Hunayf رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever performs wudu excellently, then enters Masjid Quba and bows therein four rak'ahs, that equals freeing a neck.""**"
"5900","Ka'b ibn Ujrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever performs wudu fully, then heads to Masjid Quba intending none other, and nothing carries him in the morning except prayer in Masjid Quba, then prays therein four rak'ahs reciting in each rak'ah Umm al-Quran, it is for him as the reward of one performing Umrah to the House of Allah.""**"
"5901","Jabir, meaning Ibn Abdillah رضي الله عنهما, reports that the Prophet ﷺ supplicated in Masjid al-Fath three times: Monday, Tuesday and Wednesday, and was answered on Wednesday between the two prayers, so gladness was recognized upon his face. Jabir declared that no grave important matter ever befell him except he sought that hour and supplicated therein and recognized the answer."
"5902","Jabir ibn Abdillah رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ came to the mosque, meaning al-Ahzab, laid down his cloak, stood and raised his hands extended supplicating against them without praying, then came again and supplicated against them and prayed."
"5903","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ was brought fadikh in Masjid al-Fadikh and drank it, for which it was so named, and in the narration of Abu Ya'la that a jar of fermenting fadikh was brought while he was in Masjid al-Fadikh and he drank it, for which it was named Masjid al-Fadikh."
"5904","Sahl ibn Sa'd رضي الله عنه reports giving the Messenger of Allah ﷺ drink with his hand from the well of Buda'ah."
"5905","Sahl ibn Sa'd رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ descended into the well of Buda'ah and spat therein."
"5906","Malik ibn Hamzah ibn Abi Usayd al-Sa'idi al-Khazraji reports from his father from his grandfather Abu Usayd رضي الله عنه that he possessed a well in Madinah called Bi'r Buda'ah in which the Prophet ﷺ had spat, so he announced glad tidings thereby and sought blessing through it."
"5907","Sa'd ibn Khaythamah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said he saw as though mercy fell between Bani Salim and Bani Bayadah. They asked whether they should move to its place, and he forbade moving but commanded burial therein, so they buried their dead therein: > **""As though mercy fell between Bani Salim and Bani Bayadah. No, but bury therein.""**"
"5908","Umm Qays رضي الله عنها reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ took her hand in a lane of Madinah wherein was no house until reaching Baqi' al-Gharqad, and told her that seventy thousand shall be raised from this graveyard entering Paradise without reckoning. Ukashah ibn Mihsan رضي الله عنه rose asking whether he was among them and was affirmed, then another rose asking likewise, whereupon he was told Ukashah had preceded him therein: > **""O Umm Qays, seventy thousand shall be raised from this graveyard entering Paradise without reckoning. And you. Ukashah has preceded you therein.""**"
"5909","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Uhud is a mountain which loves us and we love it.""**"
"5910","Uqbah ibn Suwayd al-Ansari reports hearing his father, a Companion of the Prophet ﷺ, say they returned with the Prophet ﷺ from Khaybar, and when Uhud appeared to him he said: > **""Allahu Akbar. Uhud is a mountain which loves us and we love it.""**"
"5911","Sahl ibn Sa'd رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Uhud is a pillar among the pillars of Paradise.""**"
"5912","Abu Abs ibn Jabr رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said concerning Uhud: > **""This is a mountain which loves us and we love it, upon a door among the doors of Paradise, and this Ayr upon a mountain which hates us and we hate it, upon a door among the doors of Hell.""**"
"5913","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Uhud is a mountain which loves us and we love it, so when you come to it eat from its trees even from its thorns.""**"
"5914","Amr ibn Awf رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that four mountains are among the mountains of Paradise, four rivers among the rivers of Paradise, and four battles among the battles of Paradise. When asked concerning the mountains he named Uhud which loves them and which they love as a mountain of Paradise, and al-Tur and Lubnan as mountains of Paradise, and the four rivers as the Nile, Euphrates, Sayhan and Jayhan, and the battles as Badr, Uhud, al-Khandaq and Hunayn: > **""Four mountains are among the mountains of Paradise, and four rivers are among the rivers of Paradise, and four battles are among the battles of Paradise. Uhud loves us and we love it, a mountain among the mountains of Paradise, and al-Tur is a mountain among the mountains of Paradise, and Lubnan is a mountain among the mountains of Paradise. And the four rivers: the Nile, the Euphrates, Sayhan and Jayhan. And the battles: Badr, Uhud, al-Khandaq and Hunayn.""**"
"5915","Sahl ibn Sa'd رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ invoked blessing upon Dhubab, a mountain in the Hijaz, and by his saying he prayed is meant he blessed it, and Ibn al-Athir said it is a mountain in Madinah."
"5916","Salamah ibn al-Akwa' رضي الله عنه reports hunting game and gifting its meat to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, then being missed by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who asked where he was. He answered that he went far for hunting by the upper Qanat toward houses, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ declared that had he hunted at al-Aqiq he would have preceded him going and met him coming, for he loves al-Aqiq: > **""Salamah, where are you? Had you hunted at al-Aqiq I would have preceded you going and met you coming, for I love al-Aqiq.""**"
"5917","Aishah رضي الله عنها reports that the Prophet ﷺ said while in al-Aqiq that a comer came to him declaring his blessed station: > **""You are in a blessed valley.""**"
"5918","Urwah ibn al-Zubayr reports that Aishah رضي الله عنها informed him she heard the Prophet ﷺ say: > **""Buthan is upon a pool among the pools of Paradise.""**"
"5919","Mihjan ibn al-Adra' رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent him for a need, then appeared while he was coming from a road of Madinah. He went with him until he ascended Uhud and faced Madinah and said, then asked who would eat its fruits, answering that birds and beasts seeking sustenance would: > **""Woe to its mother as a town whose people shall leave it at its ripest. Who shall eat its fruits? The sustenance-seeking birds and beasts.""**"
"5920","Mihjan رضي الله عنه also reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent him to Hajiz at the right of Madinah for a need, and upon return went with him until ascending Uhud and overlooking Madinah, lamenting the town its people would leave at its best. Then descending with him to the mosque door, seeing a man praying, he placed his hand upon Mihjan's shoulder and gazed sharply, asking thrice whether he deemed him truthful. When Mihjan praised him as the most worshipful of Madinah, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ warned thrice not to let him hear lest he destroy him, then declared Allah's pleasure with ease and dislike of hardship for this Ummah: > **""Woe to your mother as a town whose people shall leave you at your best. Do you deem him truthful? Beware, do not let him hear lest you destroy him. Verily Allah is pleased for this Ummah with ease and dislikes hardship for it.""**"
"5921","Jabir ibn Abdillah رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Madinah, its people shall leave it while it is moist with dates. Who shall eat it, O Messenger of Allah? The beasts and the birds.""**"
"5922","Jabir رضي الله عنه also reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""A rider shall surely pass by the side of the valley of Madinah and shall say: Once there was in this a large presence of believers.""**"
"5923","Umar ibn al-Khattab رضي الله عنه reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ foretelling a time when Madinah would be emptied of its believers. > **""The rider shall surely journey through the outskirts of Madinah, then he shall surely say: There was once in this place a great multitude of believers present.""**"
"5924","Abu Dharr رضي الله عنه reports that they were returning with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ until they sighted Dhu al-Hulayfah, whereupon some men hastened on to Madinah while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spent the night there and they spent the night with him. When morning came he asked about them, and it was said to him that they had hastened on to Madinah and to their women, so he informed them that they would yet abandon it at its finest, then he expressed his longing to know of a great sign to come. > **""They hastened on to Madinah and to the women. Verily they shall abandon it in its finest state. Would that I knew when a fire shall emerge from Yemen, from the mountain of al-Warraq, by which the necks of the camels at Busra shall be illuminated; you shall see it as the light of day.""**"
"5925","Sahl ibn Hunayf رضي الله عنه reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ as he was coming out of one of his houses dragging his cloak, foretelling the expansion of Madinah toward Sala and its subsequent desolation after a long lapse of time and the effacement of its traces. > **""Sala shall soon reach us, then there shall come upon Madinah a time when the traveller shall pass by some of its regions and shall say: This was once populous, after length of time and effacement of traces.""**"
"5926","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ foretold that the people would soon return to Madinah in such numbers that their frontier outposts would extend. > **""It is imminent that the people shall return to Madinah until their outposts shall be at Silah.""**"
"5927","Abdullah ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded meeting the returning pilgrim with greeting and handshake and requesting his supplication before he re-enters his home, for his sins have been forgiven. > **""When you meet the pilgrim, greet him with peace and shake his hand, and command him to seek forgiveness for you before he enters his house, for he is forgiven.""**"
"5928","Habib ibn Abi Thabit رحمه الله reports that he would go out with his father رحمه الله to receive the pilgrims and greet them with peace before they became soiled by worldly life."
